01:04:48.508 --> 01:04:52.378
# EPIC MUSIC

01:05:32.719 --> 01:05:33.887
DRAMATIC MUSIC PLAYS

01:05:46.099 --> 01:05:49.736
Welcome, everyone, to our Deep
Dive panel for WoW Classic.

01:05:50.270 --> 01:05:52.038
I'd like to start
with some introductions,

01:05:52.172 --> 01:05:54.573
with these fine gentlemen
with me, and myself.

01:05:54.707 --> 01:05:55.741
I'm Holly Longdale.

01:05:55.875 --> 01:05:58.011
I'm the principal producer
for WoW Classic.

01:05:58.645 --> 01:06:01.180
A little bit about
my background,

01:06:01.314 --> 01:06:03.349
I spent 13 years
working on EverQuest,

01:06:03.483 --> 01:06:04.617
I started as a game designer,

01:06:04.751 --> 01:06:07.854
and eventually became
the head of the studio

01:06:07.987 --> 01:06:08.921
for the EverQuest franchise.

01:06:09.455 --> 01:06:12.625
That said, I've always been
a WoW player, since beta,

01:06:14.460 --> 01:06:16.662
so this is obviously a dream
come true for me,

01:06:16.796 --> 01:06:17.730
and I'm thrilled
to be here with you.

01:06:18.331 --> 01:06:19.699
I'm Patrick Dawson.

01:06:20.366 --> 01:06:22.135
I'm the production director
for World of Warcraft.

01:06:22.802 --> 01:06:24.337
I've been at Blizzard
for 15 years now,

01:06:26.005 --> 01:06:27.207
and have been on WoW
for most of that time.

01:06:28.374 --> 01:06:29.942
I'm an avid WoW player myself,

01:06:30.076 --> 01:06:32.811
and really excited to open up
that Dark Portal again

01:06:32.946 --> 01:06:33.980
as we all step through it.

01:06:34.414 --> 01:06:36.248
And my name's Brian Birmingham,

01:06:36.382 --> 01:06:37.450
and I'm the lead software
engineer on WoW Classic.

01:06:38.184 --> 01:06:39.518
I've also been at Blizzard
for 15 years.

01:06:39.652 --> 01:06:41.120
I started on World of Warcraft.

01:06:41.254 --> 01:06:42.988
I spent about three years on
Hearthstone as well,

01:06:43.122 --> 01:06:44.256
then came back to
the World of Warcraft team

01:06:44.390 --> 01:06:46.325
to work on WoW Classic,

01:06:46.459 --> 01:06:48.560
and it has been a great joy
to me to work on this team.

01:06:48.928 --> 01:06:50.229
-There's a lot to talk about.
-Yeah.

01:06:50.363 --> 01:06:54.033
So, earlier we announced Burning
Crusade Classic is coming.

01:06:54.167 --> 01:06:56.101
But there's a lot more to it.

01:06:56.236 --> 01:06:57.637
Let's take a look
at the features, 'cause...

01:06:57.770 --> 01:06:59.505
Wow.
I mean, wow, right?

01:07:00.907 --> 01:07:03.109
-Wow.
-(BRIAN LAUGHS)

01:07:03.243 --> 01:07:05.978
I was so excited by
playing draenei and blood elves.

01:07:06.112 --> 01:07:09.415
HOLLY: I played both, and I will
say my heart's with Alliance.

01:07:10.116 --> 01:07:11.049
PATRICK: Hm...

01:07:11.184 --> 01:07:13.352
LAUGHTER

01:07:13.486 --> 01:07:14.419
Quiet!

01:07:14.554 --> 01:07:15.888
LAUGHTER

01:07:16.022 --> 01:07:17.490
And I also took up jewelcrafting

01:07:17.624 --> 01:07:19.058
on my draenei shaman.

01:07:19.192 --> 01:07:22.294
I wanted to corner the market.
Just the whole notion of having

01:07:22.428 --> 01:07:24.931
items with sockets, and just
exploring that whole system.

01:07:25.064 --> 01:07:26.566
I'm also
a bit of a systems nerd.

01:07:27.033 --> 01:07:32.037
So, I completely lost my time
for weeks in there.

01:07:32.171 --> 01:07:34.273
LAUGHTER

01:07:34.407 --> 01:07:35.942
Burning Crusade, I remember
being there back then.

01:07:36.075 --> 01:07:36.643
It was our first expansion ever,

01:07:38.144 --> 01:07:41.780
so all the new features was
this really cool moment of,

01:07:42.315 --> 01:07:43.749
what's coming next?
What's gonna happen?

01:07:44.217 --> 01:07:47.586
And one of the things that was
pretty impactful to the game was

01:07:47.720 --> 01:07:49.521
Horde having paladins
and Alliance having shaman.

01:07:50.223 --> 01:07:52.858
As an avid PvP-er myself,

01:07:52.992 --> 01:07:56.428
I remember going back
and trying to fight shaman,

01:07:56.562 --> 01:07:56.962
I was Alliance back then,

01:07:57.830 --> 01:07:59.131
and just not understanding
that class at all

01:07:59.265 --> 01:08:00.566
'cause my team didn't have it,

01:08:00.700 --> 01:08:04.003
so I tried to poly them,
and they'd be Grounding Totem...

01:08:04.137 --> 01:08:05.438
I didn't know what that was.

01:08:05.571 --> 01:08:07.472
"Why are shaman immune to
everything? I don't unde--"

01:08:07.607 --> 01:08:08.207
That's not what you said.

01:08:08.341 --> 01:08:09.942
Yeah, but no, I did.

01:08:10.076 --> 01:08:11.944
No, you said,
"They're cheating."

01:08:12.078 --> 01:08:15.347
LAUGHTER

01:08:15.481 --> 01:08:16.915
I understood
the polymorphed druids.

01:08:17.050 --> 01:08:18.317
LAUGHTER

01:08:18.451 --> 01:08:19.851
But shaman? Why?

01:08:19.986 --> 01:08:21.186
LAUGHTER

01:08:21.521 --> 01:08:22.989
Now I started to understand
shaman a little bit better,

01:08:23.456 --> 01:08:24.857
we got some of their benefits,

01:08:25.591 --> 01:08:27.259
I actually got to see totems

01:08:27.393 --> 01:08:28.861
being placed
onto my side for once,

01:08:29.562 --> 01:08:32.064
and got to see the frustration

01:08:32.198 --> 01:08:33.132
of dealing with Bubble Hearth
on the other.

01:08:33.266 --> 01:08:34.267
BRIAN LAUGHS

01:08:34.400 --> 01:08:36.568
Yeah, I remember
when I was doing raids,

01:08:36.703 --> 01:08:37.970
we were always jealous
of the Alliance

01:08:38.104 --> 01:08:40.039
having paladins
for Blessing of Salvation.

01:08:40.506 --> 01:08:42.741
People would be pulling aggro
off me, and I'd be yelling,

01:08:42.875 --> 01:08:43.208
"Wait for three Sunders!"

01:08:43.343 --> 01:08:44.243
LAUGHTER

01:08:44.377 --> 01:08:45.444
And the Alliance just like,

01:08:45.578 --> 01:08:47.146
"No, it's no problem,
just go. Just go."

01:08:47.280 --> 01:08:48.547
-(LAUGHS)
-Yup.

01:08:48.681 --> 01:08:50.850
So, you know, we were upset,

01:08:51.417 --> 01:08:52.851
raiding Razergore
without Earthbind.

01:08:52.985 --> 01:08:55.187
LAUGHTER

01:08:55.321 --> 01:08:57.289
I mean, really, even the playing
field for both PvE and PvP

01:08:57.423 --> 01:08:58.791
was kind of cool.

01:08:59.258 --> 01:09:00.292
But there are a ton
of new features,

01:09:00.426 --> 01:09:02.128
and instabilities with this too,
you know,

01:09:02.261 --> 01:09:03.529
as we got to Level 70,

01:09:03.663 --> 01:09:05.831
we got to experience new
concepts and new things.

01:09:06.232 --> 01:09:07.266
I remember one of
my first experiences

01:09:07.400 --> 01:09:08.234
with understanding
what it meant for...

01:09:09.035 --> 01:09:11.236
Designed by Blizzard.

01:09:11.371 --> 01:09:12.905
-BRIAN: Oh, yeah.
-I was an engineer, right?

01:09:13.039 --> 01:09:14.240
I wasn't a designer,

01:09:14.374 --> 01:09:17.276
I was working on the live ops
team at the time,

01:09:18.044 --> 01:09:21.313
and, you know, I was playing
my class, avid player of mage,

01:09:21.447 --> 01:09:23.449
and I kept, I sent this long
email to Tom Chilton,

01:09:23.583 --> 01:09:26.818
saying, like,
"Hey. Here's what mage needs."

01:09:26.953 --> 01:09:28.353
-LAUGHTER
-Dawson Manifesto.

01:09:28.488 --> 01:09:30.823
-LAUGHTER
-PATRICK: Yeah.

01:09:30.957 --> 01:09:31.490
It was a well-thought-out
forum post.

01:09:31.624 --> 01:09:34.026
LAUGHTER

01:09:34.160 --> 01:09:36.194
And what was in it,
was things like,

01:09:36.329 --> 01:09:37.996
"Bring back invisibility,

01:09:38.131 --> 01:09:39.298
let's get a threat
to offer the class."

01:09:39.432 --> 01:09:40.733
PATRICK: And I didn't tie
those two together.

01:09:40.867 --> 01:09:43.235
Like, "That's pretty good.
Give me invisibility."

01:09:43.870 --> 01:09:46.038
And just knowing that
that skill was coming,

01:09:46.172 --> 01:09:47.406
along with some other
new stuff for mages,

01:09:47.540 --> 01:09:48.674
like the Molten Armor
and the Ice Lances,

01:09:49.175 --> 01:09:51.376
it was just really cool to see
all this new stuff

01:09:51.511 --> 01:09:52.211
in Burning Crusade.

01:09:52.345 --> 01:09:53.412
On the other side of that,

01:09:53.546 --> 01:09:55.481
I was really excited
about Spell Reflect,

01:09:55.615 --> 01:09:57.349
because I was frustrated
getting polymorphed,

01:09:57.483 --> 01:09:59.418
and being able to reflect that
back on the mage...

01:09:59.552 --> 01:10:00.952
As a mage, I was not excited
for you to get that.

01:10:01.087 --> 01:10:03.523
LAUGHTER

01:10:03.656 --> 01:10:03.989
-Alas.
-I guess you weren't.

01:10:04.123 --> 01:10:05.658
LAUGHTER

01:10:05.792 --> 01:10:06.959
And there were even
some PvE encounters

01:10:07.093 --> 01:10:09.228
that had abilities
that you could reflect back.

01:10:09.362 --> 01:10:11.530
I remember that was really fun,
to find ways to time that,

01:10:11.664 --> 01:10:13.566
and use it
as a defensive ability as well.

01:10:13.699 --> 01:10:14.801
-I was a hunter.
-BRIAN: Mm-hm.

01:10:15.601 --> 01:10:16.668
Also a hunter.

01:10:16.803 --> 01:10:18.938
BRIAN: So am I now.
I switched. (LAUGHS)

01:10:19.071 --> 01:10:20.138
But I remember
just chasing beast master,

01:10:20.273 --> 01:10:21.940
and just DPS, DPS, DPS...

01:10:22.074 --> 01:10:23.509
-BRIAN: Yeah.
-PATRICK: Yep.

01:10:23.643 --> 01:10:25.378
That was it.
That was the chase, yeah.

01:10:25.912 --> 01:10:27.546
Talking about that too makes me
think of the arenas,

01:10:27.680 --> 01:10:28.814
and the arena system.

01:10:28.948 --> 01:10:30.516
PATRICK: That was
a whole new world for us.

01:10:30.650 --> 01:10:32.318
With battlegrounds and
the way they worked before,

01:10:32.919 --> 01:10:34.520
the way the matchmaking
happened,

01:10:34.654 --> 01:10:35.921
usually I was
pretty competitive in PvP,

01:10:36.055 --> 01:10:37.222
so I would either
get in there and

01:10:37.356 --> 01:10:39.491
-stomp the enemy team...
-(HOLLY LAUGHS)

01:10:39.625 --> 01:10:40.959
...who was there to be casual,

01:10:41.093 --> 01:10:42.728
or, even though
we were a good team,

01:10:42.862 --> 01:10:44.163
we weren't the best of the best,

01:10:44.297 --> 01:10:46.131
so when we ended up going
against one of those teams,

01:10:46.265 --> 01:10:47.733
it was just, like,

01:10:47.867 --> 01:10:50.135
"Well, jeez, we're just getting
stomped now. This is tough."

01:10:50.269 --> 01:10:52.771
Arenas gave everyone
an opportunity

01:10:52.905 --> 01:10:56.042
to play against
equal-minded opponents, right?

01:10:56.175 --> 01:10:57.943
PATRICK: So if you were
a little bit more casual,

01:10:58.077 --> 01:10:59.312
you get the opportunity
to play against people

01:10:59.445 --> 01:11:00.512
who weren't maybe
so competitive.

01:11:00.646 --> 01:11:03.349
Or, if you were cutting-edge,
go for it.

01:11:03.483 --> 01:11:04.883
Try to be the best of the best.

01:11:05.017 --> 01:11:06.652
And what was great about that,

01:11:06.786 --> 01:11:08.387
is that it really,
it spawned an esport.

01:11:08.521 --> 01:11:09.855
-Yeah.
-That wasn't...

01:11:09.989 --> 01:11:12.191
We didn't design it for that, we
just wanted people to have fun,

01:11:12.325 --> 01:11:14.126
but when we noticed, like,
"Hey, everybody's playing this,

01:11:14.260 --> 01:11:16.561
everybody's competing in this,
people are watching it,"

01:11:16.696 --> 01:11:17.863
we had to embrace that.

01:11:17.997 --> 01:11:19.899
PATRICK: We started
featuring it in BlizzCons,

01:11:20.032 --> 01:11:20.799
and we have
been ever since.

01:11:20.933 --> 01:11:22.201
BRIAN: Yeah, it's awesome.

01:11:22.969 --> 01:11:25.304
I think one of my favorite
things to recollect

01:11:25.438 --> 01:11:26.906
was the exciting zones
that you would travel through.

01:11:27.039 --> 01:11:28.440
One of my favorite memories
was actually

01:11:28.574 --> 01:11:30.408
in the first zone,
Hellfire Peninsula.

01:11:31.043 --> 01:11:35.047
And it was adventuring around
in Hellfire Peninsula,

01:11:35.181 --> 01:11:36.248
going around
and doing all those quests,

01:11:36.382 --> 01:11:38.350
and always living in fear of
the fel reaver.

01:11:38.985 --> 01:11:40.352
Because you'd be out there

01:11:40.486 --> 01:11:42.455
collecting parts
from a broken zeppelin,

01:11:42.588 --> 01:11:44.656
and you'd be fighting off
your helboars,

01:11:44.790 --> 01:11:46.859
and all of a sudden you'd see
the screen start to shake,

01:11:46.993 --> 01:11:48.661
and this thomp, thomp,
thomp, and then...

01:11:48.794 --> 01:11:51.029
FEL REAVER ROARS

01:11:51.163 --> 01:11:52.564
BRIAN: Bwaaah!

01:11:52.698 --> 01:11:54.400
You're like, "Oh my God, I gotta
run, he's right on top of me."

01:11:54.534 --> 01:11:55.835
That sound haunts my dreams.

01:11:55.968 --> 01:11:57.770
Oh I know, yeah,
it's terrifying.

01:11:57.904 --> 01:11:59.438
Even alive,
it's just, Feign Death.

01:11:59.572 --> 01:12:00.839
-BRIAN: Yes!
-Feign Death.

01:12:00.973 --> 01:12:03.242
-Yeah, just like that.
-LAUGHTER

01:12:03.376 --> 01:12:06.311
And then the payoff when you
later go to Netherstorm,

01:12:06.846 --> 01:12:08.347
and they give you
the quest, like,

01:12:08.481 --> 01:12:09.582
"Could you get me a part
from inside the fel reaver?

01:12:09.715 --> 01:12:11.017
You're gonna have to kill him."

01:12:11.150 --> 01:12:13.586
And you're like, "That guy that
used to terrorize me?"

01:12:13.719 --> 01:12:15.053
BRIAN: They're like,
"You could take him."

01:12:15.187 --> 01:12:16.922
And that was such
an exciting payoff,

01:12:17.056 --> 01:12:18.790
to be able to bring that
full circle

01:12:18.925 --> 01:12:20.225
and go back and see him again.

01:12:20.359 --> 01:12:23.061
For me, I snuck into beta.

01:12:23.663 --> 01:12:24.764
I got really lucky,

01:12:24.897 --> 01:12:26.365
because I could see
the construction sign

01:12:26.499 --> 01:12:27.466
for some of the zones,

01:12:27.600 --> 01:12:29.034
and of course I ignored it,

01:12:29.735 --> 01:12:31.403
-and went and explored.
-(BRIAN LAUGHS)

01:12:32.104 --> 01:12:34.340
So, I remember
running through Blade's Edge,

01:12:34.473 --> 01:12:37.643
and just seeing
dragon carcasses and skeletons

01:12:37.777 --> 01:12:40.012
impaled in the environment,

01:12:40.146 --> 01:12:42.047
and then moving on
to Netherstorm,

01:12:42.181 --> 01:12:44.116
I just was blown away,

01:12:44.250 --> 01:12:46.085
even from Zangarmarsh,
all the way through.

01:12:47.186 --> 01:12:49.155
HOLLY: Everything felt like
its own world.

01:12:49.288 --> 01:12:50.322
And just to be able
to go through that,

01:12:50.456 --> 01:12:53.759
and the delight of it,
and the epicness of it,

01:12:53.893 --> 01:12:56.361
and then you come up to Area 52.

01:12:56.495 --> 01:12:58.830
That's what I love about Wow,

01:12:58.965 --> 01:13:00.499
is you can have epic,
epic storylines,

01:13:00.633 --> 01:13:01.399
and still find lightness in it,

01:13:01.534 --> 01:13:02.901
so I just loved it.

01:13:03.035 --> 01:13:05.204
I love...
I'm an explorer, so...

01:13:05.338 --> 01:13:08.140
For me, I remember Burning
Crusade really opened up

01:13:08.274 --> 01:13:10.910
a new gameplay type for me.

01:13:11.043 --> 01:13:13.712
I became a collector
in Burning Crusade, right?

01:13:13.846 --> 01:13:16.181
And that started with
the Aldor and Scryer route.

01:13:17.049 --> 01:13:18.850
'Cause I got my Aldor rep
all the way up,

01:13:18.985 --> 01:13:20.585
and then I was thinking about
switching to Scryer,

01:13:20.720 --> 01:13:23.054
and people were like,
"You're insane, don't do that."

01:13:23.189 --> 01:13:24.122
LAUGHTER

01:13:24.256 --> 01:13:26.024
And I was like, "I bet I can."

01:13:26.158 --> 01:13:28.427
You know,
just a few people thought...

01:13:28.561 --> 01:13:29.495
And I did.

01:13:29.629 --> 01:13:31.363
And it was cool,
it was really fun,

01:13:31.497 --> 01:13:32.664
but then I started
doing other things.

01:13:32.798 --> 01:13:35.234
PATRICK: There are these new
mounts, and pets,

01:13:35.368 --> 01:13:36.702
and I went back and
even got some of the older raps,

01:13:36.836 --> 01:13:39.238
like Thorium Brotherhood
and Winterspring.

01:13:39.372 --> 01:13:41.373
But then things like
Netherwing Ledge came out...

01:13:41.507 --> 01:13:43.408
The world just opened up for me
in Burning Crusade,

01:13:43.542 --> 01:13:44.543
to really start my collection.

01:13:44.677 --> 01:13:46.678
Of course,
we also had flying mounts,

01:13:46.812 --> 01:13:48.180
which I loved,

01:13:48.314 --> 01:13:50.449
and then decided to play with
some friends on our PvP server,

01:13:51.183 --> 01:13:53.219
and was running around Hellfire,

01:13:53.352 --> 01:13:55.087
and, I...

01:13:55.888 --> 01:13:58.991
Death from above,
which was likely just Pat.

01:13:59.125 --> 01:14:00.292
-Probably.
-HOLLY: Yeah.

01:14:00.426 --> 01:14:02.328
(LAUGHS) Were you flying around
harassing people, Pat?

01:14:02.461 --> 01:14:04.262
I was getting
a lot of honorable kills.

01:14:04.397 --> 01:14:07.065
-LAUGHTER
-Back in the day.

01:14:07.199 --> 01:14:09.100
So, that's just a quick look at
some of our memories

01:14:09.235 --> 01:14:11.403
for Burning Crusade.

01:14:11.537 --> 01:14:13.405
-And that was 13 years ago.
-Mm-hm.

01:14:13.539 --> 01:14:17.542
So that code has been
hanging around for 13 years.

01:14:17.677 --> 01:14:18.744
And we're not just doing

01:14:18.878 --> 01:14:21.813
a "save-as"
Burning Crusade Classic.

01:14:21.947 --> 01:14:23.616
-No, it's harder than that.
-(HOLLY LAUGHS) Yeah.

01:14:23.749 --> 01:14:25.383
So let's get into
some of those details.

01:14:25.518 --> 01:14:27.786
So, one of the challenges
has been

01:14:27.920 --> 01:14:30.188
that we don't actually want to
ship on the old code.

01:14:30.322 --> 01:14:33.725
And the reason for that is
because it's got a lot of bugs,

01:14:33.859 --> 01:14:35.894
and difficulties
with the architecture

01:14:36.028 --> 01:14:37.195
that we don't wanna
have to deal with.

01:14:37.329 --> 01:14:38.663
But we do want that old data.

01:14:38.798 --> 01:14:39.865
And we talked about this

01:14:39.999 --> 01:14:41.967
when we did the original launch
of Classic,

01:14:42.101 --> 01:14:43.769
was how the data
is really the thing

01:14:43.903 --> 01:14:45.170
that describes how the game is
supposed to be played

01:14:45.304 --> 01:14:47.072
and what's supposed
to happen in it.

01:14:47.840 --> 01:14:49.308
And so, we want
to bring that old data

01:14:49.442 --> 01:14:50.376
and we want to run it
on the modern code.

01:14:50.509 --> 01:14:52.477
The same as we did for Classic.

01:14:52.611 --> 01:14:56.881
And there's 750,000 rows of
data, that we have to go through

01:14:57.016 --> 01:15:00.019
and convert from their old
formats to their new formats,

01:15:00.152 --> 01:15:02.888
and fortunately we have
really talented engineers,

01:15:03.022 --> 01:15:04.856
and we run automated scripts
that go through this,

01:15:04.990 --> 01:15:07.025
but even after
we've run these scripts,

01:15:07.159 --> 01:15:09.928
we have to compare them
with how it used to behave,

01:15:10.062 --> 01:15:12.030
and make sure that
we've converted correctly.

01:15:12.164 --> 01:15:13.231
And then there's even
some things

01:15:13.365 --> 01:15:14.533
that are making it more
difficult this time,

01:15:14.667 --> 01:15:16.735
like the fact that we've made
a couple

01:15:16.869 --> 01:15:17.469
of minor changes or bug fixes

01:15:17.603 --> 01:15:18.603
in the Classic data,

01:15:19.171 --> 01:15:21.473
and we want to make sure
that we keep those

01:15:21.607 --> 01:15:22.574
as we're going into
Burning Crusade,

01:15:22.708 --> 01:15:24.476
so we have to merge
those changes

01:15:24.610 --> 01:15:25.611
with the changes that happened

01:15:25.745 --> 01:15:27.012
with the Burning Crusade
originally,

01:15:27.146 --> 01:15:29.681
and make sure that we have
a coherent set of changes

01:15:29.815 --> 01:15:31.416
that make sense to bring us

01:15:31.550 --> 01:15:32.817
from Classic
into the Burning Crusade.

01:15:32.952 --> 01:15:35.988
There's an enormous amount of
time spent looking at

01:15:36.122 --> 01:15:38.090
"Okay, this is
how it came out now."

01:15:38.224 --> 01:15:40.092
We have a reference client,
that is...

01:15:41.560 --> 01:15:42.661
the original.

01:15:42.795 --> 01:15:44.129
That's right,
we do use the old code for that.

01:15:44.263 --> 01:15:45.297
So we still have it,

01:15:45.431 --> 01:15:46.866
we just don't want to ship
on that anymore.

01:15:46.999 --> 01:15:48.967
We have the old code,
with the old data,

01:15:49.101 --> 01:15:50.869
knows exactly
how it's supposed to behave,

01:15:51.003 --> 01:15:52.004
so those things
work together,

01:15:52.138 --> 01:15:53.038
and we can bring
that up internally,

01:15:53.172 --> 01:15:54.106
and take a look at it, and say,

01:15:54.240 --> 01:15:55.841
"Okay, this is
how it was back then,

01:15:55.975 --> 01:15:57.609
this is exactly
what it used to look like."

01:15:57.743 --> 01:15:58.977
And we do sometimes find things

01:15:59.111 --> 01:16:01.347
that are bugs
we still want to fix,

01:16:01.480 --> 01:16:02.547
but a lot of times
it will tell us, like,

01:16:02.681 --> 01:16:03.715
"Oh, this thing is not a bug,

01:16:03.849 --> 01:16:05.517
this is actually
the intended behavior."

01:16:05.651 --> 01:16:07.720
Or this is something
we don't really remember right.

01:16:07.853 --> 01:16:10.355
There's a tendency to
want to chase your own memory,

01:16:10.489 --> 01:16:13.225
like, "I remember it being like
this!", and it's like, "No."

01:16:13.993 --> 01:16:16.128
It's not always
exactly the way you remember it.

01:16:16.262 --> 01:16:17.963
Everybody has
that human failing, even us,

01:16:18.097 --> 01:16:20.265
and so a lot of times
we'll have an argument.

01:16:20.399 --> 01:16:22.667
"I remember it this way."
"Well, I remember it this way."

01:16:22.802 --> 01:16:23.669
"Let's go and check.
We can look."

01:16:24.403 --> 01:16:27.072
Well, another reason
to ship on the new code

01:16:27.206 --> 01:16:30.008
is really just the years
of stability improvements,

01:16:30.142 --> 01:16:32.243
and the fact that the world
didn't look the same back

01:16:32.378 --> 01:16:33.978
in 2007 as it does today.

01:16:34.113 --> 01:16:35.047
BRIAN: That's true.

01:16:35.181 --> 01:16:36.948
There wasn't
cloud architecture then.

01:16:37.082 --> 01:16:40.452
I remember, I had the privilege
of being on the live ops team,

01:16:40.586 --> 01:16:42.654
where I got to see
what all the servers look like,

01:16:42.788 --> 01:16:44.756
and how it was all architected.

01:16:45.291 --> 01:16:46.991
And to pull back the curtain
a bit on this, the way it was,

01:16:47.126 --> 01:16:49.894
is everything
was its own server,

01:16:50.029 --> 01:16:52.163
right, they didn't really
talk to each other very well,

01:16:52.298 --> 01:16:56.134
so each realm
had 11 blades on it,

01:16:56.268 --> 01:16:57.335
and these blades were just
bare metal blades,

01:16:57.469 --> 01:16:59.705
and one blade was for Azeroth,

01:16:59.839 --> 01:17:01.506
one blade was for Kalimdor,
one was for Outland...

01:17:01.640 --> 01:17:03.241
And then we had some
for instances,

01:17:03.375 --> 01:17:05.009
and you know, back then,

01:17:05.144 --> 01:17:06.945
what would happen
without cloud is

01:17:07.079 --> 01:17:08.680
you would try
to request resources,

01:17:08.814 --> 01:17:10.916
and maybe a lot of people were
running dungeons,

01:17:11.050 --> 01:17:13.051
and so it would give you back
things like

01:17:13.185 --> 01:17:14.619
"Transfer Aborted:
instance not found."

01:17:14.753 --> 01:17:17.122
Oh, boy. We don't want to go
back to those days.

01:17:17.256 --> 01:17:19.891
So now, we can take advantage
of things like the cloud,

01:17:20.025 --> 01:17:21.359
and not have
those resource issues,

01:17:21.493 --> 01:17:24.496
so shipping on this new code is
a big improvement in stability.

01:17:24.630 --> 01:17:26.398
Yeah, and so we're basically

01:17:26.532 --> 01:17:28.967
weaving
all these threads together.

01:17:29.101 --> 01:17:31.604
We did finally get it built
for Burning Crusade Classic.

01:17:32.304 --> 01:17:32.838
Super excited.

01:17:35.007 --> 01:17:37.008
The team gets together,
we're preparing for a play test,

01:17:37.142 --> 01:17:38.277
and we go to create
our characters,

01:17:39.245 --> 01:17:41.146
and we see...

01:17:42.681 --> 01:17:44.382
this blood elf, who, you know,

01:17:44.516 --> 01:17:45.984
he remembers the Sunwell.

01:17:46.919 --> 01:17:48.520
-HOLLY: He forgot his pants.
-BRIAN: Yeah?

01:17:48.654 --> 01:17:49.187
So everything didn't work
the first time?

01:17:49.321 --> 01:17:50.255
LAUGHTER

01:17:50.389 --> 01:17:52.157
What? I know.

01:17:52.291 --> 01:17:55.093
-Yeah, I did mention, we had...
-Sabotage!

01:17:55.227 --> 01:17:57.262
...to go check for problems in
those conversion scripts.

01:17:57.396 --> 01:17:58.763
And so yeah, sure enough,

01:17:59.098 --> 01:18:00.999
what's going on here is

01:18:01.133 --> 01:18:02.268
that this guy thinks he's
dressed but he isn't,

01:18:02.401 --> 01:18:04.102
and you can see
why he thinks he's dressed--

01:18:04.236 --> 01:18:05.704
Does that happen to you a lot?

01:18:05.838 --> 01:18:07.805
-(BRIAN LAUGHS)
-Only in my high school dreams.

01:18:07.940 --> 01:18:09.474
LAUGHTER

01:18:09.608 --> 01:18:10.542
You can see,
if you look at his calves,

01:18:10.676 --> 01:18:12.844
you can see
that they're flared out

01:18:12.978 --> 01:18:14.880
because he thinks
he's wearing boots.

01:18:15.014 --> 01:18:16.181
BRIAN: And the way the system
works is,

01:18:16.315 --> 01:18:18.616
we first set the geometry up
for all the items

01:18:18.751 --> 01:18:20.385
that you're supposed
to be wearing,

01:18:20.519 --> 01:18:22.687
and then we layer on various
images that we call textures,

01:18:22.821 --> 01:18:25.891
and we'd use those to build up
a single composite texture

01:18:26.025 --> 01:18:27.393
that's then wrapped
around the geometry,

01:18:27.526 --> 01:18:29.861
but of course, in this case,
when we were layering them on,

01:18:29.995 --> 01:18:31.029
none of the textures
were assigned,

01:18:31.163 --> 01:18:35.100
so we just started
with his naked body,

01:18:35.234 --> 01:18:37.536
and then was going to go layer
on textures and didn't find any.

01:18:37.670 --> 01:18:39.437
So it thinks he's dressed.
It's removed the underwear

01:18:39.571 --> 01:18:41.339
'cause it thinks
it's put clothes on,

01:18:41.473 --> 01:18:42.541
but there's nothing there,

01:18:42.675 --> 01:18:43.676
they're all
completely transparent.

01:18:43.809 --> 01:18:44.910
It's like
The Emperor's New Clothes.

01:18:45.044 --> 01:18:46.612
He's wearing invisible clothes,

01:18:46.745 --> 01:18:48.814
only that's really
what's happening.

01:18:48.948 --> 01:18:49.982
-(LAUGHS)
-HOLLY: That's crazy.

01:18:50.115 --> 01:18:52.751
And then of course
I wanted to try draenei,

01:18:52.885 --> 01:18:55.720
because that's where my heart
lies, I'm an Alliance girl.

01:18:55.854 --> 01:18:56.788
(BRIAN LAUGHS)

01:18:56.922 --> 01:18:58.857
And this draenei looks to be

01:18:58.991 --> 01:19:00.092
heads above the rest.

01:19:00.225 --> 01:19:01.893
-PATRICK: That's not the Exodar.
-BRIAN: Yeah.

01:19:02.027 --> 01:19:02.995
HOLLY: No, it... Definitely
not.

01:19:03.128 --> 01:19:04.363
PATRICK: That's Dun Morogh.

01:19:04.496 --> 01:19:06.564
It is,
and that's exactly the problem.

01:19:06.699 --> 01:19:08.100
It looks like
the draenei's too tall,

01:19:08.233 --> 01:19:09.734
but the real problem is
we're in the wrong place.

01:19:09.868 --> 01:19:11.169
And so, yeah.

01:19:11.303 --> 01:19:13.605
When you go to the Dun Morogh
character select screen,

01:19:13.739 --> 01:19:15.373
of course
the camera's positioned

01:19:15.507 --> 01:19:16.374
for much shorter races,

01:19:16.508 --> 01:19:18.543
like gnomes and dwarves.

01:19:18.677 --> 01:19:21.680
BRIAN: So it's knee-high
on a draenei.

01:19:21.814 --> 01:19:23.182
And then, water.

01:19:24.116 --> 01:19:25.217
Really, Brian,
we have water in Hellfire?

01:19:25.351 --> 01:19:26.685
-BRIAN: Yeah, so...
-HOLLY: Go for a swim?

01:19:26.819 --> 01:19:28.653
BRIAN: It turns out that
Outland has

01:19:28.787 --> 01:19:30.722
a funny, unique feature

01:19:30.856 --> 01:19:31.890
that it doesn't have any oceans,

01:19:32.024 --> 01:19:33.291
because the water just
all falls off the edges

01:19:33.425 --> 01:19:35.593
and goes out into space,
or something.

01:19:35.728 --> 01:19:37.763
I'm not sure how that works
in the game universe.

01:19:37.896 --> 01:19:38.997
-Magic!
-BRIAN: But yeah. (LAUGHS)

01:19:39.131 --> 01:19:40.299
There's no ocean there,

01:19:40.432 --> 01:19:42.834
but we'd accidentally flagged it
to generate one

01:19:42.968 --> 01:19:44.369
so we ended up flooding
the entire world with an ocean

01:19:44.503 --> 01:19:47.039
until we found that
and turned it back off (LAUGHS).

01:19:48.073 --> 01:19:49.674
So through all
of these processes,

01:19:49.808 --> 01:19:52.411
yes, we're obviously
fixing what is broken,

01:19:53.679 --> 01:19:56.048
but also,
we thought it was a good time

01:19:56.181 --> 01:20:00.285
to start enacting
what we call #somechanges...

01:20:00.419 --> 01:20:01.453
(KEVIN LAUGHS)

01:20:01.587 --> 01:20:04.823
...to gameplay
and how things should work.

01:20:04.957 --> 01:20:06.391
Yeah, when you think about it,

01:20:07.126 --> 01:20:09.728
with Classic, our guiding
principle was no changes.

01:20:09.862 --> 01:20:11.363
Our goal was to provide
as true-to-life,

01:20:11.497 --> 01:20:13.698
authentic experience of Classic

01:20:13.832 --> 01:20:15.167
that we possibly could
with the community.

01:20:15.801 --> 01:20:16.535
And we accomplished that.

01:20:17.169 --> 01:20:18.936
What was interesting to me

01:20:19.071 --> 01:20:22.474
is to see this vibrant community
that embraced it, and then said,

01:20:23.175 --> 01:20:25.711
"Hey, but we're not
exactly the same

01:20:25.844 --> 01:20:26.878
as we were 15 years ago, right?"

01:20:27.012 --> 01:20:28.213
And they're right.

01:20:28.347 --> 01:20:29.914
PATRICK: The times have
changed. The world has changed.

01:20:30.682 --> 01:20:34.452
And so, some of those things
that maybe were out there,

01:20:34.586 --> 01:20:38.590
that were not great,
and were warts back in the day,

01:20:38.724 --> 01:20:41.293
is not something that
we would want to ship again.

01:20:41.427 --> 01:20:42.827
Like something that
feels a little buggy,

01:20:42.961 --> 01:20:43.995
or wrong, or incorrect,

01:20:44.129 --> 01:20:46.098
so the idea behind this is,

01:20:46.231 --> 01:20:49.367
let's provide what the
Burning Crusade experience was,

01:20:49.501 --> 01:20:50.568
without those warts.

01:20:50.702 --> 01:20:52.271
This really, I think,
illustrates the...

01:20:54.073 --> 01:20:56.307
The difference
is spell-batching.

01:20:56.975 --> 01:20:58.543
We actually had a lot
of requests originally

01:20:58.677 --> 01:20:59.677
for spell-batching

01:20:59.812 --> 01:21:01.413
in that no-changes charge,

01:21:02.681 --> 01:21:03.448
where they said,
"Hey, I remember

01:21:03.582 --> 01:21:04.382
when I used to be able to have

01:21:04.516 --> 01:21:06.418
two mages polymorph one another,

01:21:06.552 --> 01:21:08.153
and they'd both
end up polymorphed,"

01:21:08.287 --> 01:21:09.354
and that's cute and fun,
I kind of like that,

01:21:09.488 --> 01:21:11.189
and so we said,
"Okay, we can put that back in."

01:21:11.723 --> 01:21:13.691
But the system that causes that
is of course

01:21:13.826 --> 01:21:15.293
that the spells are batched up

01:21:15.427 --> 01:21:17.229
and processed
in these giant batches,

01:21:17.362 --> 01:21:18.396
but over the last two years,

01:21:18.530 --> 01:21:20.665
people realized
that what that comes with

01:21:20.799 --> 01:21:23.635
is about half a second
of built-in latency,

01:21:23.769 --> 01:21:25.137
where you push a button

01:21:25.270 --> 01:21:28.206
and it doesn't actually take
effect for that half a second,

01:21:28.340 --> 01:21:30.175
while it's queueing up
the other ones to go with it.

01:21:30.309 --> 01:21:32.344
And people were starting to
find that frustrating.

01:21:32.478 --> 01:21:34.278
I talked to a lot of people
who said

01:21:34.413 --> 01:21:36.415
they were excited about it when
it first came out with Classic,

01:21:36.548 --> 01:21:38.149
but after they'd been
playing with it for two years,

01:21:38.283 --> 01:21:40.285
they're really ready
to see it go,

01:21:40.419 --> 01:21:41.386
and we're ready
to take it out too,

01:21:41.520 --> 01:21:43.254
so we're removing
spell-batching.

01:21:43.388 --> 01:21:45.323
That's definitely great.

01:21:45.457 --> 01:21:46.624
I remember, every two weeks
I'd get a bug report,

01:21:46.758 --> 01:21:47.759
Vanish is broken again.

01:21:47.893 --> 01:21:50.362
It was because
of stuff like this.

01:21:50.496 --> 01:21:52.597
If you Vanish
when a Fireball hits you...

01:21:52.731 --> 01:21:54.065
-...it's gonna pop you out.
-Exactly.

01:21:54.199 --> 01:21:56.301
And so we were trying to do
all these techy solutions to it,

01:21:56.435 --> 01:21:58.136
but the real answer is
just don't spell-batch.

01:21:58.270 --> 01:22:00.205
Yeah.

01:22:00.339 --> 01:22:01.572
But we didn't have the ability
to do that then,

01:22:01.707 --> 01:22:03.275
-so glad we're doing it now.
-Yeah.

01:22:03.842 --> 01:22:06.111
Another one
that I can think of is

01:22:06.245 --> 01:22:07.546
how the rating community
has matured, right?

01:22:07.679 --> 01:22:11.349
Things were very different back
in 2004, 2005 timelines,

01:22:11.483 --> 01:22:13.284
compared to today.

01:22:13.418 --> 01:22:17.488
So, the rating community
knows exactly what's going on,

01:22:17.623 --> 01:22:18.623
and they want
more of a challenge,

01:22:18.757 --> 01:22:20.792
so rather than
always giving them

01:22:20.926 --> 01:22:22.194
the last version of the boss,

01:22:22.327 --> 01:22:23.762
the final form of the boss,

01:22:23.896 --> 01:22:26.464
which maybe is being
hit with a few nerfs,

01:22:26.598 --> 01:22:27.832
let's not do that anymore.

01:22:27.966 --> 01:22:29.501
Let's give 'em a more
challenging version of the boss.

01:22:29.635 --> 01:22:32.337
I'll give you an example
of M'uru, right,

01:22:32.471 --> 01:22:34.673
where the initial version
of that boss

01:22:34.806 --> 01:22:35.407
had a lot of spell pushback,

01:22:36.942 --> 01:22:38.676
and so that made it really tough

01:22:38.810 --> 01:22:40.478
to bring casting classes
to that fight.

01:22:40.612 --> 01:22:42.114
We did fix that pretty quickly,

01:22:42.247 --> 01:22:44.849
and changed the fight
to allow for that.

01:22:44.983 --> 01:22:47.752
Now that's a change we probably
would incorporate in this.

01:22:47.886 --> 01:22:49.288
We don't want people
to feel excluded,

01:22:49.421 --> 01:22:52.490
or not part of being able to do
a fight like that.

01:22:53.058 --> 01:22:54.725
But then, probably a month
or so after that,

01:22:54.860 --> 01:22:55.394
we nerfed M'uru's health.

01:22:55.994 --> 01:22:57.462
That doesn't need to happen.

01:22:57.596 --> 01:22:59.831
PATRICK: Let's keep it in the
hard form.

01:22:59.965 --> 01:23:02.300
We can evaluate any changes we
need to make after that, but...

01:23:02.935 --> 01:23:04.169
Yeah, we'll be like,
"Hey, final form,

01:23:04.303 --> 01:23:06.305
we're not even gonna look at it.
Let's call it a day."

01:23:06.438 --> 01:23:09.074
Yeah. So how did your mage
handle the pushback?

01:23:09.208 --> 01:23:11.276
-Uh, I just... was sad.
-BRIAN: Sat on the bench?

01:23:11.410 --> 01:23:13.878
-HOLLY: This feels personal!
-LAUGHTER

01:23:14.012 --> 01:23:17.449
No, I was the raid leader, so I
got to choose who went, but...

01:23:17.583 --> 01:23:18.049
The one mage allowed to go.

01:23:18.183 --> 01:23:19.484
LAUGHTER

01:23:19.618 --> 01:23:21.586
That was, honestly,
one of my favorite fights ever

01:23:21.720 --> 01:23:22.654
on World of Warcraft.

01:23:22.788 --> 01:23:24.322
That was a really fun fight.

01:23:24.456 --> 01:23:25.657
That's awesome.

01:23:25.791 --> 01:23:27.558
Another change that
we were talking about making is

01:23:27.693 --> 01:23:30.562
along the lines of the PvP
balance, and faction balance,

01:23:30.696 --> 01:23:33.598
is, obviously,
with Burning Crusade,

01:23:33.732 --> 01:23:35.533
paladins are introduced
to the Horde,

01:23:35.667 --> 01:23:37.668
and shamans are introduced
to the Alliance,

01:23:37.803 --> 01:23:40.538
and that was intended to be
a balancing of the factions,

01:23:40.672 --> 01:23:41.906
and let's make them even.

01:23:42.040 --> 01:23:43.875
And then they wanted to keep
a little bit of flavor

01:23:44.009 --> 01:23:45.744
of that faction identity,

01:23:45.877 --> 01:23:47.278
so they kept Seal of Blood
on the Horde,

01:23:47.412 --> 01:23:50.081
and Seal of Vengeance
on the Alliance.

01:23:50.215 --> 01:23:51.483
It was intended to be
equivalent power abilities

01:23:51.617 --> 01:23:54.018
that were just
slightly different in flavor,

01:23:54.152 --> 01:23:56.554
but in retrospect, Seal of Blood
was just so much better,

01:23:56.688 --> 01:23:57.755
and so one of the changes
we are gonna make

01:23:57.889 --> 01:23:59.124
is we are gonna give

01:23:59.258 --> 01:23:59.925
the opposite faction's seal

01:24:00.058 --> 01:24:01.560
to the other faction,

01:24:01.693 --> 01:24:03.995
but we're gonna do
that at max level.

01:24:04.129 --> 01:24:06.264
So we keep just a little bit
of that faction identity,

01:24:06.398 --> 01:24:09.000
where Horde will get Seal of
Blood at Level 64, on schedule,

01:24:09.134 --> 01:24:10.969
and then get Seal of Vengeance
at Level 70,

01:24:11.103 --> 01:24:14.572
and similarly Alliance will get
Seal of Vengeance at Level 64,

01:24:14.706 --> 01:24:17.042
and then their equivalent,
Seal of the Martyr, at Level 70.

01:24:17.175 --> 01:24:19.677
Another thing we want to talk
about is arena teams.

01:24:19.811 --> 01:24:22.547
BRIAN: When it first came out
in Burning Crusade,

01:24:22.681 --> 01:24:24.282
the arena team system
only let you have

01:24:24.416 --> 01:24:26.585
the same number
of players on your team,

01:24:26.718 --> 01:24:28.186
that was the size of
the bracket it was for.

01:24:28.320 --> 01:24:30.355
So, for example, a 3v3 team

01:24:30.489 --> 01:24:32.324
could only have
three players on the team,

01:24:32.457 --> 01:24:35.293
so if someone was sick,
or unavailable one night,

01:24:35.427 --> 01:24:36.427
you couldn't play.

01:24:36.561 --> 01:24:37.929
That felt really frustrating,

01:24:38.063 --> 01:24:39.431
so we're definitely gonna go
with the last version of that,

01:24:39.564 --> 01:24:42.200
where you could have twice
the size of the bracket

01:24:42.334 --> 01:24:44.069
on your team roster,

01:24:44.202 --> 01:24:45.503
so that you had alternates
you could swap in and out,

01:24:45.637 --> 01:24:47.472
and then that way,

01:24:47.606 --> 01:24:48.373
as long as anybody
had participated

01:24:48.507 --> 01:24:51.376
in I think 30% of the matches,

01:24:51.510 --> 01:24:53.477
then they got the full credit
for the week of participation.

01:24:53.612 --> 01:24:55.146
Is that why we did personal
ratings versus team ratings?

01:24:55.280 --> 01:24:56.281
That came later.

01:24:56.415 --> 01:24:57.916
We're also considering
whether or not

01:24:58.050 --> 01:24:59.717
we wanna do something with that,

01:24:59.851 --> 01:25:03.154
because another thing that was
problematic about arenas

01:25:03.288 --> 01:25:06.558
was the way that the Elo system
was so inflationary,

01:25:06.692 --> 01:25:07.559
and so we're talking
about whether or not

01:25:07.693 --> 01:25:09.027
we want to move forward with

01:25:09.161 --> 01:25:11.462
converting that to something
like the modern MMR system,

01:25:11.596 --> 01:25:13.898
so that it isn't
as punishing to people

01:25:14.032 --> 01:25:17.535
in terms of waiting hours for a
match at the highest levels,

01:25:17.669 --> 01:25:18.603
and things like that.

01:25:18.737 --> 01:25:19.637
And another change.

01:25:19.771 --> 01:25:22.907
We're going to give
players access

01:25:23.041 --> 01:25:25.477
to blood elf and draenei early,

01:25:25.610 --> 01:25:28.813
so that before we actually
open the Dark Portal,

01:25:28.947 --> 01:25:30.449
you'll have a chance
to level those up.

01:25:30.582 --> 01:25:31.916
That's definitely not something
we did originally.

01:25:32.050 --> 01:25:33.985
-(LAUGHS)
-Yeah!

01:25:34.119 --> 01:25:35.854
I remember, I had a friend who
was really frustrated

01:25:35.987 --> 01:25:37.755
that the rest of us were
running around in Outland,

01:25:37.889 --> 01:25:40.257
and he was like, "I'm still back
in Wetlands." Yeah.

01:25:40.392 --> 01:25:42.026
LAUGHTER

01:25:42.160 --> 01:25:43.094
-"What is going on?"
-HOLLY: Yeah.

01:25:43.228 --> 01:25:44.362
"When can I play with you guys?"

01:25:44.496 --> 01:25:45.730
So yeah, I'm really glad
we're doing that.

01:25:45.864 --> 01:25:47.632
-HOLLY: Yeah.
-Is that pre-patch?

01:25:47.766 --> 01:25:48.800
-Yeah, that's the plan.
-Very cool.

01:25:48.934 --> 01:25:50.735
Pre-patch will come out
and you'll be able

01:25:50.869 --> 01:25:52.136
to start your blood elves
and draenei.

01:25:52.270 --> 01:25:54.005
And then... content.

01:25:54.139 --> 01:25:55.907
So, we've got
some phases happening.

01:25:56.041 --> 01:25:57.275
-Yeah.
-HOLLY: Yeah.

01:25:57.409 --> 01:25:59.044
So the content phases
that we got coming are exciting.

01:25:59.177 --> 01:26:01.646
We're gonna start with Karazhan,

01:26:02.381 --> 01:26:05.283
and Gruul's Lair
and Magtheridon's Lair.

01:26:05.917 --> 01:26:08.486
BRIAN: And we want to start
with just that at release,

01:26:08.620 --> 01:26:10.121
because we found with Classic

01:26:10.255 --> 01:26:12.657
that people really enjoy
that gear progression,

01:26:12.791 --> 01:26:14.191
and that's where all
the Tier Four gear is,

01:26:14.326 --> 01:26:15.627
is with those raids.

01:26:15.761 --> 01:26:18.462
And we want people to have
an opportunity to level up,

01:26:18.597 --> 01:26:19.664
catch up to their friends,

01:26:19.798 --> 01:26:21.466
and all go through
these things together.

01:26:21.600 --> 01:26:22.900
So as we unlock each chapter,

01:26:23.034 --> 01:26:24.468
we want to make sure everybody's
had enough time to be

01:26:24.603 --> 01:26:25.537
a part of that chapter.

01:26:25.670 --> 01:26:28.139
And the next chapter after that,

01:26:28.273 --> 01:26:30.508
will be Phase Two, which will be
Serpentshrine Cavern,

01:26:30.642 --> 01:26:32.944
-and Tempest Keep.
-HOLLY: Yep.

01:26:33.078 --> 01:26:34.612
Then next we move on
to Phase Three,

01:26:34.746 --> 01:26:36.080
which is Hyjal
and the Black Temple.

01:26:36.982 --> 01:26:40.184
BRIAN: And the reason that's a
change is of course, originally,

01:26:40.318 --> 01:26:43.354
Hyjal was available from the
launch of Burning Crusade,

01:26:43.488 --> 01:26:44.555
but nobody could get in

01:26:44.689 --> 01:26:46.357
because of the way the
achievements work,

01:26:46.491 --> 01:26:48.927
so we'll unlock that as a later
chapter alongside Black Temple.

01:26:49.895 --> 01:26:53.264
And then, that'll go with
Arena Season Two at that point.

01:26:53.398 --> 01:26:56.034
And Arena Season Three will
come out alongside Zul'Aman,

01:26:56.168 --> 01:26:57.468
with a little bit of catch-up
gear for anyone

01:26:57.602 --> 01:26:58.804
who's taken that long
to catch up.

01:26:58.937 --> 01:27:01.372
It's another smaller instance,
a ten-man.

01:27:01.940 --> 01:27:03.141
And then finally,

01:27:03.275 --> 01:27:05.343
Phase Five will be
Sunwell and Arena Season Four.

01:27:05.477 --> 01:27:07.478
-PATRICK: Tons of content.
-HOLLY: Been very busy.

01:27:07.612 --> 01:27:08.646
LAUGHTER

01:27:08.780 --> 01:27:10.748
It is, it's an expansion
with a lot in it.

01:27:10.882 --> 01:27:12.850
And so we spent
a lot of time talking

01:27:12.984 --> 01:27:14.118
about Burning Crusade Classic,

01:27:14.252 --> 01:27:15.020
and Burning Crusade,

01:27:15.887 --> 01:27:18.623
which is exciting,
we're very excited about it,

01:27:18.757 --> 01:27:22.160
but there's a lot to talk about,
there's a lot coming,

01:27:22.294 --> 01:27:24.529
and we want to walk through how
this is going to materialize.

01:27:24.663 --> 01:27:26.564
Well yeah,
really the question is,

01:27:26.698 --> 01:27:27.965
we have a diverse player base

01:27:28.099 --> 01:27:30.502
where people have a lot of
different wants and needs.

01:27:30.635 --> 01:27:32.737
How do we get there? How do we
get to the Burning Crusade?

01:27:32.871 --> 01:27:34.472
Well, the first group of people

01:27:34.606 --> 01:27:37.041
that we wanted to make sure
that we were serving well

01:27:37.175 --> 01:27:39.410
is those people who do want to
go to the Burning Crusade.

01:27:39.544 --> 01:27:41.879
We feel that most of the
community is interested in this,

01:27:42.013 --> 01:27:43.214
they're gonna want to do this,

01:27:43.348 --> 01:27:44.615
we want to make it
simple and easy for them.

01:27:44.749 --> 01:27:46.884
So, how are we gonna do that?

01:27:47.018 --> 01:27:49.154
As we said earlier, you're going
to be able to make a choice.

01:27:49.287 --> 01:27:52.223
So we'll talk about and explore
these choices in some detail,

01:27:52.357 --> 01:27:54.459
and I'll give you some examples.
(CHUCKLES)

01:27:54.593 --> 01:27:58.329
So, to start with, the current
servers you're on now,

01:27:58.463 --> 01:28:00.197
Classic live servers,

01:28:00.332 --> 01:28:01.866
are gonna become what we're
calling progression servers.

01:28:02.000 --> 01:28:04.302
And what that means is,

01:28:04.436 --> 01:28:05.870
the servers that are live now

01:28:06.004 --> 01:28:08.172
will always unlock
any expansion we release.

01:28:08.740 --> 01:28:11.409
So, when we get to
our patch day,

01:28:11.543 --> 01:28:13.445
you're gonna be presented
with a choice.

01:28:13.578 --> 01:28:15.780
Do you want to continue on
into the Burning Crusade,

01:28:16.748 --> 01:28:18.416
or do you want
to move to Classic?

01:28:18.550 --> 01:28:21.019
So, in that space,
you're gonna do that

01:28:21.152 --> 01:28:22.620
for every single character.

01:28:22.754 --> 01:28:25.723
For my example,
I'm on Wyrmrest Accord.

01:28:25.857 --> 01:28:27.525
So I've got a Level 60

01:28:27.659 --> 01:28:30.595
I'm definitely taking that on
through to Burning Crusade,

01:28:30.729 --> 01:28:31.763
but I've got a couple
of mid-levels

01:28:31.897 --> 01:28:33.965
that I know I want to move
with my friends.

01:28:34.099 --> 01:28:35.833
Those are characters
I have level

01:28:35.967 --> 01:28:37.001
with my friends in classing

01:28:37.135 --> 01:28:38.470
and I want to continue
that journey with them.

01:28:38.603 --> 01:28:41.238
We want to make it
as easy and seamless as possible

01:28:41.373 --> 01:28:42.974
to just get right into
Burning Crusade,

01:28:43.108 --> 01:28:44.275
but there are options.

01:28:44.409 --> 01:28:46.378
Well that's awesome,
I'm excited to hear that.

01:28:46.511 --> 01:28:49.213
I know there's a lot of
my friends, for example,

01:28:49.347 --> 01:28:53.551
who maybe didn't really play
Classic when it first came out,

01:28:53.685 --> 01:28:54.452
and so now they're behind.

01:28:55.120 --> 01:28:57.288
And they're interested in
playing the Burning Crusade.

01:28:57.422 --> 01:28:58.356
This was, for a lot of people,

01:28:58.490 --> 01:28:59.591
their favorite expansion
that we've done.

01:29:01.459 --> 01:29:04.028
We want to make sure that
those people are served too,

01:29:04.162 --> 01:29:05.530
and one of the ways
we can do that

01:29:05.664 --> 01:29:08.833
is provide a boost to people who
are interested in coming back,

01:29:08.967 --> 01:29:10.034
but maybe aren't interested

01:29:10.168 --> 01:29:12.403
in doing the Classic WoW
experience,

01:29:12.537 --> 01:29:14.338
but want to hop right in to
Outlands as quickly as possible.

01:29:14.472 --> 01:29:15.573
Yeah, so, the team,

01:29:15.707 --> 01:29:17.408
we spent quite a bit of time
discussing it

01:29:17.542 --> 01:29:20.544
because we want to make sure
we respect the time

01:29:20.679 --> 01:29:23.915
and the people that have been
playing Classic for two years,

01:29:24.049 --> 01:29:27.618
but also we want people to
be able to enjoy Burning Crusade

01:29:27.752 --> 01:29:30.455
if they loved it the first time
and maybe missed Classic.

01:29:30.589 --> 01:29:35.293
So, we are gonna offer
a Level 58 boost for purchase,

01:29:35.427 --> 01:29:36.594
but it does come
with some restrictions.

01:29:36.728 --> 01:29:37.362
So, it's gonna be
one per account.

01:29:38.563 --> 01:29:41.599
It's not gonna be usable
on blood elf or draenei.

01:29:42.334 --> 01:29:45.603
It'll have some dungeon blues,
it'll have Level 40 mount skill,

01:29:45.737 --> 01:29:47.671
no professions, it's the basics.

01:29:47.806 --> 01:29:50.675
Basically your Outlands
starting kit.

01:29:50.809 --> 01:29:51.876
That's cool.
That's a really great way

01:29:52.010 --> 01:29:53.511
to jump into the game, for sure.

01:29:53.645 --> 01:29:55.580
-Yeah, I like that.
-I'll be looking forward to it.

01:29:55.714 --> 01:29:58.649
Look, I know a lot of people
came back to play WoW Classic.

01:29:58.783 --> 01:30:00.051
That's all they want,
all they ever wanted.

01:30:00.185 --> 01:30:01.619
They're like, "You know what?

01:30:01.753 --> 01:30:04.422
Burning Crusade, that's awesome,
people are gonna love that,

01:30:04.556 --> 01:30:06.491
but for me,
I came back for WoW Classic."

01:30:06.625 --> 01:30:08.693
What do they get to do?
What's their options?

01:30:08.827 --> 01:30:10.528
Yeah, that was
really important for us too.

01:30:10.662 --> 01:30:14.064
So, we had all sat down
and discussed

01:30:14.199 --> 01:30:16.768
how we should frame this,

01:30:16.901 --> 01:30:18.836
so, when we get
to our patch day,

01:30:18.970 --> 01:30:20.972
which is prior
to the Dark Portal opening,

01:30:21.773 --> 01:30:26.077
when I said before, I'll go back
to my example, for Wyrmrest,

01:30:26.211 --> 01:30:28.546
we will be launching
Classic-era realms

01:30:28.680 --> 01:30:31.983
at the same time
as we do our patch,

01:30:32.517 --> 01:30:33.818
and you will have that choice.

01:30:33.952 --> 01:30:37.021
So, when you make the choice to
go to Burning Crusade Classic,

01:30:37.989 --> 01:30:39.924
or you make the choice,
like on my example,

01:30:40.058 --> 01:30:41.325
my Level 40 druid,

01:30:41.459 --> 01:30:44.195
I want to move that character
to Classic era,

01:30:44.329 --> 01:30:45.963
and what Classic era means,

01:30:46.097 --> 01:30:48.232
is that those particular servers

01:30:48.366 --> 01:30:50.968
are going to stay
in that era forever,

01:30:51.102 --> 01:30:53.604
so that is going to remain
Classic till the end of time.

01:30:53.738 --> 01:30:55.406
It's never going to
unlock expansions.

01:30:55.940 --> 01:30:57.308
You'll be able
to make that choice

01:30:57.442 --> 01:30:58.710
for every single character.

01:30:58.843 --> 01:30:59.877
Do you go on to Burning Crusade,

01:31:00.011 --> 01:31:01.279
or do you choose
to go to Classic era?

01:31:01.413 --> 01:31:05.350
Let's say you're behind
the times a little bit.

01:31:05.483 --> 01:31:06.517
You'll still be able
to have that choice.

01:31:06.651 --> 01:31:08.953
We haven't locked it
to a time window.

01:31:09.087 --> 01:31:10.788
You're not gonna miss
the boat to era.

01:31:10.922 --> 01:31:11.956
The choice sounds awesome,

01:31:12.090 --> 01:31:13.724
and choice in WoW
is something meaningful,

01:31:13.858 --> 01:31:15.993
it's always been.

01:31:16.127 --> 01:31:17.194
I think it's something
that's important

01:31:17.328 --> 01:31:18.830
when we look at the community

01:31:18.963 --> 01:31:20.465
that you're gonna choose to go
to the Burning Crusade

01:31:20.598 --> 01:31:21.666
or you're gonna choose
to go to Classic.

01:31:22.934 --> 01:31:24.369
Some people may not be
comfortable with that choice.

01:31:24.502 --> 01:31:26.938
And while we think that
that choice is important,

01:31:28.306 --> 01:31:29.273
we also recognize
that there are folks

01:31:29.407 --> 01:31:31.109
that are gonna want
to play both.

01:31:31.242 --> 01:31:32.176
-Right? Like-- Yeah. See.
-LAUGHTER

01:31:32.310 --> 01:31:33.244
Right here.

01:31:33.378 --> 01:31:34.112
Maybe you have
some stuff left to do

01:31:34.245 --> 01:31:35.613
in Classic that you wanted,

01:31:35.747 --> 01:31:36.981
you want to
keep that character in Classic,

01:31:37.115 --> 01:31:39.250
but you're also interested,

01:31:39.384 --> 01:31:40.685
that same character
wants to raid with your friends.

01:31:40.819 --> 01:31:43.521
-BRIAN: That's exactly--
-So what does that look like?

01:31:43.655 --> 01:31:44.622
How do we make that happen?

01:31:44.756 --> 01:31:46.691
So we're gonna offer
a paid service

01:31:46.825 --> 01:31:48.927
to let you make a copy
of your character,

01:31:49.060 --> 01:31:50.395
and I know a copy's a big deal,
so like you said,

01:31:50.528 --> 01:31:52.429
this is something
we don't want to make

01:31:52.564 --> 01:31:54.164
just an automatic choice
for everybody,

01:31:54.299 --> 01:31:55.533
but I know that for me,

01:31:55.667 --> 01:31:59.069
it really is gonna be that pull
to go to both places,

01:31:59.204 --> 01:32:00.705
so I want to be able to say,
like, "You know what?

01:32:00.839 --> 01:32:01.606
I'm willing to pay for this.

01:32:01.740 --> 01:32:03.308
I want a copy of my character

01:32:03.441 --> 01:32:04.475
that lives
in Classic era forever,

01:32:04.609 --> 01:32:06.210
while I also go
to Burning Crusade

01:32:06.344 --> 01:32:08.079
with that character."

01:32:08.646 --> 01:32:09.881
That way,
I can stay with my friends

01:32:10.014 --> 01:32:12.116
who are really excited
to go to Karazhan,

01:32:12.250 --> 01:32:13.184
I'm really excited
to go to Karazhan,

01:32:13.318 --> 01:32:14.385
I want to see my hunter
go to Karazhan,

01:32:14.519 --> 01:32:16.520
but if I'm missing
a couple pieces

01:32:16.654 --> 01:32:18.589
of my Tier Three set
from Naxxramas,

01:32:18.723 --> 01:32:20.891
I still have an opportunity
to go find a new guild there,

01:32:21.025 --> 01:32:23.927
and go through Naxxramas again
until I finish that set.

01:32:24.062 --> 01:32:26.998
We'll be spending a lot of time
communicating these details,

01:32:27.132 --> 01:32:28.833
so even as much detail
as we're giving now,

01:32:28.967 --> 01:32:30.068
it's very high-level,

01:32:30.201 --> 01:32:31.402
there's going to be a lot more
detail to come,

01:32:31.536 --> 01:32:33.037
so keep an ear out.

01:32:33.171 --> 01:32:34.972
But, with that said... So, Pat?

01:32:35.707 --> 01:32:36.640
-Hm?
-When's all this happening?

01:32:36.775 --> 01:32:37.775
Oh.

01:32:37.909 --> 01:32:39.844
LAUGHTER

01:32:39.978 --> 01:32:40.911
Soon.

01:32:41.045 --> 01:32:42.447
-Yay!
-LAUGHTER

01:32:42.580 --> 01:32:45.583
So yeah, this is something
that's important to get.

01:32:45.717 --> 01:32:47.685
People are playing
through Naxxramas,

01:32:47.819 --> 01:32:49.120
we want to make sure
they have time to do that,

01:32:49.254 --> 01:32:52.290
but we are looking at,
this will be happening in 2021.

01:32:53.625 --> 01:32:56.461
For sure,
we're committed to that.

01:32:56.594 --> 01:32:57.962
And what we're looking at is
starting the beta rather soon.

01:32:58.930 --> 01:33:00.431
So hopefully
that beta will go up,

01:33:00.565 --> 01:33:01.832
and we get that running,

01:33:01.966 --> 01:33:03.401
the community can come
and participate, help us out.

01:33:04.769 --> 01:33:06.304
We'll start to hear
some feedback

01:33:06.437 --> 01:33:07.572
on the things
we've talked about today.

01:33:08.206 --> 01:33:09.908
If there's any
adjustments to make,

01:33:10.041 --> 01:33:10.742
we wanna make sure
we have a long enough

01:33:10.875 --> 01:33:12.043
beta period for that.

01:33:12.177 --> 01:33:13.310
Then we'll get that out
to the players,

01:33:13.444 --> 01:33:15.045
so you can all enter
the Dark Portal.

01:33:15.180 --> 01:33:16.413
-Yay!
-Yeah, it's exciting.

01:33:16.548 --> 01:33:18.649
LAUGHTER

01:33:18.783 --> 01:33:21.018
So, any final words?

01:33:21.152 --> 01:33:22.520
Yeah,
I just talked about the beta,

01:33:22.654 --> 01:33:24.789
and I will say just
to the community,

01:33:24.923 --> 01:33:27.458
you all have been
so instrumental

01:33:27.592 --> 01:33:29.993
in making Classic
the success that it has been,

01:33:30.128 --> 01:33:31.462
and you'll be equally
instrumental

01:33:31.596 --> 01:33:33.865
in making the Burning Crusade
a tremendous success also,

01:33:33.998 --> 01:33:37.435
so, please, get in that beta,
play, give us your feedback,

01:33:37.569 --> 01:33:38.469
we want to hear
all about it, so...

01:33:39.671 --> 01:33:41.071
Enjoy, welcome,
and let's have some fun.

01:33:41.206 --> 01:33:42.507
-Yay!
-Yeah.

01:33:42.640 --> 01:33:44.642
And it's been the highlight
of my professional career

01:33:44.776 --> 01:33:45.977
to work on WoW Classic

01:33:46.110 --> 01:33:46.878
and have the support and love
of this community.

01:33:47.812 --> 01:33:49.480
It is really special to me,

01:33:49.614 --> 01:33:50.814
and I'm really excited to be
sharing this with all of you

01:33:50.949 --> 01:33:51.983
in the Burning Crusade.

01:33:53.218 --> 01:33:55.386
Yeah, and for me,

01:33:55.520 --> 01:33:57.388
I've been playing Classic
since it launched,

01:33:59.190 --> 01:34:00.958
and I wasn't working
at Blizzard then,

01:34:01.092 --> 01:34:02.893
and it's changed
my life, obviously.

01:34:03.027 --> 01:34:04.695
(LAUGHS)

01:34:04.829 --> 01:34:07.665
So, from me
and the entire WoW team,

01:34:07.799 --> 01:34:10.401
we want to thank you for
bringing Azeroth to life for us,

01:34:10.535 --> 01:34:12.603
so we will definitely be
seeing you in game

01:34:12.737 --> 01:34:13.805
in whatever flavor you choose.

01:34:14.505 --> 01:34:16.707
(CHUCKLES) And until then, stay
Classic.

01:34:17.041 --> 01:34:18.776
SOFT MUSIC PLAYS

01:35:41.125 --> 01:35:44.495
(MENACING MUSIC PLAYING)

01:35:46.264 --> 01:35:48.132
Ever since we
announced Diablo IV

01:35:48.266 --> 01:35:49.400
at the last
BlizzCon in 2019,

01:35:50.468 --> 01:35:52.203
we've been sharing
updates with you guys

01:35:52.337 --> 01:35:53.337
through our blog,

01:35:53.471 --> 01:35:55.373
whether it's items,

01:35:55.573 --> 01:35:56.807
skills,

01:35:57.275 --> 01:35:59.610
zones, even
user interface updates.

01:35:59.744 --> 01:36:01.712
You guys seem to
have really enjoyed that.

01:36:01.846 --> 01:36:04.548
Today, to mark the
special occasion of BlizzConline

01:36:04.682 --> 01:36:06.851
on Blizzard's
30th anniversary,

01:36:07.118 --> 01:36:10.787
rather than doing a blog, we're
going to share lots of videos,

01:36:10.922 --> 01:36:13.924
introduce a new class, like
we saw in the Opening Ceremony,

01:36:14.826 --> 01:36:16.460
and cover some
open world topics, including

01:36:16.694 --> 01:36:17.628
mounts,

01:36:17.762 --> 01:36:19.230
camps and PvP.

01:36:19.364 --> 01:36:23.133
This is the ultimate blog
post. We get to hand deliver it.

01:36:23.267 --> 01:36:24.568
This is the dream.

01:36:24.702 --> 01:36:28.372
It's all finally out there
and we can talk about all of it.

01:36:28.573 --> 01:36:31.942
Just in case some of you missed
it in the Opening Ceremony,

01:36:32.076 --> 01:36:33.077
let's take a look at the
Rogue announcement video.

01:36:36.881 --> 01:36:40.484
MAN: Your prayers
do not fall on deaf ears.

01:36:40.618 --> 01:36:43.320
(OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING)

01:36:43.454 --> 01:36:46.324
Just know they
are being heard.

01:36:48.359 --> 01:36:50.561
There is justice
within the Light...

01:36:51.829 --> 01:36:54.399
but you
have to be patient.

01:36:57.301 --> 01:36:59.404
Now, pray with me.

01:37:01.906 --> 01:37:04.108
May Akarat
guide and protect me.

01:37:05.276 --> 01:37:08.946
WOMAN: May
Akarat guide and protect me.

01:37:09.647 --> 01:37:12.182
May he shepherd my soul...

01:37:12.316 --> 01:37:14.752
May he shepherd my soul...

01:37:15.520 --> 01:37:17.922
...and grant it mercy.

01:37:20.391 --> 01:37:22.226
...and grant it mercy.

01:37:23.327 --> 01:37:24.595
What are you doing here?

01:37:24.729 --> 01:37:27.265
This is not the time.

01:37:28.499 --> 01:37:29.934
But this is confession.

01:37:32.370 --> 01:37:35.206
And I have sinned.

01:37:37.742 --> 01:37:38.543
I was a thief...

01:37:39.844 --> 01:37:42.680
who stole from
those more fortunate.

01:37:42.814 --> 01:37:44.715
(DRAMATIC,
PERCUSSIVE MUSIC PLAYING)

01:37:46.417 --> 01:37:49.220
I strayed
from your Light...

01:37:50.321 --> 01:37:53.257
and found my
trade in the Shadows.

01:37:57.161 --> 01:37:58.529
They call it murder.

01:37:59.664 --> 01:38:03.334
I say job well done.

01:38:03.468 --> 01:38:04.569
(YELLING)

01:38:06.671 --> 01:38:08.439
(GRUNTING AND YELLING)

01:38:10.341 --> 01:38:11.576
PRIEST: Stop.

01:38:12.076 --> 01:38:12.410
Stop!

01:38:13.211 --> 01:38:14.312
Enough!

01:38:17.982 --> 01:38:19.150
I am a thief...

01:38:19.750 --> 01:38:21.319
a heretic...

01:38:22.053 --> 01:38:24.822
a murderer, Father.

01:38:26.457 --> 01:38:28.826
Will Akarat save me?

01:38:31.696 --> 01:38:32.797
(CHUCKLES)

01:38:33.498 --> 01:38:34.165
You mock our Light...

01:38:36.200 --> 01:38:39.504
but those monsters
were a scourge upon my flock.

01:38:41.706 --> 01:38:41.906
And you...

01:38:45.176 --> 01:38:48.412
you were the
answer to their prayers.

01:38:49.780 --> 01:38:51.682
WOMAN:
Then we are settled.

01:38:52.283 --> 01:38:53.151
And you owe me.

01:38:56.320 --> 01:38:58.322
The name you seek is...

01:39:02.460 --> 01:39:04.829
Thank the Heavens for you.

01:39:06.063 --> 01:39:07.098
Heavens?

01:39:07.832 --> 01:39:09.033
I assure you, Father...

01:39:11.569 --> 01:39:12.570
the Heavens
didn't send me.

01:39:21.112 --> 01:39:21.212
(GRUNTING)

01:39:23.314 --> 01:39:24.348
(DRAMATIC,
PERCUSSIVE MUSIC PLAYING)

01:39:39.764 --> 01:39:39.830
(HORSE WHINNIES)

01:39:42.400 --> 01:39:44.068
(SHATTERING)

01:39:50.875 --> 01:39:51.108
(ROGUE YELLS)

01:40:01.085 --> 01:40:01.185
(SCREECHES)

01:40:06.991 --> 01:40:09.193
(OMINOUS MUSIC PLAYING)

01:40:31.082 --> 01:40:33.284
Wow, I love that ending.

01:40:33.417 --> 01:40:34.418
(CHUCKLES)

01:40:35.019 --> 01:40:36.320
There's two things.

01:40:36.454 --> 01:40:38.689
There's the Rain of Arrows
off the mount for the win.

01:40:38.823 --> 01:40:39.890
Then,

01:40:40.224 --> 01:40:43.593
when the guy takes
the ear and he stretches it

01:40:43.728 --> 01:40:45.596
onto that hook
right at the end.

01:40:45.730 --> 01:40:46.697
It's like...
(SHUDDERS AND CHUCKLES)

01:40:46.831 --> 01:40:49.567
LUIS: That ear
box never gets old.

01:40:49.700 --> 01:40:51.268
I always wondered
where those ears from Diablo II,

01:40:51.402 --> 01:40:52.969
when you kill
other players, went.

01:40:53.104 --> 01:40:54.305
JOHN: Now you know.

01:40:54.438 --> 01:40:56.907
There's some creepy
guy in a church collecting ears

01:40:57.041 --> 01:40:57.974
and putting them in a box.

01:40:58.109 --> 01:40:58.576
What we always thought.

01:40:58.709 --> 01:41:01.044
No spoilers,

01:41:01.178 --> 01:41:02.713
but we're going to be hearing a
little bit more about world PvP

01:41:02.847 --> 01:41:04.014
later in the segment,

01:41:04.148 --> 01:41:06.817
but ears are
definitely making a comeback.

01:41:07.084 --> 01:41:08.285
Let's go
back to the class,

01:41:08.419 --> 01:41:10.421
because there's
nothing more exciting

01:41:10.554 --> 01:41:11.988
to be able to
introduce to a Diablo game

01:41:12.390 --> 01:41:14.658
than another class
for players to check out.

01:41:14.792 --> 01:41:18.028
This is a pretty cool thing. We
don't get to do this very often.

01:41:18.162 --> 01:41:19.896
I think it was
you that said, John,

01:41:20.031 --> 01:41:21.065
"There's always
room around the campfire."

01:41:21.198 --> 01:41:22.500
Always a little room.

01:41:22.633 --> 01:41:25.736
We had our very core
archetypes to begin with.

01:41:26.070 --> 01:41:28.405
The brute
strength of the Barbarian,

01:41:28.539 --> 01:41:30.907
the arcane
knowledge of the Sorceress,

01:41:31.208 --> 01:41:34.077
a return of a shape-shifting
classic with the Druid.

01:41:34.278 --> 01:41:38.215
No RPG line-up is complete
without that dexterity class

01:41:38.349 --> 01:41:41.419
that's defeating enemies
with finesse, with speed.

01:41:42.153 --> 01:41:45.088
We kept coming
back to the idea of bringing it

01:41:45.222 --> 01:41:49.626
to the godfather of all
dexterity-based archetypes,

01:41:50.061 --> 01:41:53.397
the Rogue,
that we saw in Diablo I.

01:41:53.531 --> 01:41:56.300
You've got to be
excited to have the Rogue back.

01:41:56.434 --> 01:41:59.402
I know that when we were
exploring different classes,

01:41:59.537 --> 01:42:02.306
you would talk about some of
your experiences with Diablo I.

01:42:02.940 --> 01:42:04.207
For sure.

01:42:04.342 --> 01:42:06.543
One of the
things that's super cool is

01:42:07.044 --> 01:42:10.413
the Rogue was always
able to adapt their play style.

01:42:10.548 --> 01:42:14.217
Fans of the Diablo I, Diablo
II Rogues will be able to create

01:42:14.352 --> 01:42:15.353
very archetypical Rogues,

01:42:15.486 --> 01:42:17.087
starting with
the weapons selection.

01:42:17.221 --> 01:42:18.489
On the melee weapons side,

01:42:18.622 --> 01:42:20.257
Rogues can use
swords and daggers,

01:42:22.860 --> 01:42:24.829
as well as bows and
crossbows on the ranged side.

01:42:26.397 --> 01:42:30.234
JOHN: The dagger
attacks mixed with bow attacks,

01:42:30.368 --> 01:42:32.036
it just looks
great on screen.

01:42:32.703 --> 01:42:33.637
With all the classes

01:42:33.771 --> 01:42:34.905
having this
strong identity

01:42:35.039 --> 01:42:37.141
as far as the
animations and what they do,

01:42:37.274 --> 01:42:39.643
you think about the
Druid and shape-shifting

01:42:39.777 --> 01:42:40.844
or the Barbarian

01:42:40.978 --> 01:42:42.712
with his leap attacks,

01:42:43.814 --> 01:42:45.682
there's these great things
you discover with the Rogue

01:42:45.816 --> 01:42:48.084
that you can't really
do with other classes.

01:42:48.319 --> 01:42:51.188
I love that you
can start cosmetically

01:42:51.322 --> 01:42:54.458
from such an iconic place where
you're wearing the red armor,

01:42:54.925 --> 01:42:57.761
you look like a part of the
Sisterhood of the Sightless Eye

01:42:57.895 --> 01:42:59.329
from the original games.

01:42:59.830 --> 01:42:59.896
You can maybe even

01:43:01.132 --> 01:43:03.267
add some tattoos
into the mix, some scars,

01:43:04.135 --> 01:43:05.669
make that Rogue your own.

01:43:06.203 --> 01:43:07.771
Or if your
fantasy of the Rogue

01:43:07.905 --> 01:43:11.141
is a more
traditional RPG outlaw,

01:43:11.375 --> 01:43:13.377
you're definitely
able to create that

01:43:13.511 --> 01:43:15.913
and put on your
hood and half mask

01:43:16.046 --> 01:43:18.515
and be more of a
shadowy character.

01:43:18.649 --> 01:43:20.684
JOHN: The character
customization, you mentioned it,

01:43:20.818 --> 01:43:25.522
being able to craft the class of
your dreams or your nightmares,

01:43:25.656 --> 01:43:27.958
was a feature that I was
nervous about when we started,

01:43:28.092 --> 01:43:30.227
because we'd never
had this in a Diablo game.

01:43:30.361 --> 01:43:31.729
But seeing
where it is now,

01:43:31.862 --> 01:43:33.697
it's one of those
things I'm super excited about,

01:43:33.831 --> 01:43:35.432
with where we're at.

01:43:35.566 --> 01:43:37.367
There's a lot of
creativity and fun looks.

01:43:37.501 --> 01:43:41.605
You can be that
tattooed, criminal outlaw

01:43:41.739 --> 01:43:43.040
or you can be a
more noble-looking

01:43:43.474 --> 01:43:47.745
thief with good morals.

01:43:49.013 --> 01:43:51.048
One of the things
that we kept running with,

01:43:51.182 --> 01:43:51.749
beyond the cosmetics,

01:43:51.882 --> 01:43:52.883
was customization.

01:43:53.551 --> 01:43:57.421
We had this niche
to fill in the class line-up

01:43:57.555 --> 01:43:59.890
that spoke to the finesse
player, to the dexterity player.

01:44:00.991 --> 01:44:03.327
Everyone's idea
of what that could be

01:44:03.461 --> 01:44:04.895
was a little
bit different,

01:44:05.029 --> 01:44:07.931
whether it leaned
more on range or melee,

01:44:08.232 --> 01:44:11.368
but we took that a
little bit of a step further.

01:44:11.569 --> 01:44:15.305
We wanted to have
each class in our game

01:44:15.439 --> 01:44:17.841
have that one
thing that makes you go,

01:44:17.975 --> 01:44:19.543
"That's bullshit!"
when you see a player use it,

01:44:19.677 --> 01:44:20.744
so you want to
re-roll that class,

01:44:20.878 --> 01:44:22.579
because no
other class gets it.

01:44:22.880 --> 01:44:25.315
For the Rogue,
it's the class specialization.

01:44:25.449 --> 01:44:27.117
The 3 specializations
are Combo Points...

01:44:29.286 --> 01:44:29.386
Shadow Realm...

01:44:32.156 --> 01:44:32.623
and Exploit Weakness.

01:44:34.492 --> 01:44:35.426
I like Shadow
Realm, because I can

01:44:35.559 --> 01:44:38.295
take the problem
and make it smaller.

01:44:38.429 --> 01:44:40.163
I just pull 3 guys
into the Shadow Realm

01:44:40.297 --> 01:44:42.065
and duke it out.

01:44:42.199 --> 01:44:43.333
Then I come back in

01:44:43.467 --> 01:44:46.069
and I'm like,
"All right, smaller problem."

01:44:46.804 --> 01:44:48.772
I love the idea of
Exploit Weakness.

01:44:48.906 --> 01:44:51.908
The way it works is, during
some of the enemy attacks,

01:44:52.376 --> 01:44:55.212
players will see an icon
flash over the enemy's head

01:44:55.346 --> 01:44:56.746
during which time
they will be get to retaliate

01:44:56.881 --> 01:44:59.816
with much more
damage than an ordinary attack.

01:45:00.217 --> 01:45:01.184
If you're
quick on the draw,

01:45:01.318 --> 01:45:03.387
this is the
specialization for you.

01:45:03.521 --> 01:45:06.390
I'm a little bit
skill-capped there.

01:45:06.524 --> 01:45:08.892
Every time I
try to make it work,

01:45:09.026 --> 01:45:11.227
I'm just a little bit
shy of the skill level

01:45:11.362 --> 01:45:12.296
that's required for that.

01:45:12.663 --> 01:45:13.797
It's almost like
you're a surgeon.

01:45:14.598 --> 01:45:17.467
It requires a
really strong ability

01:45:17.601 --> 01:45:19.636
to assess what's happening

01:45:19.770 --> 01:45:21.104
around you.

01:45:21.238 --> 01:45:23.640
My personal favorite is the
Combo Points specialization.

01:45:23.774 --> 01:45:25.742
As you might expect,

01:45:25.876 --> 01:45:28.445
this ability lets you
build multiple combo points,

01:45:28.579 --> 01:45:30.647
which enhance your spender's
effectiveness in combat.

01:45:30.781 --> 01:45:33.617
Basically, you get
into this rhythm of combat

01:45:33.751 --> 01:45:35.519
of 3
builders to a spender.

01:45:35.653 --> 01:45:37.388
It lets you build up to
these big, big moments.

01:45:38.856 --> 01:45:40.424
It almost makes a
rhythm game out of combat,

01:45:40.558 --> 01:45:41.658
which I really enjoy.

01:45:41.926 --> 01:45:44.727
The way the class
specializations work

01:45:44.862 --> 01:45:47.430
is you will work with one
of the world groups of Rogues

01:45:48.098 --> 01:45:50.568
around the world in
Sanctuary that you will find

01:45:50.968 --> 01:45:53.003
and do class-specific quests
that no other class can do.

01:45:53.370 --> 01:45:55.772
Whether it's the
Sisterhood of the Sightless Eye,

01:45:55.906 --> 01:45:58.242
the remnants of that Order,
that you choose to work with,

01:45:58.676 --> 01:46:00.577
or the
mercenaries of Kehjistan

01:46:00.711 --> 01:46:04.281
or the outlaw smugglers
of the swamps of Hawezar.

01:46:04.848 --> 01:46:07.617
Depending on what
speaks to your class fantasy,

01:46:07.751 --> 01:46:10.553
or depending on which
specialization speaks to you

01:46:10.688 --> 01:46:11.755
or you find more powerful,

01:46:12.122 --> 01:46:14.424
you'll be able to
complete that quest line

01:46:14.558 --> 01:46:16.192
and activate
that specialization,

01:46:17.528 --> 01:46:20.764
which you can mix and match
with any of the play styles.

01:46:20.898 --> 01:46:23.200
You could be a Combo
Points, ranged or melee player,

01:46:23.334 --> 01:46:25.235
but you can also be
an Exploit Weakness,

01:46:25.369 --> 01:46:27.904
range or
melee or hybrid player.

01:46:28.272 --> 01:46:30.640
It just gives the
class huge variety

01:46:30.774 --> 01:46:32.375
and builds
and play styles.

01:46:32.509 --> 01:46:34.978
I love how that ties
into the open world aspect,

01:46:35.512 --> 01:46:38.315
the ability to go to
Kehjistan or Hawazar

01:46:38.449 --> 01:46:41.051
and get these special
quests only the Rogue can get.

01:46:41.318 --> 01:46:43.320
That touches on a
thing I love that we've done,

01:46:43.454 --> 01:46:46.723
which is connecting the weapons
and equipment to regions.

01:46:47.157 --> 01:46:48.424
When you're
exploring the world

01:46:48.559 --> 01:46:50.627
and you pick up
a sword in Kehjistan,

01:46:50.761 --> 01:46:51.261
it's a scimitar.

01:46:52.830 --> 01:46:54.530
If you're up in Scosglen,
which is up in the north,

01:46:54.665 --> 01:46:56.634
it's more of a broadsword.

01:46:58.335 --> 01:47:01.771
It's a subtle thing,
but I think it really...

01:47:01.905 --> 01:47:04.441
Armors you get
in different regions

01:47:04.575 --> 01:47:05.809
look like
they're from the place.

01:47:06.343 --> 01:47:09.479
Giving that sense of
place in the world is something

01:47:09.613 --> 01:47:13.283
that every time I
play the game, I feel it,

01:47:13.417 --> 01:47:15.251
I see it in subtle ways.

01:47:15.786 --> 01:47:17.421
It really
enhances the overall sense

01:47:17.554 --> 01:47:19.189
of immersion
and experience.

01:47:19.323 --> 01:47:20.857
I want to see
what's around the corner.

01:47:20.991 --> 01:47:22.092
The weapons are also,

01:47:23.994 --> 01:47:25.128
going back all
the way to Diablo II,

01:47:25.262 --> 01:47:27.797
such an
integral part of the class,

01:47:27.931 --> 01:47:29.399
and the ability to
augment your weapon

01:47:30.034 --> 01:47:32.970
through magic, through
enhancements is something that

01:47:33.604 --> 01:47:34.805
really spoke to us

01:47:34.938 --> 01:47:36.139
when we tried to tap
into the core of the class.

01:47:37.107 --> 01:47:40.043
Going back to those
mercenaries that you could hire,

01:47:40.177 --> 01:47:41.378
it was always cool to see

01:47:41.512 --> 01:47:45.448
what kind of special
arrow shot they would come with.

01:47:46.684 --> 01:47:48.785
It's something we paid
homage to with the Imbue system,

01:47:49.553 --> 01:47:51.655
all the way from
directly elemental Imbues

01:47:51.789 --> 01:47:53.457
like the Frost Imbue,

01:47:53.590 --> 01:47:56.659
which feels a lot like

01:47:56.794 --> 01:47:59.229
the ice arrows that the
D2 mercenaries used to shoot.

01:47:59.930 --> 01:48:01.865
We expanded it
to feel a little gritter,

01:48:01.999 --> 01:48:03.400
a little
darker, and do things

01:48:03.534 --> 01:48:05.335
like Poison Imbue
and Shadow Imbue.

01:48:06.370 --> 01:48:07.303
The ability to take

01:48:08.605 --> 01:48:11.107
any sort of attack and mix
and match with those Imbues.

01:48:11.542 --> 01:48:13.243
Once that clicked for me,

01:48:13.377 --> 01:48:15.078
that I could take
any ability and Imbue it

01:48:15.212 --> 01:48:17.314
with either of
those elements,

01:48:17.448 --> 01:48:20.016
Rain of Arrows with Frost
and Rain of Arrows with acid

01:48:20.150 --> 01:48:22.852
is completely different.

01:48:22.986 --> 01:48:24.321
You're essentially
freezing the battlefield.

01:48:25.656 --> 01:48:27.357
And I just love
Poison Imbue because of...

01:48:29.059 --> 01:48:31.494
the gruesome deaths that
all the monsters experience.

01:48:32.362 --> 01:48:34.864
We've had a ton
of fun making that art.

01:48:34.998 --> 01:48:38.234
It's always fun to see the
goat men melt into nothing.

01:48:38.368 --> 01:48:41.839
You just kill everything
on the screen in a gory mess,

01:48:42.973 --> 01:48:44.474
which never
really gets old.

01:48:44.608 --> 01:48:46.509
It's a flexible
way to take a build

01:48:46.643 --> 01:48:48.211
that you're
already comfortable with,

01:48:48.345 --> 01:48:49.646
that you already like.

01:48:49.847 --> 01:48:52.448
Say you're really comfortable
with Flurry and Shadow Step

01:48:52.583 --> 01:48:53.850
as your melee skills

01:48:54.318 --> 01:48:56.286
and you find yourself
needing more crowd control,

01:48:57.187 --> 01:48:58.655
without
changing your play style,

01:48:58.789 --> 01:49:01.492
you can just add the
Frost Imbue to your bar.

01:49:01.859 --> 01:49:04.727
Now you're able to
use those same abilities

01:49:04.862 --> 01:49:07.063
to slow down enemies
or build up to a full freeze.

01:49:07.197 --> 01:49:11.067
Frost Imbue plays pretty
well with group play as well.

01:49:11.201 --> 01:49:13.603
I can freeze an enemy and
somebody else can blow it up.

01:49:13.737 --> 01:49:16.873
Or also, the way that
Chills work in the game,

01:49:17.474 --> 01:49:18.876
they build up
to a full freeze.

01:49:19.009 --> 01:49:21.411
So if your best
buddy is a Sorceress player,

01:49:22.212 --> 01:49:24.447
your Chill spells
and your Frost Imbues

01:49:24.581 --> 01:49:26.850
will actually
build up faster

01:49:26.984 --> 01:49:27.617
than if you were

01:49:27.751 --> 01:49:29.152
using a single player.

01:49:29.286 --> 01:49:32.823
Ask to open with
Blizzard before you Frost Imbue

01:49:32.956 --> 01:49:35.458
and you'll be
freezing enemies twice as fast,

01:49:35.592 --> 01:49:36.426
which is awesome synergy.

01:49:36.560 --> 01:49:39.128
The last thing

01:49:39.263 --> 01:49:42.065
that's worth spending some time
talking about with the Rogue is

01:49:42.199 --> 01:49:44.134
how mobile the class is,

01:49:44.268 --> 01:49:48.405
how able the
class is to close the gap

01:49:48.539 --> 01:49:49.339
between
themselves and the enemy,

01:49:49.773 --> 01:49:51.708
even while mounted.

01:49:51.842 --> 01:49:53.943
You might find
yourself a full screen away

01:49:54.077 --> 01:49:56.213
and dismounting
into a Rain of Arrows,

01:49:56.346 --> 01:49:59.816
closing the rest of
that gap with a Dash,

01:49:59.950 --> 01:50:02.452
and then getting back out of
there, dropping some caltrops.

01:50:02.586 --> 01:50:03.953
They can basically

01:50:04.087 --> 01:50:06.689
control the fluid
motion of combat

01:50:06.824 --> 01:50:08.625
in a way that
no other class can.

01:50:09.693 --> 01:50:11.194
That's
something that, beyond all

01:50:11.328 --> 01:50:13.463
of the other customization
options that you have,

01:50:13.597 --> 01:50:15.765
whether it's
the play style,

01:50:15.899 --> 01:50:18.201
whether it's the range at
which you engage with enemies,

01:50:18.468 --> 01:50:19.402
whether it's
the cosmetics,

01:50:19.603 --> 01:50:22.172
the sheer
mobility of the class

01:50:22.606 --> 01:50:25.007
lets you adapt
to the situation.

01:50:25.142 --> 01:50:29.012
Even when you would have totally
been toast as another class,

01:50:29.146 --> 01:50:31.214
a room full of elites,

01:50:31.682 --> 01:50:35.185
you're able to use offensive
abilities in creative ways.

01:50:35.419 --> 01:50:37.420
Abilities like
Shadow Step,

01:50:37.554 --> 01:50:39.589
abilities like
Shadow Realm,

01:50:39.723 --> 01:50:41.191
abilities like Dash,

01:50:41.325 --> 01:50:43.826
you're able to use
them to your advantage

01:50:43.961 --> 01:50:46.930
in a way that gets you out
of a very dangerous situation

01:50:47.064 --> 01:50:50.000
by picking that
one, lone skeleton archer

01:50:50.133 --> 01:50:52.169
that's not
surrounded by everything else.

01:50:52.302 --> 01:50:54.271
You can Shadow Step
there, get out of the way,

01:50:54.872 --> 01:50:56.106
and live to
fight another day,

01:50:56.240 --> 01:50:58.474
which feels like
such a Rogue thing to do.

01:50:58.609 --> 01:50:59.643
JOHN:
When I play the Rogue,

01:50:59.776 --> 01:51:01.244
I really do feel
surgical precision.

01:51:01.378 --> 01:51:05.315
I really feel like I can
move through the whole screen.

01:51:05.816 --> 01:51:08.051
It feels
kind of effortless

01:51:08.185 --> 01:51:09.920
and almost
sometimes a little unfair.

01:51:10.821 --> 01:51:12.322
When I go into the
Shadow Realm and I'm dashing,

01:51:12.456 --> 01:51:15.091
I'm like, "Am I
cheating? Did I break the game?"

01:51:15.225 --> 01:51:16.760
We'll hear more
about PvP in a little bit,

01:51:16.894 --> 01:51:19.596
but I couldn't go
without mentioning

01:51:19.730 --> 01:51:23.099
that some of our most
feared PvP players on the team

01:51:23.233 --> 01:51:24.434
play Rogue.

01:51:24.568 --> 01:51:26.636
It's just the way
they're able to surprise you.

01:51:26.770 --> 01:51:28.271
You might be
with a group of friends

01:51:28.739 --> 01:51:31.307
and that mobility lets them
go to the middle of the group,

01:51:31.441 --> 01:51:33.943
unleash a Rain of
Arrows that's Frost Imbued

01:51:34.077 --> 01:51:35.812
and before you
know it, you're all frozen.

01:51:35.946 --> 01:51:39.416
Looking at a Rogue while
you're frozen, let me tell you,

01:51:39.549 --> 01:51:42.286
there are fewer scarier things
in our game right now than that.

01:51:42.920 --> 01:51:46.189
So this was the
ultimate blog post.

01:51:46.323 --> 01:51:47.457
This has
been a lot of fun,

01:51:47.591 --> 01:51:49.092
getting to share a new
addition to the campfire.

01:51:50.127 --> 01:51:52.762
There probably will
be more in the future.

01:51:52.896 --> 01:51:54.230
We always say
there's a little bit more room.

01:51:54.364 --> 01:51:57.267
That's right. That pretty
much wraps it up for the Rogue.

01:51:57.401 --> 01:51:59.969
So, why don't we
go to Joe and Careena

01:52:00.103 --> 01:52:01.772
and learn more about those
open world features we promised,

01:52:03.340 --> 01:52:04.740
Thank you to Luis and John

01:52:04.875 --> 01:52:06.209
for introducing the Rogue,

01:52:06.343 --> 01:52:08.178
which I'm really excited

01:52:08.312 --> 01:52:10.380
we finally got a chance
to talk about and announce.

01:52:10.614 --> 01:52:11.848
We're here
today to talk to you

01:52:12.182 --> 01:52:13.950
about Diablo IV
and the open world.

01:52:15.052 --> 01:52:17.820
I think the best way to start
this conversation, Careena,

01:52:17.955 --> 01:52:21.390
would be for me to ask you
what are you most excited about,

01:52:21.858 --> 01:52:24.360
of what we're adding in
Diablo IV in the open world?

01:52:24.494 --> 01:52:25.696
What feature,
what mechanic,

01:52:25.829 --> 01:52:27.664
what are you most
excited to talk about?

01:52:28.498 --> 01:52:30.200
I am really excited about

01:52:31.134 --> 01:52:33.402
exploring the world.

01:52:33.537 --> 01:52:34.871
I am a huge
completist naturally,

01:52:35.005 --> 01:52:36.005
that's the way I play.

01:52:36.139 --> 01:52:38.241
I love
exploring and looking out

01:52:38.375 --> 01:52:40.176
into every
single nook and cranny.

01:52:40.310 --> 01:52:42.178
The more this
game has been growing,

01:52:42.312 --> 01:52:43.613
particularly
since last BlizzCon,

01:52:44.781 --> 01:52:46.016
the more fun I'm having

01:52:46.149 --> 01:52:49.586
riding around the
open world on my mount

01:52:49.720 --> 01:52:51.020
and exploring
everything I possibly can.

01:52:51.154 --> 01:52:52.756
It's really fun, I think.

01:52:53.924 --> 01:52:55.258
Now that
we've taken Sanctuary

01:52:55.392 --> 01:52:58.428
and made it so much larger when
you're playing through the game,

01:52:59.096 --> 01:53:01.865
when you compare it
to Diablo III or Diablo II.

01:53:01.999 --> 01:53:06.302
There's tons of exploration and
movement throughout the world

01:53:06.436 --> 01:53:07.703
in those games, obviously.

01:53:08.238 --> 01:53:09.205
But now we've
really been able to embrace

01:53:09.339 --> 01:53:10.506
the open world concept,

01:53:10.640 --> 01:53:13.176
we can dive much deeper

01:53:13.310 --> 01:53:16.178
into how Sanctuary
actually all fits together,

01:53:16.446 --> 01:53:17.881
be able to ride

01:53:18.015 --> 01:53:19.750
from Scosglen to
the Fractured Peaks.

01:53:20.017 --> 01:53:22.752
It's very
interesting that way.

01:53:22.886 --> 01:53:24.287
One of the things about
that I think is really neat

01:53:24.421 --> 01:53:27.557
is the collection
aspect of mounts.

01:53:28.125 --> 01:53:30.326
So, there's all
these horse breeds

01:53:30.460 --> 01:53:33.163
and other mounts you
can find throughout the world

01:53:33.296 --> 01:53:36.800
from completing puzzles
or finding hidden locations

01:53:36.933 --> 01:53:39.035
or slaying
particular kinds of monsters.

01:53:39.336 --> 01:53:41.938
Find various horses or things
to customize your horse with,

01:53:42.072 --> 01:53:43.806
like horse armor,

01:53:43.940 --> 01:53:46.276
there are hoof
plates you can get

01:53:46.410 --> 01:53:48.345
and trophies to show
off your accomplishments.

01:53:48.578 --> 01:53:51.080
There's lots of ways you
can personalize your mount

01:53:51.214 --> 01:53:52.782
and make it feel
like your companion.

01:53:52.916 --> 01:53:54.217
It's really cool,
like you mentioned,

01:53:54.351 --> 01:53:56.786
with the horse shoes,
you get to change the speed

01:53:56.920 --> 01:53:57.987
at which your
horse can run.

01:53:58.121 --> 01:54:01.057
I like to be able to
customize the horse

01:54:01.191 --> 01:54:03.827
to the type of gameplay that
I like to enjoy as a player.

01:54:03.960 --> 01:54:05.127
It's really fun.

01:54:05.262 --> 01:54:07.264
I like the amount of
variety that you give us.

01:54:07.631 --> 01:54:10.800
There's more to
mounts than that as well.

01:54:10.967 --> 01:54:14.104
One thing we knew we didn't want
to do with mounts in Diablo IV

01:54:14.237 --> 01:54:15.638
was we didn't
want to have jousting.

01:54:15.772 --> 01:54:18.241
This idea of
fighting from horseback.

01:54:18.375 --> 01:54:19.810
It's not really the
Diablo experience.

01:54:19.943 --> 01:54:22.845
Yeah, that's
something I'm super excited for.

01:54:23.847 --> 01:54:26.116
At last BlizzCon,
we had the Sorceress.

01:54:26.249 --> 01:54:27.717
We showed her
dismount skill, Ice Dash,

01:54:27.851 --> 01:54:30.153
where she shoots
forwards off her mount

01:54:30.287 --> 01:54:32.355
and destroys
everything in her path.

01:54:32.489 --> 01:54:34.357
With the Rogue, we got
to have a little bit more fun.

01:54:34.991 --> 01:54:36.860
The animation department
has done a fantastic job

01:54:36.993 --> 01:54:37.660
of having her leap up

01:54:37.794 --> 01:54:38.995
into the air

01:54:39.229 --> 01:54:42.832
and fire down the Rain of
Arrows that destroys everything.

01:54:43.633 --> 01:54:46.469
It's really cool.
It feels really good.

01:54:46.603 --> 01:54:48.271
JOE: Leaping
from your saddle

01:54:48.405 --> 01:54:51.374
and raining arrows down
on your enemies as you pass by,

01:54:51.975 --> 01:54:54.177
then get in the fray
and starting to stab people

01:54:54.311 --> 01:54:55.378
and poison people,

01:54:55.846 --> 01:54:56.947
that is

01:54:57.080 --> 01:54:58.948
spot on what
we're looking for.

01:54:59.082 --> 01:55:01.851
Being able to jump in, engage
with enemies, from your horse,

01:55:01.985 --> 01:55:03.920
and then duke it
out using your skills

01:55:04.054 --> 01:55:04.988
and everything
else like we want.

01:55:05.122 --> 01:55:06.456
It's interesting that

01:55:07.157 --> 01:55:08.691
your mount
can't get damaged,

01:55:08.825 --> 01:55:11.227
but if a monster attacks
you while you're mounted,

01:55:11.361 --> 01:55:12.462
you will be
forced to dismount.

01:55:12.596 --> 01:55:15.998
So, there is a
flipside of judging for yourself

01:55:16.133 --> 01:55:18.334
when you do
want to use that skill

01:55:18.468 --> 01:55:20.370
to prevent you from being
forced off unintentionally.

01:55:21.404 --> 01:55:23.305
There's definitely a
bit of gameplay there

01:55:23.440 --> 01:55:24.741
to make sure you use it

01:55:24.875 --> 01:55:27.043
in a way that's appropriate to
help you get where you're going

01:55:27.177 --> 01:55:28.411
and fight the
monsters you want to.

01:55:28.745 --> 01:55:31.347
One of the things I'm most
excited about for the open world

01:55:31.481 --> 01:55:33.149
is this feature we
have called camps.

01:55:33.850 --> 01:55:37.286
Camps are basically
these strongholds of evil

01:55:37.454 --> 01:55:38.254
out in the overworld.

01:55:38.989 --> 01:55:42.425
You've got to
remember that, decades ago,

01:55:42.559 --> 01:55:43.793
during the
events of Reaper of Souls

01:55:43.927 --> 01:55:45.261
when
Malthael rose to power,

01:55:45.395 --> 01:55:47.898
the Reapers
killed 9 out of 10 people

01:55:48.798 --> 01:55:49.999
throughout Sanctuary.

01:55:50.133 --> 01:55:51.200
A tremendous
number of people passed away.

01:55:52.435 --> 01:55:55.338
Now there's a big power
vacuum out here decades later,

01:55:55.539 --> 01:55:59.075
and these monsters have started
to move into these places.

01:55:59.209 --> 01:56:01.845
Bandits, monsters, goat men, all
kinds of different creatures,

01:56:01.978 --> 01:56:03.146
skeletons,
the undead, have started

01:56:04.047 --> 01:56:05.882
to resurface and
come back into these areas

01:56:06.016 --> 01:56:08.051
and take
them for themselves.

01:56:08.618 --> 01:56:09.619
You, as the
hero of Sanctuary,

01:56:09.753 --> 01:56:11.220
get to wander
through these spaces

01:56:11.354 --> 01:56:13.856
and begin to reclaim them
for the people of Sanctuary.

01:56:14.457 --> 01:56:16.326
That's part of what
makes them really neat.

01:56:16.893 --> 01:56:19.028
Each of them
have their own story.

01:56:19.162 --> 01:56:20.863
They're their own unique,
fixed place in the world.

01:56:20.997 --> 01:56:23.332
Unlike quests, quests are
something that happen randomly

01:56:23.466 --> 01:56:25.101
and you can come
across them anywhere,

01:56:25.268 --> 01:56:27.170
camps are a
permanent fixture

01:56:27.304 --> 01:56:30.306
that you, as the player,
when you come across that evil,

01:56:30.574 --> 01:56:32.241
can actually
have a real impact

01:56:32.709 --> 01:56:35.144
on the open world
that everyone else can see.

01:56:35.278 --> 01:56:37.013
Because, once you
have defeated that evil,

01:56:38.215 --> 01:56:39.482
one of the
really fun things is

01:56:39.616 --> 01:56:43.219
that you
basically unlock that camp

01:56:43.353 --> 01:56:46.289
and you make a new waypoint that
previously didn't exist for you.

01:56:46.423 --> 01:56:48.158
Suddenly all the
marketplaces open up

01:56:48.291 --> 01:56:50.159
and now you
have a blacksmith

01:56:50.293 --> 01:56:52.195
in a location that
previously didn't exist,

01:56:52.329 --> 01:56:53.063
which I think is cool.

01:56:53.396 --> 01:56:54.063
JOE: What
we're looking at here,

01:56:55.165 --> 01:56:57.733
this was a village,
the village of Qara-Yisu.

01:56:57.968 --> 01:56:59.635
Now, it's fallen to ruins.

01:57:00.270 --> 01:57:03.006
The people here in the
dry steppes had a problem.

01:57:03.139 --> 01:57:04.206
The problem was

01:57:04.708 --> 01:57:07.410
there were bloodthirsty
cannibals that dwelled nearby

01:57:07.544 --> 01:57:09.445
and would
regularly raid their community.

01:57:10.247 --> 01:57:11.448
The people had
to find a solution.

01:57:11.581 --> 01:57:14.050
They weren't strong
enough to defend themselves.

01:57:14.284 --> 01:57:16.652
So, they made a
dark bargain with a demon

01:57:17.287 --> 01:57:19.856
and they were able
to gather enough power

01:57:19.990 --> 01:57:22.559
to push these cannibals
out and protect their community.

01:57:22.993 --> 01:57:24.560
But not without great
cost, as you can see here.

01:57:24.694 --> 01:57:27.062
You're helping re-settle

01:57:27.197 --> 01:57:29.165
parts of Sanctuary when you take
these camps back from the enemy.

01:57:29.633 --> 01:57:31.568
Vendors will come back

01:57:31.701 --> 01:57:33.269
and now there
might be new quest-givers,

01:57:33.403 --> 01:57:34.670
new stories
that need to be told.

01:57:34.871 --> 01:57:37.306
Dungeon entrances can appear in
some of these places afterwards.

01:57:37.440 --> 01:57:38.908
There's all kinds
of things that occur

01:57:39.042 --> 01:57:40.409
after you take
over the Camp

01:57:40.543 --> 01:57:42.545
and liberate it
from forces of darkness.

01:57:43.446 --> 01:57:46.982
Again, it's so cool to see
you permanently change the world

01:57:47.117 --> 01:57:48.318
as you find these places,

01:57:48.451 --> 01:57:50.186
wander through and
solve these problems.

01:57:50.687 --> 01:57:52.521
One of the other
neat things about camps,

01:57:52.689 --> 01:57:55.391
and in general the
overworld broadly speaking,

01:57:55.659 --> 01:57:56.893
is we have many
more opportunities now

01:57:58.194 --> 01:57:59.996
to play with movement.

01:58:00.130 --> 01:58:02.264
CAREENA: It
pushes the bar vertically

01:58:02.399 --> 01:58:03.199
as well as horizontally.

01:58:03.600 --> 01:58:05.201
We now have been
playing around with the idea

01:58:05.335 --> 01:58:06.936
of jumping across chasms

01:58:07.404 --> 01:58:09.906
to allow the player to
get access to a location

01:58:10.040 --> 01:58:11.374
they
previously didn't have.

01:58:11.508 --> 01:58:14.444
JOE: There's all kinds of areas
players can find their own path,

01:58:14.577 --> 01:58:15.678
allowing them
extra movement options

01:58:15.812 --> 01:58:18.280
they haven't had
in previous games.

01:58:18.415 --> 01:58:19.349
CAREENA: We
are also in a vertical

01:58:19.482 --> 01:58:21.651
top-down camera
for the environment,

01:58:21.785 --> 01:58:25.121
so it really breaks up the
plane that you typically see

01:58:25.255 --> 01:58:27.824
not only as a developer or an
animator, but also as a player.

01:58:27.957 --> 01:58:28.857
There's such
a huge variety

01:58:29.559 --> 01:58:31.427
of things that
you can possibly do

01:58:31.561 --> 01:58:34.063
to mix it up and make
things a little bit different.

01:58:34.197 --> 01:58:35.932
There's certain
puzzle elements to it.

01:58:36.800 --> 01:58:38.902
It's not straightforward
or always obvious.

01:58:39.035 --> 01:58:40.870
It's not just a matter

01:58:41.004 --> 01:58:43.172
of running through the
role and getting to your goal.

01:58:43.306 --> 01:58:44.440
You have to think.

01:58:44.908 --> 01:58:44.974
Definitely.

01:58:45.875 --> 01:58:47.010
Now, Careena,

01:58:47.911 --> 01:58:50.913
I know that you're
very excited about PvP.

01:58:51.047 --> 01:58:52.048
(LAUGHS)

01:58:52.182 --> 01:58:53.149
I'm a huge fan of PvP.

01:58:53.283 --> 01:58:56.085
I love the first
time we had a chance

01:58:56.219 --> 01:58:58.354
as a whole
team to play PvP.

01:58:58.488 --> 01:59:00.756
The amount of
feedback generated,

01:59:00.890 --> 01:59:02.926
I think it took Design
3 days to go through it all.

01:59:03.159 --> 01:59:05.094
What that showed me is

01:59:05.595 --> 01:59:07.931
that everyone
either loves it or hates it.

01:59:08.064 --> 01:59:10.433
I personally
love it. I really enjoy it.

01:59:10.567 --> 01:59:12.635
The way that
PvP works is that

01:59:13.036 --> 01:59:16.506
the Hatred of Mephisto
has been bubbling up from Hell

01:59:16.639 --> 01:59:19.008
and has created
these areas in the open world

01:59:19.142 --> 01:59:20.443
that you can
freely walk into

01:59:20.910 --> 01:59:22.278
that

01:59:23.113 --> 01:59:26.048
consumes you and makes you turn
against your fellow players.

01:59:26.282 --> 01:59:29.419
JOE: PvP, even in these
areas, these Fields of Hatred,

01:59:30.754 --> 01:59:33.022
it's not mandatory.

01:59:33.156 --> 01:59:35.992
These are optional
places people can choose to go

01:59:36.359 --> 01:59:38.461
to collect
Shards of Hatred

01:59:38.595 --> 01:59:40.230
that they can
collect from killing monsters,

01:59:40.764 --> 01:59:42.999
from completing
events, opening chests,

01:59:43.133 --> 01:59:44.067
all kinds of ways
to earn these things,

01:59:44.200 --> 01:59:45.601
not
necessarily killing players.

01:59:45.835 --> 01:59:47.937
As you earn
these Shards of Hatred,

01:59:48.071 --> 01:59:50.005
they're in an
un-purified state.

01:59:50.140 --> 01:59:51.641
They're in a basic state.

01:59:52.108 --> 01:59:54.944
You need to bring
them to a Purification Event

01:59:55.078 --> 01:59:58.648
in order to turn them into
a currency that you can use

01:59:58.848 --> 02:00:01.651
in the nearby, small encampments
around the Fields of Hatred.

02:00:01.785 --> 02:00:03.052
What's neat about that

02:00:03.186 --> 02:00:05.488
is when you're
trying to purify these things,

02:00:05.622 --> 02:00:07.856
that's when
everybody nearby finds out

02:00:07.991 --> 02:00:10.159
and hears, "Someone's trying to
complete a Purification Ritual,

02:00:10.293 --> 02:00:12.628
we need to go
and kill those players

02:00:12.762 --> 02:00:14.363
and take their
shards before they finish."

02:00:15.665 --> 02:00:18.768
Once you purify them, they're
yours, you can't lose them.

02:00:19.002 --> 02:00:20.670
But before they're
purified, they're free game.

02:00:21.438 --> 02:00:23.706
It's really
important to note that,

02:00:23.840 --> 02:00:26.576
even if you don't want to
engage with othe PvP players,

02:00:26.709 --> 02:00:28.544
you can actually
explore those areas

02:00:28.678 --> 02:00:31.513
and collect those Shards of
Hatred from specific monsters,

02:00:32.115 --> 02:00:33.950
but the second
you need to turn them in

02:00:34.083 --> 02:00:35.384
and purify
them into a currency

02:00:35.518 --> 02:00:37.453
where you can
spend them on gear,

02:00:37.587 --> 02:00:38.087
trophies and
other content,

02:00:39.489 --> 02:00:41.624
you are hostile yourself

02:00:41.758 --> 02:00:43.993
and can be attacked
during that single moment.

02:00:44.127 --> 02:00:46.429
But, I think that
creates a lot of opportunity

02:00:46.563 --> 02:00:49.632
for you to team up with
friends for just that one moment

02:00:49.766 --> 02:00:52.201
in order to gain the reward you
spent so much time collecting.

02:00:52.502 --> 02:00:55.237
You called out earlier that
the Shards of Hatred are used

02:00:55.371 --> 02:00:57.706
for some of the vendors you'd
find in the Fields of Hatred,

02:00:58.441 --> 02:01:02.545
there are some special vendors
that only arrive in these areas

02:01:02.679 --> 02:01:05.181
who'll have special
costumes or new mounts

02:01:05.315 --> 02:01:08.284
and weapons and
things along those lines.

02:01:08.418 --> 02:01:09.485
Nothing
strictly more powerful

02:01:09.619 --> 02:01:11.654
than stuff you
get in other parts,

02:01:11.788 --> 02:01:13.923
but stuff that speaks to
your desire as a PvP player.

02:01:14.557 --> 02:01:16.025
I think one of
the things that's neat

02:01:16.159 --> 02:01:18.094
about the
system in general is

02:01:18.228 --> 02:01:20.196
that as a player,
if you choose to go hostile,

02:01:20.330 --> 02:01:22.999
betray the
players who are near you,

02:01:23.132 --> 02:01:25.168
try to get as many
Shards of Hatred as you can,

02:01:25.301 --> 02:01:26.535
you're going
to slowly begin

02:01:26.669 --> 02:01:30.773
to infuse yourself with
that same Curse of Mephisto

02:01:31.107 --> 02:01:33.175
and you're going to
become a Vessel of Hatred.

02:01:33.309 --> 02:01:35.178
Once you do that,

02:01:35.812 --> 02:01:38.548
everyone within a
very large radius of you

02:01:38.681 --> 02:01:40.283
is going to be able to
see you as a threat on the map

02:01:41.551 --> 02:01:42.117
and they'll get a
bonus for killing you.

02:01:42.719 --> 02:01:44.420
So when that occurs,

02:01:44.554 --> 02:01:46.955
you have a set amount
of time you need to survive.

02:01:47.090 --> 02:01:47.690
If you're able to do that

02:01:47.824 --> 02:01:49.559
without running away or

02:01:49.692 --> 02:01:51.727
jumping into a town
portal trying to escape,

02:01:51.861 --> 02:01:54.497
if you can do that and finish
that period and manage to escape

02:01:54.631 --> 02:01:56.999
or defeat people who are
coming to try to destroy you,

02:01:57.367 --> 02:01:59.135
you'll get a really
big bonus at the end.

02:01:59.435 --> 02:02:02.171
That's been really exciting to
watch in our play tests already.

02:02:02.305 --> 02:02:04.340
People are
wrestling for this status

02:02:04.474 --> 02:02:06.308
and trying to defend
it once they achieve it

02:02:06.543 --> 02:02:10.646
There's also the ears that
you get to collect as a trophy.

02:02:10.780 --> 02:02:12.247
It harkens back to D2,
where I think you could...

02:02:12.582 --> 02:02:15.084
sell the ears for 1 gold.

02:02:15.218 --> 02:02:16.852
I don't know
if we'll do that.

02:02:16.986 --> 02:02:18.621
I know we played with
them as a currency at one point,

02:02:18.755 --> 02:02:20.522
but I think now
it's just a cool trophy

02:02:20.657 --> 02:02:21.590
that you get to
collect and show off.

02:02:22.025 --> 02:02:24.027
Yeah, right now,
a very cool trophy.

02:02:24.160 --> 02:02:26.162
The thing that was
neat about the D2 ears was,

02:02:26.296 --> 02:02:28.664
you could fill up your
whole inventory with them,

02:02:28.798 --> 02:02:31.467
being able to see the people
that you took the ears from,

02:02:31.601 --> 02:02:33.369
seeing their
character names on there.

02:02:33.503 --> 02:02:35.805
It was always the
interesting, fun part

02:02:35.939 --> 02:02:38.407
and proof that "Yeah,
I got that person that time."

02:02:38.675 --> 02:02:40.576
We want to make sure
we embraced that aspect of it.

02:02:40.710 --> 02:02:41.911
That's the important part.

02:02:42.045 --> 02:02:44.013
I think that's going
to be a great call back

02:02:44.147 --> 02:02:45.114
to Diablo II PvP.

02:02:45.248 --> 02:02:48.284
Yeah. I love
where PvP is going

02:02:48.418 --> 02:02:51.353
and I love that
it is only one part

02:02:51.487 --> 02:02:54.357
of how large
and open the world is,

02:02:54.924 --> 02:02:56.892
and really harkens
back to the adventurer in me

02:02:57.026 --> 02:02:58.561
when I play the
game. How do you feel?

02:02:59.462 --> 02:03:00.663
I'm super excited.

02:03:00.797 --> 02:03:02.898
There's so much to
look forward to in Diablo IV.

02:03:03.032 --> 02:03:05.134
What we talked about today
just scratches the surface.

02:03:05.335 --> 02:03:07.002
In general,
as a Diablo fan,

02:03:07.136 --> 02:03:07.970
there's a lot to
be looking forward to

02:03:08.104 --> 02:03:09.538
in the
franchise in the future.

02:03:10.139 --> 02:03:11.774
Thank you so
much for watching today.

02:03:11.908 --> 02:03:13.409
We look forward to
speaking about this game

02:03:13.543 --> 02:03:14.677
and others in future.

02:03:48.911 --> 02:03:50.880
# EPIC ORCHESTRAL MUSIC

02:03:51.514 --> 02:03:53.115
Welcome,
everybody, to BlizzCon.

02:03:53.249 --> 02:03:56.819
We know that you
all want to hear more

02:03:56.953 --> 02:03:58.688
about Overwatch 2.

02:03:59.922 --> 02:04:03.459
Allow us to invite
you behind the scenes

02:04:03.593 --> 02:04:05.260
to explain more
of what we're doing

02:04:05.395 --> 02:04:06.496
and why.

02:04:06.629 --> 02:04:09.098
Everybody, please hit
record. Fingers crossed.

02:04:09.232 --> 02:04:10.365
We lost Scott.

02:04:10.500 --> 02:04:12.267
It's off to a good start.

02:04:12.402 --> 02:04:12.502
This is going great.

02:04:15.338 --> 02:04:17.073
JEFF K: We've
been assembling

02:04:17.206 --> 02:04:18.608
one of the best teams
that I have ever worked with.

02:04:18.741 --> 02:04:21.343
I'm nervous. (LAUGHS)

02:04:21.477 --> 02:04:25.581
And I'm so proud of the progress
that we have made together.

02:04:27.316 --> 02:04:29.485
I really liked the
stylized drop down.

02:04:29.619 --> 02:04:31.286
Imagine that
it's coming forward.

02:04:31.421 --> 02:04:32.821
Bom-bom.
It starts to spin.

02:04:32.955 --> 02:04:35.157
JULIA: The scale
and the scope of this game

02:04:35.291 --> 02:04:37.893
has meant that we've
needed to grow substantially.

02:04:38.027 --> 02:04:39.328
STAMMERS

02:04:40.096 --> 02:04:42.799
(LAUGHS)
Sorry. Let me start over.

02:04:42.932 --> 02:04:45.434
Overall, I think we've
done incredibly well as a team,

02:04:45.568 --> 02:04:45.935
moving to work from home.

02:04:46.869 --> 02:04:49.338
BOTH LAUGH

02:04:49.472 --> 02:04:50.506
Oh, shadow play.

02:04:51.874 --> 02:04:53.910
AARON: There have
definitely been challenges,

02:04:54.043 --> 02:04:55.644
but the team
is really putting

02:04:55.778 --> 02:04:57.046
everything we've
got behind this game.

02:04:58.247 --> 02:05:01.917
To me, it's the next big
step in the Overwatch universe.

02:05:02.051 --> 02:05:05.788
New heroes, game
modes, major story beats.

02:05:05.922 --> 02:05:07.590
DYLAN: With
Competitive and PvE,

02:05:07.724 --> 02:05:10.359
I see Overwatch 2 as the
Overwatch for all of my friends.

02:05:11.060 --> 02:05:12.194
We're always inspired

02:05:12.328 --> 02:05:13.729
by what Story and
Franchise Development does

02:05:13.863 --> 02:05:15.898
with their
animated shorts.

02:05:17.233 --> 02:05:19.134
We really wanted to
bring that cinematic experience

02:05:19.268 --> 02:05:20.670
into the game.

02:05:20.803 --> 02:05:23.806
You can invest in your
heroes and grow them over time.

02:05:23.940 --> 02:05:26.208
I am super
geeked-out about Overwatch 2.

02:05:26.342 --> 02:05:27.609
I can't wait for
people to play this game.

02:05:27.744 --> 02:05:28.978
It gets better and better.

02:05:29.912 --> 02:05:30.646
JEFF K: There are some
things that we just can't do

02:05:30.780 --> 02:05:31.914
without a sequel.

02:05:36.152 --> 02:05:40.022
So, thank you so
much for your patience so far,

02:05:40.156 --> 02:05:43.025
and I hope you enjoy
this behind-the-scenes look

02:05:43.159 --> 02:05:44.393
at Overwatch 2.

02:05:49.499 --> 02:05:53.569
All of our players at home, they
want to see one of the new maps

02:05:53.703 --> 02:05:55.971
that we've never
talked about before.

02:05:56.105 --> 02:05:57.206
Really? I can just, like...

02:05:58.741 --> 02:06:00.810
I can just talk about
anything that I want to?

02:06:01.878 --> 02:06:02.812
Yes.

02:06:02.945 --> 02:06:04.646
You're serving
this up to the guy

02:06:04.781 --> 02:06:08.450
that has unintentionally leaked
information in past interviews?

02:06:08.584 --> 02:06:10.185
LAUGHTER

02:06:10.319 --> 02:06:11.787
You can do
it officially now.

02:06:11.921 --> 02:06:13.255
OK.

02:06:13.389 --> 02:06:18.994
So, one of the maps that I'm
most excited for in Overwatch 2

02:06:19.128 --> 02:06:20.029
is our Rome map.

02:06:21.464 --> 02:06:24.333
#
TRADITIONAL ITALIAN MUSIC

02:06:24.467 --> 02:06:25.902
We always
want Overwatch to feel

02:06:26.035 --> 02:06:28.670
like this globetrotting
adventure for our players,

02:06:28.805 --> 02:06:29.939
so we're
having a lot of fun

02:06:30.072 --> 02:06:31.673
coming up with the
Overwatch version of Rome.

02:06:31.808 --> 02:06:33.809
We wanted a very romantic,

02:06:33.943 --> 02:06:36.913
sort of this powerful feeling
of old-world architecture.

02:06:38.247 --> 02:06:40.149
One of the most
exciting things for me

02:06:40.283 --> 02:06:42.685
is the early
building of these maps,

02:06:42.819 --> 02:06:44.687
where we get
to sit down together

02:06:44.821 --> 02:06:46.822
with key people from
the Environment team,

02:06:46.956 --> 02:06:49.324
level
designers, effects groups.

02:06:49.458 --> 02:06:51.894
We'll spend time talking
about moments in the game

02:06:52.028 --> 02:06:53.228
that we
really want to see,

02:06:53.362 --> 02:06:54.629
like the Colosseum,

02:06:54.764 --> 02:06:57.032
or a grand view of the
hills of Rome in the backdrop.

02:06:57.166 --> 02:07:00.102
And then we'll go
back and do a paint-over,

02:07:00.236 --> 02:07:02.804
or we'll do a concept painting
of certain things based on that.

02:07:02.939 --> 02:07:04.773
We try to draw inspiration

02:07:04.907 --> 02:07:06.442
from as many
different sources as possible.

02:07:06.576 --> 02:07:08.310
For example,

02:07:08.444 --> 02:07:12.447
one of our Environment artists
had just taken a trip to Rome,

02:07:12.582 --> 02:07:15.450
and he returned
with thousands of pictures

02:07:15.585 --> 02:07:19.388
and was so excited to
work on a map set in Italy.

02:07:20.122 --> 02:07:22.491
There's a lot of
ancient architecture in Rome

02:07:22.625 --> 02:07:25.828
that represents the
empire that it used to be,

02:07:25.962 --> 02:07:27.296
so we brought
some of that back.

02:07:27.430 --> 02:07:29.798
Some of the things
that are destroyed in real life

02:07:29.932 --> 02:07:30.799
we kind of rebuilt

02:07:30.933 --> 02:07:33.602
in kind of our
Overwatch style.

02:07:33.736 --> 02:07:35.437
AARON: It's one of
the most beautiful pieces

02:07:35.571 --> 02:07:36.705
of environment art

02:07:36.839 --> 02:07:39.141
that I think we've
made for the entire game,

02:07:39.275 --> 02:07:41.177
and it just
absolutely took my breath away.

02:07:46.549 --> 02:07:47.949
Can we reveal more maps?

02:07:48.084 --> 02:07:49.584
Yes. Scott...

02:07:49.719 --> 02:07:51.120
LAUGHTER

02:07:51.254 --> 02:07:54.290
Scott, why don't you have the
honor? Why don't you reveal...

02:07:54.423 --> 02:07:56.492
Is there an artified...
Pick an artified one.

02:07:56.626 --> 02:07:59.695
I know a map I
want to talk about.

02:07:59.829 --> 02:08:00.997
It's the one that I
get super excited about.

02:08:01.697 --> 02:08:03.132
New York City.

02:08:04.100 --> 02:08:07.136
New York City is just an
amazing Overwatch location.

02:08:07.904 --> 02:08:09.705
# SYNTH MUSIC

02:08:09.839 --> 02:08:13.275
We're really striving to make
it as authentic as possible,

02:08:13.409 --> 02:08:14.977
while still putting
this Overwatch spin on it.

02:08:15.111 --> 02:08:17.479
DION: There's a
lot of amazing buildings

02:08:17.613 --> 02:08:18.814
and
architecture pieces that just,

02:08:18.948 --> 02:08:20.850
especially for an
artist, stand out a lot,

02:08:20.983 --> 02:08:22.551
and they use this
kind of art deco style

02:08:22.685 --> 02:08:25.554
from the 1920s, 1950s.

02:08:26.689 --> 02:08:29.124
We started in an area that's
a little bit like the Village.

02:08:29.258 --> 02:08:31.060
There's some
smaller shops there,

02:08:31.193 --> 02:08:34.229
there's a fire
station, little pizza places

02:08:34.363 --> 02:08:37.266
and things that people that
are familiar with New York City

02:08:37.400 --> 02:08:39.869
will either recognize

02:08:40.002 --> 02:08:42.437
or maybe see the reference
that we're trying to make

02:08:42.571 --> 02:08:44.040
with some of our
different locations.

02:08:44.173 --> 02:08:46.141
It feels like
something you haven't seen

02:08:46.275 --> 02:08:47.609
in other games before

02:08:47.743 --> 02:08:49.845
because it's
uniquely Overwatch.

02:08:50.546 --> 02:08:52.547
SIREN WAILING IN DISTANCE

02:08:53.115 --> 02:08:54.883
JEFF K: Well,
we should move on,

02:08:55.017 --> 02:08:57.253
otherwise we just have
to reveal more maps, so...

02:08:58.120 --> 02:09:00.289
We were talking about PvP.

02:09:00.423 --> 02:09:04.893
And, you know, one thing that
I think is really interesting

02:09:05.027 --> 02:09:07.996
about PvP in Overwatch 2

02:09:08.130 --> 02:09:09.899
is some of the
philosophical changes

02:09:10.032 --> 02:09:13.034
we're making
to the approach.

02:09:13.169 --> 02:09:14.903
PvP feels
different and new.

02:09:15.037 --> 02:09:17.772
We're
upgrading our combat feel.

02:09:17.907 --> 02:09:19.575
The roles are
playing differently

02:09:19.709 --> 02:09:21.043
when it comes
to all-new maps.

02:09:21.177 --> 02:09:26.014
It's a pretty big departure from
where we are on live right now.

02:09:26.148 --> 02:09:28.617
So I can't wait to
see where we end up

02:09:28.751 --> 02:09:28.951
with it by the time
we launch this thing.

02:09:34.357 --> 02:09:35.857
We're also experimenting with
an idea we call Role Passives,

02:09:35.992 --> 02:09:37.593
which are
passive abilities

02:09:37.727 --> 02:09:39.995
that a hero can have
based on what role they are.

02:09:40.129 --> 02:09:42.131
For example, currently,
in our internal build,

02:09:42.264 --> 02:09:44.399
the tank heroes all
have knockback reduction

02:09:44.533 --> 02:09:45.567
against them,

02:09:45.701 --> 02:09:47.636
and they generate
less ultimate charge

02:09:47.770 --> 02:09:49.004
for enemies
shooting at them.

02:09:49.138 --> 02:09:50.939
Damage-dealing heroes
have a movement-speed bonus,

02:09:51.073 --> 02:09:53.341
which is great for
flanking around the map.

02:09:53.476 --> 02:09:55.544
And healing
heroes, support heroes

02:09:55.678 --> 02:09:57.813
have
automatic healing that

02:09:57.947 --> 02:09:59.815
kicks in after they haven't
taken damage for a while,

02:09:59.949 --> 02:10:02.151
very similar to Mercy's passive,
but a bit of a lower rate.

02:10:02.284 --> 02:10:04.320
One of the more
shocking changes

02:10:04.453 --> 02:10:08.090
that we've been
exploring in Overwatch 2 PvP

02:10:08.224 --> 02:10:11.359
is a change to
the tank role entirely,

02:10:11.494 --> 02:10:13.862
that's applied
to all of the tanks.

02:10:13.996 --> 02:10:17.599
We want to try to make
them more toe-to-toe brawlers,

02:10:17.733 --> 02:10:19.134
and less characters that

02:10:19.268 --> 02:10:22.238
just stand back
and protect other people.

02:10:22.371 --> 02:10:23.405
So, for
example, for Reinhardt,

02:10:23.539 --> 02:10:26.008
we've given him
two charges of Fire Strike

02:10:26.142 --> 02:10:27.209
so he can throw Fire
Strikes a lot more aggressively

02:10:27.343 --> 02:10:28.510
and more often.

02:10:28.644 --> 02:10:31.646
Also, his Charge,
he's able to cancel it now,

02:10:31.781 --> 02:10:33.381
and you can steer
it more aggressively

02:10:33.516 --> 02:10:35.650
so you can not
quite turn a corner,

02:10:35.785 --> 02:10:37.519
but you can definitely
more accurately pin targets.

02:10:37.653 --> 02:10:38.620
Because you can cancel it,

02:10:38.754 --> 02:10:40.589
it allows you to
use it more aggressively

02:10:40.723 --> 02:10:42.258
and go after
those key targets

02:10:42.391 --> 02:10:44.793
without feeling like
you're going to sacrifice

02:10:44.927 --> 02:10:46.194
all of your
positioning to get there.

02:10:46.328 --> 02:10:48.830
He's almost
more terrifying now,

02:10:48.964 --> 02:10:50.432
being able to unleash
his full arsenal more often

02:10:50.566 --> 02:10:52.201
than just being
the guy with a shield.

02:10:53.669 --> 02:10:56.138
The changes to
Reinhardt that we're trying -

02:10:56.272 --> 02:10:58.808
and they might not
ship, that's just the reality -

02:10:58.941 --> 02:11:02.578
but the changes
that we're trying right now

02:11:02.711 --> 02:11:06.448
are to try to embrace more of
that instinct that players have

02:11:06.582 --> 02:11:07.849
when they
want to play a big,

02:11:07.983 --> 02:11:11.120
burly character
that looks aggressive,

02:11:11.253 --> 02:11:13.389
and feels like it
should be aggressive.

02:11:20.863 --> 02:11:23.098
Shortly after
BlizzCon, we spun up this group,

02:11:23.232 --> 02:11:26.635
our combat feel
group, to really just work on:

02:11:26.769 --> 02:11:31.373
What happens when
a player holds the trigger?

02:11:31.507 --> 02:11:32.774
We're
putting a ton of effort

02:11:32.908 --> 02:11:35.944
into looking at
all of our characters

02:11:36.078 --> 02:11:40.048
and trying to give them an
even more visceral weapon feel.

02:11:40.182 --> 02:11:45.454
From sound to VFX, to animation,
to the design of these units,

02:11:45.588 --> 02:11:49.859
all new sounds for a lot
of our weapons in the game,

02:11:49.992 --> 02:11:52.728
and a whole new
sound system that's driving it.

02:11:52.862 --> 02:11:53.796
We didn't
just level up a unit,

02:11:53.929 --> 02:11:55.631
we levelled up
the rest of the game.

02:11:59.235 --> 02:12:01.169
When we first
started working on Overwatch,

02:12:01.303 --> 02:12:05.006
we spent quite a bit of
time working on gunfire sounds

02:12:05.141 --> 02:12:07.409
and just general combat feel
to make it feel really awesome.

02:12:07.543 --> 02:12:10.211
We're revisiting everything.
Nothing's off the table.

02:12:10.346 --> 02:12:12.848
So we're even
looking at small things,

02:12:12.982 --> 02:12:14.616
such as quick-melee
attacks that every hero has.

02:12:14.750 --> 02:12:16.418
So they have
a new audio pass,

02:12:16.552 --> 02:12:21.089
and a new feel and visual
effect when you hit things.

02:12:21.223 --> 02:12:23.892
One of the bigger efforts we're
undertaking for Overwatch 2

02:12:24.026 --> 02:12:26.762
is what we're calling
our weapon 2.0 sound pass.

02:12:26.896 --> 02:12:30.165
This is a big overhaul to
the weapon systems in the game.

02:12:30.299 --> 02:12:33.602
That means the way
the gun feels in your hands.

02:12:33.736 --> 02:12:35.037
You can feel the
ammo running out

02:12:35.171 --> 02:12:36.438
as you're
getting lower on the clip.

02:12:36.572 --> 02:12:39.508
We've really tried
to amplify those sounds

02:12:39.642 --> 02:12:41.377
to better
encapsulate the gameplay

02:12:41.510 --> 02:12:43.479
and the gunplay that
comes with Overwatch 2.

02:12:45.114 --> 02:12:46.849
GUNFIRE REVERBERATING

02:12:46.982 --> 02:12:48.417
Key for the weapons
was getting them to feel

02:12:48.551 --> 02:12:50.485
like they're in
their environment.

02:12:50.619 --> 02:12:52.320
This meant working
with convolution reverb.

02:12:52.454 --> 02:12:55.190
It's a new system that we've
implemented into Overwatch.

02:12:55.324 --> 02:12:58.827
It's a way that we can capture
the acoustics of an environment

02:12:58.961 --> 02:13:01.396
and transport it onto
the sounds that we're using.

02:13:01.530 --> 02:13:04.933
So we went and captured tails of
guns in different environments

02:13:05.067 --> 02:13:06.668
through our weapon shoots.

02:13:06.802 --> 02:13:08.804
GUNFIRE REVERBERATING

02:13:12.274 --> 02:13:13.642
Wow.

02:13:14.376 --> 02:13:15.911
GUNSHOT REVERBERATES

02:13:17.012 --> 02:13:19.014
GUNFIRE REVERBERATING

02:13:19.782 --> 02:13:21.016
And then we've
cut them and applied it

02:13:21.150 --> 02:13:22.785
so we can support a
lot more environments.

02:13:24.453 --> 02:13:25.721
We're now
recording outdoor...

02:13:26.522 --> 02:13:28.257
urban outdoor...

02:13:28.958 --> 02:13:30.659
warehouse tails...

02:13:31.493 --> 02:13:32.528
tight tunnels...

02:13:33.128 --> 02:13:34.229
small rooms,

02:13:34.363 --> 02:13:37.165
and it gives a lot more
presence to the weapons

02:13:37.299 --> 02:13:38.967
in the way that
they react in the world.

02:13:39.101 --> 02:13:42.770
The shooter genre has evolved a
lot since Overwatch 1 came out.

02:13:42.905 --> 02:13:44.106
On top of that,
we really want to push

02:13:44.240 --> 02:13:46.808
the visceral nature
of how we do combat.

02:13:46.942 --> 02:13:49.778
So we focused a lot not just
on the wonderful sound effects,

02:13:49.912 --> 02:13:53.348
but also with how the
gun moves as you shoot,

02:13:53.482 --> 02:13:56.551
so you can really feel every
single shot leaving the chamber.

02:13:56.685 --> 02:13:59.187
And there's a lot of subtle
little tweaks we have to do

02:13:59.321 --> 02:14:01.723
on the gameplay-engineering
side to make that feel visceral.

02:14:01.857 --> 02:14:05.860
Everything from making sure that
the camera shakes are crisp,

02:14:05.995 --> 02:14:07.863
that if you get
shot, you know exactly...

02:14:07.997 --> 02:14:10.265
These tight indicators show up
on the outside of your reticle.

02:14:10.399 --> 02:14:12.200
All these things play
into making the game feel

02:14:12.334 --> 02:14:14.836
not just tight
and responsive,

02:14:14.970 --> 02:14:16.171
but also
modern and up-to-date.

02:14:16.939 --> 02:14:18.940
Take 76, for example.

02:14:19.074 --> 02:14:20.975
What we wanted to do in
Overwatch 2 with his weapon

02:14:21.110 --> 02:14:23.344
was make it really
feel incredibly powerful,

02:14:23.479 --> 02:14:26.414
and that happens with different
elements that mix together.

02:14:26.548 --> 02:14:27.749
But it really
feels like the gun

02:14:27.883 --> 02:14:29.817
is almost just
outside of your control.

02:14:29.952 --> 02:14:33.255
A lot of that comes down to
this camera-shake technology

02:14:33.389 --> 02:14:35.623
where, every time
you fire the weapon,

02:14:35.758 --> 02:14:36.458
you want to
feel like it's running

02:14:36.592 --> 02:14:37.626
through your entire body,

02:14:37.760 --> 02:14:39.928
and the camera shake gives
that extra bite

02:14:40.062 --> 02:14:41.163
to the visceral shot
out of the chamber.

02:14:41.297 --> 02:14:43.365
It makes him
feel really great.

02:14:43.499 --> 02:14:46.702
GEOFF: You guys got a sneak peek
of Sojourn from last BlizzCon.

02:14:46.835 --> 02:14:49.705
She's been under
development for quite a while,

02:14:49.838 --> 02:14:50.939
and she's
quickly, at this point,

02:14:51.073 --> 02:14:53.308
become a lot of
people's favorites, in-house.

02:14:53.442 --> 02:14:57.245
When we look back at Overwatch
when it was originally created,

02:14:57.379 --> 02:14:58.280
there were a lot of
heroes that were made,

02:14:58.414 --> 02:15:00.148
from a
gameplay standpoint,

02:15:00.282 --> 02:15:01.783
surrounding a weapon type.

02:15:01.917 --> 02:15:05.387
For example, Pharah was
based on a rocket launcher hero.

02:15:05.521 --> 02:15:07.723
Widowmaker's
clearly the sniper.

02:15:07.856 --> 02:15:09.591
We thought there was
a weapon that was missing

02:15:09.725 --> 02:15:10.992
that would be fun to
play with, and that's a railgun.

02:15:11.126 --> 02:15:12.994
It's so much
fun in the playtest

02:15:13.128 --> 02:15:14.462
to have this
really powerful shot

02:15:14.596 --> 02:15:18.266
that can rip through enemies if
you really are accurate with it.

02:15:18.400 --> 02:15:19.634
She's all
about that aim skill,

02:15:19.768 --> 02:15:21.270
so if you've got that
aim skill, you'll love her.

02:15:22.805 --> 02:15:25.808
We're still
exploring new game modes,

02:15:25.941 --> 02:15:29.978
and we're also
reevaluating older game modes

02:15:30.112 --> 02:15:32.814
that people
are more critical of.

02:15:32.948 --> 02:15:34.015
You know,
we're of the mindset,

02:15:34.149 --> 02:15:37.719
maybe 2CP
doesn't exist in Overwatch 2,

02:15:37.853 --> 02:15:41.957
and maybe there's a new,
cool game mode that replaces it.

02:15:42.091 --> 02:15:47.229
We really want Overwatch 2
to feel like the next evolution,

02:15:47.363 --> 02:15:50.365
a true sequel
to the first game,

02:15:50.499 --> 02:15:52.067
not an add-on.

02:15:52.201 --> 02:15:53.902
It's not a small part.

02:15:54.036 --> 02:15:56.704
It's not an
extension of the original game.

02:15:56.839 --> 02:15:58.040
This is an evolution

02:15:58.173 --> 02:16:00.641
and a replacement
to the original game.

02:16:00.776 --> 02:16:01.710
And I think it's exciting.

02:16:05.381 --> 02:16:08.650
Hero Missions are
probably one of the things

02:16:08.784 --> 02:16:10.118
that's the hardest to
wrap your head around

02:16:10.252 --> 02:16:12.788
if you've never
played one of them.

02:16:13.689 --> 02:16:15.357
# DRAMATIC
ORCHESTRAL MUSIC

02:16:17.393 --> 02:16:19.161
AARON: The goal
around Hero Missions

02:16:19.294 --> 02:16:24.600
is for these to be this
co-op PvE experience.

02:16:24.733 --> 02:16:26.668
I personally only
play Competitive mode.

02:16:26.802 --> 02:16:28.036
I'm all about
that competition.

02:16:28.170 --> 02:16:30.005
And I have friends that
don't want to do that at all,

02:16:30.139 --> 02:16:32.173
so I'm really
looking to a game

02:16:32.307 --> 02:16:34.242
that I can play
for hours and hours

02:16:34.376 --> 02:16:35.544
with friends who
aren't into Competitive,

02:16:35.677 --> 02:16:38.346
and it's also
something I'm interested in.

02:16:38.480 --> 02:16:41.516
Hero Missions are the
content that people are playing

02:16:41.650 --> 02:16:43.418
as they are
levelling up their heroes.

02:16:43.552 --> 02:16:46.788
And so, for a
system like this to really sing,

02:16:46.922 --> 02:16:49.791
you need a
lot of missions.

02:16:49.925 --> 02:16:51.226
We don't want
players to feel like

02:16:51.360 --> 02:16:53.295
they're just in this
grind to get to the top.

02:16:54.696 --> 02:16:57.165
There's a lot of really,
really good back-end technology

02:16:57.299 --> 02:16:58.666
that we're exploring

02:16:58.801 --> 02:17:01.169
so that the heroes
are constantly bringing

02:17:01.303 --> 02:17:03.038
their personality

02:17:03.172 --> 02:17:05.073
and some light story to
these Hero Missions as well.

02:17:06.675 --> 02:17:09.243
The goal is to
make as many as possible,

02:17:09.378 --> 02:17:11.547
hundreds of Hero Missions.

02:17:12.915 --> 02:17:14.115
We've explored a
lot of different ways

02:17:14.249 --> 02:17:16.718
of getting to
that much content.

02:17:16.852 --> 02:17:19.054
They'll have
different sets of enemy units

02:17:19.188 --> 02:17:21.056
that people will
be fighting against,

02:17:21.190 --> 02:17:23.592
and there's
different Hero Missions types.

02:17:23.725 --> 02:17:26.328
At the same time,
Hero Missions can take place

02:17:26.462 --> 02:17:29.397
in all of the multiplayer
maps that we've done,

02:17:29.531 --> 02:17:32.501
and we're also adding new
spaces onto some of these maps.

02:17:36.238 --> 02:17:38.940
BILL: It's a huge
challenge for the art team.

02:17:39.074 --> 02:17:41.810
We take maps that a lot
of people love and recognize,

02:17:41.944 --> 02:17:45.013
and we have to add a lot
more art and level design to it.

02:17:45.147 --> 02:17:47.449
JEFF K: In this one
Hero Mission playtest,

02:17:47.583 --> 02:17:49.251
we came up to
an area in King's Row

02:17:49.384 --> 02:17:51.319
that usually
has a gate on it.

02:17:51.453 --> 02:17:53.255
Suddenly, that gate opened

02:17:53.388 --> 02:17:55.023
and I saw a new
area of King's Row

02:17:55.157 --> 02:17:57.693
that I had
never seen before.

02:17:57.826 --> 02:18:00.862
DION: You may be
playing a payload game type,

02:18:00.996 --> 02:18:02.630
and the payload may
decide to take this new route

02:18:02.764 --> 02:18:05.234
instead of
going the usual route.

02:18:05.367 --> 02:18:07.769
That was
really, really cool.

02:18:07.903 --> 02:18:09.538
It was like this
eye-opening, special moment.

02:18:13.475 --> 02:18:17.512
Lan, our Lead Tech
Artist on the Environment side,

02:18:17.646 --> 02:18:19.847
very early,
on her own free time,

02:18:19.982 --> 02:18:23.317
made a prototype of a sandstorm
on the Temple of Anubis map.

02:18:23.452 --> 02:18:25.320
At the time, we didn't know
what we wanted to do with that,

02:18:25.454 --> 02:18:27.121
but it looked awesome.

02:18:27.256 --> 02:18:28.923
We looked at it
and went, "Oh, my God.

02:18:29.057 --> 02:18:30.459
We absolutely
have to do this."

02:18:30.592 --> 02:18:32.727
DION: So we put
some new technology in place

02:18:32.861 --> 02:18:34.729
that allows us to
do this dynamically.

02:18:34.863 --> 02:18:37.199
You start the
mission off, clear day.

02:18:37.332 --> 02:18:38.666
Mid-way
through the mission,

02:18:38.800 --> 02:18:41.169
suddenly this sandstorm or
heavy weather would show up.

02:18:41.303 --> 02:18:43.972
It made the world just
feel so much more alive.

02:18:46.542 --> 02:18:48.876
DION: There's a
sunset, daytime, night-time,

02:18:49.011 --> 02:18:51.146
but depending on
where you are in the world,

02:18:51.280 --> 02:18:52.346
these look different.

02:18:52.481 --> 02:18:54.082
California or
Hollywood would have

02:18:54.216 --> 02:18:56.818
what we call
a California sun.

02:18:56.952 --> 02:18:59.354
In Numbani, there's a great
African sun that happens there.

02:18:59.488 --> 02:19:01.923
You start to get a
sense of space and mood.

02:19:02.057 --> 02:19:03.659
This is what really
brings the levels to life.

02:19:05.961 --> 02:19:07.595
BILL: For me,
the dynamic world's exciting,

02:19:07.729 --> 02:19:10.231
just seeing these
dramatic landscapes,

02:19:10.365 --> 02:19:12.501
the wind and the
atmosphere blowing by.

02:19:12.634 --> 02:19:14.736
It's a much more cinematic
experience for the player.

02:19:15.771 --> 02:19:17.606
Before you start a
mission, you look at a map.

02:19:18.640 --> 02:19:21.075
It's
night-time in Necropolis,

02:19:21.209 --> 02:19:23.578
or there's a
sandstorm in Necropolis.

02:19:23.712 --> 02:19:24.679
Players can make
some comp choices

02:19:24.813 --> 02:19:26.514
based on
knowing this information.

02:19:26.648 --> 02:19:28.449
Characters like
Hanzo or Widowmaker

02:19:28.584 --> 02:19:30.251
start to be a
little more valuable

02:19:30.385 --> 02:19:31.620
because they have
abilities that allow them

02:19:31.753 --> 02:19:33.622
to see through
the sand more clearly.

02:19:36.758 --> 02:19:39.794
Are we going to talk about
any of the types of objectives

02:19:39.928 --> 02:19:41.863
in Hero Missions?

02:19:41.997 --> 02:19:44.266
Yeah, let's talk
about the objectives.

02:19:44.399 --> 02:19:46.934
Players are being
asked to do different things

02:19:47.069 --> 02:19:48.936
from one Hero
Mission to the next

02:19:49.071 --> 02:19:50.338
so that they all feel
really fresh to the player.

02:19:50.472 --> 02:19:53.074
For example,
we have Gather and Return,

02:19:53.208 --> 02:19:55.143
where you're trying to
grab these different canisters

02:19:55.277 --> 02:19:57.279
to safeguard
them so they don't go off

02:19:57.412 --> 02:19:59.414
and affect the
population in a negative way.

02:19:59.548 --> 02:20:00.482
It creates this
really cool tension

02:20:00.616 --> 02:20:02.817
in the world
where, on the one hand,

02:20:02.951 --> 02:20:03.985
you're trying to split up
and grab these canisters

02:20:04.119 --> 02:20:06.454
to be as
efficient as possible,

02:20:06.588 --> 02:20:07.755
but at the same time,

02:20:07.889 --> 02:20:08.689
now you're having these
special enemy units spawn

02:20:08.824 --> 02:20:10.292
that are really difficult.

02:20:10.425 --> 02:20:12.761
And if you get
caught by yourself,

02:20:12.894 --> 02:20:15.430
it turns into a pretty
bad situation really quickly.

02:20:15.564 --> 02:20:16.164
It feels
extremely cooperative

02:20:16.298 --> 02:20:17.866
in a very different way

02:20:18.000 --> 02:20:19.601
to interact with the
Overwatch universe.

02:20:21.703 --> 02:20:24.539
We're experimenting
with all sorts of ideas.

02:20:24.673 --> 02:20:27.175
Just internally, we have
names like Wall of Death,

02:20:27.309 --> 02:20:30.178
Scavenger
Hunt and Kill Quest.

02:20:30.312 --> 02:20:32.513
With all these
new mechanics

02:20:32.648 --> 02:20:34.449
and enemies for
people to experience,

02:20:34.583 --> 02:20:37.385
players will be able to
jump in, night after night,

02:20:37.519 --> 02:20:39.320
play different
Hero Missions,

02:20:39.454 --> 02:20:41.589
and then work
through the progression system,

02:20:41.723 --> 02:20:42.790
levelling up their heroes.

02:20:42.924 --> 02:20:44.960
# EPIC ORCHESTRAL MUSIC

02:20:50.465 --> 02:20:54.135
At BlizzCon in 2019 we started
to talk about progression,

02:20:54.269 --> 02:20:58.506
and we showed the very early
version of our Talent system,

02:20:58.640 --> 02:21:00.107
which I think
was really cool.

02:21:00.242 --> 02:21:03.544
We had that one Talent where
Mei became the rolling snowball,

02:21:03.679 --> 02:21:05.146
which was a big hit.

02:21:05.280 --> 02:21:07.081
We've really
blown that system out.

02:21:07.215 --> 02:21:08.482
I'm excited about Talents.

02:21:08.617 --> 02:21:11.352
You can play the same
hero in so many different ways.

02:21:11.486 --> 02:21:13.054
Now, with the skill tree,

02:21:13.188 --> 02:21:14.655
you can have fun every
night doing different things

02:21:14.790 --> 02:21:16.758
and kind of experimenting.

02:21:16.892 --> 02:21:20.829
Yeah, the Talent
system is really deep and rich,

02:21:20.962 --> 02:21:23.030
and every single
hero has different trees.

02:21:23.165 --> 02:21:25.767
You might
open up 76's tree

02:21:25.901 --> 02:21:28.970
and, as you're levelling
and picking new Talents,

02:21:29.104 --> 02:21:30.671
you're starting to
feel your hero change.

02:21:31.440 --> 02:21:35.043
We've had some pretty hilarious
versions of the healing one,

02:21:35.177 --> 02:21:37.612
where his Biotic
Field travels with him,

02:21:37.746 --> 02:21:39.781
and it also
repulses enemies.

02:21:39.915 --> 02:21:41.115
We called it
the snowplow build,

02:21:41.249 --> 02:21:43.651
where 76 is
running through spaces,

02:21:43.785 --> 02:21:45.453
pushing enemies
away from him. (LAUGHS)

02:21:45.587 --> 02:21:46.988
Designing
these is super fun

02:21:47.122 --> 02:21:49.390
because we get to break all the
rules that we've established.

02:21:49.524 --> 02:21:52.861
We get to really take the
gloves off and do crazy things.

02:21:52.994 --> 02:21:55.396
Junkrat can
dual-wield grenade launchers,

02:21:55.530 --> 02:21:57.932
and we've had Mercy
wield an area-of-effect rez

02:21:58.066 --> 02:22:00.935
on her whole team at once, at
super-long range, through walls.

02:22:01.069 --> 02:22:03.338
It's been like a mad
scientist making this stuff.

02:22:03.472 --> 02:22:04.872
You're all used to
using these kinetic weapons,

02:22:05.006 --> 02:22:08.076
like Soldier: 76's rifle,

02:22:08.210 --> 02:22:10.478
or a fire attack, like what
Reinhardt's Fire Strike does.

02:22:10.612 --> 02:22:13.815
As soon as you start to mutate
these things with Talents,

02:22:13.949 --> 02:22:14.616
all of a sudden,
maybe you're doing freeze damage

02:22:14.750 --> 02:22:16.718
or electrical damage.

02:22:16.852 --> 02:22:18.820
It gives the animators a
bunch of crazy opportunities.

02:22:18.954 --> 02:22:21.156
The animations are going
to change to show them freezing

02:22:21.289 --> 02:22:22.356
and shattering,

02:22:22.491 --> 02:22:24.692
and if you shock
someone with a lightning attack,

02:22:24.826 --> 02:22:27.228
they can shake in place and
maybe chain to other enemies.

02:22:27.362 --> 02:22:28.830
Stuff to make you feel

02:22:28.964 --> 02:22:30.031
like you're
controlling the battlefield

02:22:30.165 --> 02:22:30.398
in novel ways.

02:22:33.168 --> 02:22:34.635
As a huge RPG nerd myself,

02:22:34.770 --> 02:22:38.005
the first time I opened up the
Talent tree system it was like,

02:22:38.140 --> 02:22:41.176
"Oh, my gosh. This is
speaking my love language."

02:22:41.309 --> 02:22:43.244
I just want to
plan and I want to see:

02:22:43.378 --> 02:22:44.412
Where do I want to invest,

02:22:44.546 --> 02:22:46.247
and how am I going
to play this character?

02:22:46.381 --> 02:22:47.815
Yes, it's so great!

02:22:47.949 --> 02:22:50.118
Like, you start at
the bottom of the tree...

02:22:50.252 --> 02:22:53.154
-Yes, exactly!
-Like, "Tracer gets to do what?"

02:22:53.288 --> 02:22:54.555
And then you start working

02:22:54.689 --> 02:22:56.323
and planning how
you're going to get down there.

02:22:56.458 --> 02:22:58.192
It's like... It's so fun.

02:22:58.326 --> 02:22:59.294
LAUGHTER

02:23:05.667 --> 02:23:09.904
When we look
back at BlizzCon 2019

02:23:10.038 --> 02:23:12.573
and we talk about some
of the criticisms that we had,

02:23:13.408 --> 02:23:16.043
one of them, as a
development team, that we felt

02:23:16.178 --> 02:23:19.414
was that the combat
was just not engaging enough.

02:23:19.548 --> 02:23:21.249
So the fact
that our combat

02:23:21.383 --> 02:23:25.152
wasn't highly
engaging to us as players

02:23:25.287 --> 02:23:29.223
meant that we had a
problem with the enemy units,

02:23:29.357 --> 02:23:32.493
in that they just weren't
feeling interesting enough.

02:23:32.627 --> 02:23:34.595
Just because we
show something to the public,

02:23:34.729 --> 02:23:36.631
that doesn't mean
that is what it's going to be.

02:23:36.765 --> 02:23:37.732
If we make
discoveries where

02:23:37.866 --> 02:23:39.634
things aren't
working how we want,

02:23:39.768 --> 02:23:41.769
they're not reading how
we want, they're not fun enough,

02:23:41.903 --> 02:23:45.206
we're totally down to
reinvestigate, reiterate,

02:23:45.340 --> 02:23:46.674
and really
find ways to level

02:23:46.808 --> 02:23:49.644
all aspects of
things until they feel good.

02:23:49.778 --> 02:23:54.449
I would say a
major focus of all of 2020

02:23:54.583 --> 02:23:59.220
was to make the Null Sector
enemy units more engaging,

02:23:59.354 --> 02:24:01.622
and some of this
was adding new units

02:24:01.756 --> 02:24:04.259
and evolving
other units that we had.

02:24:04.392 --> 02:24:07.963
Interesting combat for
us is varied combat for us.

02:24:08.630 --> 02:24:10.765
So sometimes
we're going to ask you

02:24:10.899 --> 02:24:13.634
to protect
something in Overwatch 2.

02:24:13.768 --> 02:24:16.404
Sometimes we're just going
to ask you to get someplace.

02:24:16.538 --> 02:24:19.773
Sometimes we might ask you
to escort something across a map

02:24:19.908 --> 02:24:21.475
while it's being attacked.

02:24:21.610 --> 02:24:23.444
So it's a
different type of spawning,

02:24:23.578 --> 02:24:25.346
and it's a different
type of units that are in there,

02:24:25.480 --> 02:24:27.215
and it's a
simpler objective.

02:24:27.349 --> 02:24:29.117
There's a lot of units
Aaron is talking about.

02:24:29.251 --> 02:24:30.452
Different
units, different types,

02:24:30.585 --> 02:24:31.852
they serve
different purposes,

02:24:31.987 --> 02:24:33.888
you do
different things to them.

02:24:34.022 --> 02:24:35.756
It's going to help
a lot of us find the fun

02:24:35.891 --> 02:24:37.558
and make sure that
Overwatch 2 is a blast.

02:24:38.293 --> 02:24:40.361
One of those
sets of enemies

02:24:40.495 --> 02:24:43.797
is what we internally
call "objective units,"

02:24:43.932 --> 02:24:47.902
and they're typically units
that don't even attack players.

02:24:48.036 --> 02:24:50.638
The simplest version would
be something like the Breacher.

02:24:50.772 --> 02:24:52.774
It's two legs with this
huge bomb on its back,

02:24:52.908 --> 02:24:55.309
and it was
built to do one thing:

02:24:55.443 --> 02:24:59.380
Slowly and methodically
march towards its objective.

02:24:59.514 --> 02:25:01.783
And then it transforms,

02:25:01.917 --> 02:25:03.451
and the bomb
opens up like a flower,

02:25:03.585 --> 02:25:05.453
starts spinning
and charging up,

02:25:05.587 --> 02:25:07.188
and you hear
this awesome sound,

02:25:07.322 --> 02:25:09.323
and you know you
have only a little bit of time

02:25:09.457 --> 02:25:11.926
before it's going to explode
and you have to take it out.

02:25:12.060 --> 02:25:13.061
BEEPING INTENSIFIES

02:25:16.598 --> 02:25:18.466
GEOFF: We have
units like the Slicers,

02:25:18.600 --> 02:25:20.001
which you guys
have seen before.

02:25:20.135 --> 02:25:21.336
Little... We
call them chickens.

02:25:21.469 --> 02:25:24.238
(LAUGHS) They're little
chickens. They run really fast.

02:25:24.372 --> 02:25:25.640
We have a guy we're
experimenting with now

02:25:25.774 --> 02:25:27.942
who flies and
drops these bombs

02:25:28.076 --> 02:25:30.678
that create these
ever-expanding smoke fields

02:25:30.812 --> 02:25:31.646
that you have
to escape from

02:25:31.780 --> 02:25:33.214
because
they're super deadly,

02:25:33.348 --> 02:25:35.650
so you really get to use your
mobility options to escape that.

02:25:35.784 --> 02:25:37.718
AARON: There's
things like the Puller.

02:25:37.852 --> 02:25:42.456
You could be moving
through these darker alleys,

02:25:42.590 --> 02:25:44.125
and as soon as you
hear that Puller spawn,

02:25:44.259 --> 02:25:48.029
it suddenly becomes
really spooky and kind of scary.

02:25:48.163 --> 02:25:53.000
This very tall, elegant-looking
character that's blindfolded.

02:25:53.134 --> 02:25:56.404
Three orbs that circle
around her as her eyes.

02:25:57.238 --> 02:26:00.508
And we basically spun those orbs
around her head as a big tell,

02:26:00.642 --> 02:26:01.843
launched
them at the player,

02:26:01.977 --> 02:26:03.445
and then it
activates this tractor beam

02:26:03.578 --> 02:26:05.546
that's bringing you
in and bringing you in.

02:26:05.680 --> 02:26:07.649
And then you get
this awesome anticipation

02:26:07.782 --> 02:26:09.417
of the hair opening up

02:26:09.551 --> 02:26:10.384
so you know
what's about to happen.

02:26:10.518 --> 02:26:12.520
RUMBLING

02:26:13.588 --> 02:26:14.789
One thing we've
recently been experimenting with

02:26:14.923 --> 02:26:17.859
is this idea
of elite units.

02:26:17.993 --> 02:26:20.061
We didn't want the elite
units to feel like they're just,

02:26:20.195 --> 02:26:22.630
"This guy's got double
health and double damage."

02:26:22.764 --> 02:26:23.831
We want to make
sure the elite units have

02:26:23.965 --> 02:26:25.533
different
behaviors and attacks.

02:26:25.667 --> 02:26:28.536
So, for
example, an omnic grunt,

02:26:28.670 --> 02:26:32.006
normally he just fires
his gun, he dies pretty simply.

02:26:32.140 --> 02:26:33.407
If you fight
an elite grunt,

02:26:33.541 --> 02:26:36.711
his weapon fires
in a burst-fire pattern.

02:26:36.845 --> 02:26:37.879
It's very
deadly if you're close.

02:26:38.013 --> 02:26:39.480
And if you
manage to take him out,

02:26:39.614 --> 02:26:41.282
they don't
just die right away.

02:26:41.416 --> 02:26:43.083
Instead, they crawl
on the ground towards you

02:26:43.218 --> 02:26:45.387
and you try to back away,

02:26:45.520 --> 02:26:47.722
because if they get
to you they can explode.

02:26:50.625 --> 02:26:51.759
RYAN:
Artillery, at BlizzCon,

02:26:51.893 --> 02:26:53.761
we're all just
staring at this,

02:26:53.895 --> 02:26:55.896
like, "What are we
going to do to level this up?"

02:26:56.031 --> 02:26:57.832
One of the things we really
felt wasn't coming through

02:26:57.966 --> 02:26:59.167
was the damage states.

02:26:59.300 --> 02:27:02.904
We intend for players
to shoot these guns off,

02:27:03.038 --> 02:27:04.071
but currently it's not
reading as something

02:27:04.205 --> 02:27:05.506
that can happen easily.

02:27:05.640 --> 02:27:08.876
RYAN: We changed the design,
we changed the animation,

02:27:09.010 --> 02:27:11.045
and we go back and forth
and just continually iterate.

02:27:11.179 --> 02:27:12.980
We decided,
"Hey, you know what?

02:27:13.114 --> 02:27:15.450
We're going to just delete
half of these damage states

02:27:15.583 --> 02:27:17.285
so now it's
super readable."

02:27:17.419 --> 02:27:18.453
From an
animation standpoint,

02:27:18.586 --> 02:27:20.788
we decided on the
spinning-barrel version.

02:27:20.922 --> 02:27:23.324
It just did this
really nice kickback.

02:27:23.458 --> 02:27:25.493
The tip of the barrel
opens up when it's about to fire

02:27:25.627 --> 02:27:27.595
and shifts into
place, and boom!

02:27:28.797 --> 02:27:32.533
It's really amazing to
be fighting an artillery unit,

02:27:32.667 --> 02:27:34.001
and as you're
shooting the guns on the side

02:27:34.135 --> 02:27:35.503
they're visibly
taking damage.

02:27:35.637 --> 02:27:36.704
Then you
can blow them off,

02:27:36.838 --> 02:27:39.206
and it reacts and
stumbles to the side.

02:27:39.340 --> 02:27:41.809
It makes you feel like
you're there and it's super fun.

02:27:41.943 --> 02:27:42.877
In a lot of the stuff
we've showed so far,

02:27:43.011 --> 02:27:44.945
you're fighting Null Sector.

02:27:45.080 --> 02:27:47.115
These are evil killing machines
intent on marching you down.

02:27:47.248 --> 02:27:49.917
The way you stop
them is by shooting them,

02:27:50.051 --> 02:27:52.887
and we want to make sure that
feels responsive and visceral,

02:27:53.021 --> 02:27:55.289
really making sure
that when they take a hit,

02:27:55.423 --> 02:27:57.025
it feels like their whole
body has been punched,

02:27:57.158 --> 02:27:58.959
almost like
hit by a truck.

02:27:59.094 --> 02:28:01.095
That's what
really sells this fantasy

02:28:01.229 --> 02:28:02.563
that you're
playing these superheroes.

02:28:02.697 --> 02:28:04.766
One of the cool pieces of
technology that we've created

02:28:04.899 --> 02:28:06.801
we call
chain-hit reactions.

02:28:06.935 --> 02:28:09.237
Oftentimes, you'll find
Null Sector bunched up,

02:28:09.370 --> 02:28:12.507
marching, maybe
half a dozen units abreast.

02:28:12.640 --> 02:28:13.741
You expect,
if you shoot one,

02:28:13.875 --> 02:28:14.742
they're going to
knock into their buddies,

02:28:14.876 --> 02:28:16.845
so we made that happen.

02:28:18.279 --> 02:28:18.947
Turns out it's
really fun, too.

02:28:26.321 --> 02:28:28.956
One of the things that we
showed last year at BlizzCon

02:28:29.090 --> 02:28:30.190
was the new
looks for our heroes.

02:28:30.325 --> 02:28:32.427
The theory
going in was like,

02:28:32.560 --> 02:28:34.395
you want to preserve
the essence of your hero,

02:28:34.529 --> 02:28:36.831
but also show
that hero's evolved

02:28:36.965 --> 02:28:39.466
and changed
and move forward.

02:28:39.601 --> 02:28:41.002
So, we have a
lot more new looks,

02:28:41.136 --> 02:28:43.104
so I'm excited to see
how players and fans react

02:28:43.238 --> 02:28:45.339
to all of the ones
we've done so far.

02:28:45.473 --> 02:28:48.909
Maybe we'll share
some of those new looks today,

02:28:49.043 --> 02:28:49.644
which would be cool.

02:28:50.945 --> 02:28:53.614
We're going to
reveal McCree's new look

02:28:53.748 --> 02:28:55.716
for Overwatch 2,
as well as Pharah,

02:28:55.850 --> 02:28:57.719
and we're really excited to show
off some of our first villains,

02:28:57.852 --> 02:28:59.887
with Reaper
and Widowmaker.

02:29:00.021 --> 02:29:02.957
But first, I wanted to talk a
little bit about technical-wear,

02:29:03.091 --> 02:29:04.592
kind of like
technical clothing.

02:29:04.726 --> 02:29:08.529
What that really is is
using very techy fabrics,

02:29:08.663 --> 02:29:11.665
as well as having very
intricate but functional straps,

02:29:11.799 --> 02:29:14.269
designed in a way that is
very aesthetically pleasing.

02:29:15.570 --> 02:29:18.172
With McCree, we took a
couple of different approaches.

02:29:18.306 --> 02:29:19.240
He's got this
classic cowboy look

02:29:19.374 --> 02:29:21.976
that's really
hard to mess with,

02:29:22.110 --> 02:29:23.777
because if you try to make him
look too sophisticated

02:29:23.912 --> 02:29:25.112
or too techy,

02:29:25.246 --> 02:29:26.814
it might not feel
right for the character.

02:29:26.948 --> 02:29:28.015
But as a concept artist,

02:29:28.149 --> 02:29:30.251
you always want to
change things around

02:29:30.385 --> 02:29:33.120
and try different
shapes and silhouettes.

02:29:33.254 --> 02:29:36.390
We did identify his red serape
as the main thing for McCree.

02:29:36.524 --> 02:29:37.724
We also tried a
couple of different things

02:29:37.859 --> 02:29:39.660
with McCree's cowboy hat.

02:29:39.794 --> 02:29:42.196
We didn't really want
to mess with that too much

02:29:42.330 --> 02:29:44.198
because that's a
very iconic part of him,

02:29:44.332 --> 02:29:46.467
so that's another
piece that we kept.

02:29:46.601 --> 02:29:48.570
We tried some
retro cowboy looks

02:29:48.703 --> 02:29:50.738
that mixed in
some tech here and there,

02:29:50.872 --> 02:29:52.907
some that were a
little more techy than others,

02:29:53.041 --> 02:29:55.542
some that were a little
bit more classic cowboy.

02:29:55.677 --> 02:29:58.212
We gave him a
little bit of a longer beard,

02:29:58.346 --> 02:30:01.148
and it really gives
him a nice, more aged look.

02:30:02.483 --> 02:30:04.919
What's different about Pharah
than some of our other heroes

02:30:05.053 --> 02:30:06.621
is Pharah's
completely covered in armor.

02:30:06.754 --> 02:30:09.557
We didn't want to
change her silhouette too much.

02:30:09.691 --> 02:30:11.793
It was more about experimenting
on different colors.

02:30:11.926 --> 02:30:14.929
So it felt really appropriate
for her to be the one

02:30:15.063 --> 02:30:16.597
to harken back to
those original Overwatch colors

02:30:16.731 --> 02:30:18.599
of white and blue.

02:30:18.733 --> 02:30:20.501
One of the things that we
wanted to update on Pharah

02:30:20.635 --> 02:30:21.669
was her visor.

02:30:22.403 --> 02:30:24.439
Instead of being completely
opaque and reflective,

02:30:24.572 --> 02:30:27.375
we tried a little bit more
of a transparent approach.

02:30:27.508 --> 02:30:30.144
And being able to
see through it a little bit now

02:30:30.278 --> 02:30:32.480
allows us to see
some more of her emotions.

02:30:33.414 --> 02:30:35.782
Reaper was actually the one
I was very excited to work on,

02:30:35.917 --> 02:30:37.518
because I could
see a lot of potential

02:30:37.652 --> 02:30:40.121
in how we could
integrate his cloak

02:30:40.255 --> 02:30:42.923
and his cowl
and his armor,

02:30:43.057 --> 02:30:45.092
and how we could make
all of those shapes really sing

02:30:45.226 --> 02:30:48.062
within that
classic Reaper silhouette.

02:30:48.196 --> 02:30:49.997
We tried a couple of
takes with the first pass,

02:30:50.131 --> 02:30:53.668
like completely silver
arms, more layers to his jacket.

02:30:53.801 --> 02:30:56.804
Reaper's mask is
obviously pretty sacred to us,

02:30:56.938 --> 02:30:58.306
but the thing
you'll notice immediately

02:30:58.439 --> 02:31:00.007
is that it's now
completely silver.

02:31:00.141 --> 02:31:02.343
Instead of the bone white,

02:31:02.477 --> 02:31:05.480
he's got this
almost deadly, edgy silver

02:31:05.613 --> 02:31:07.214
that just gives him
that classic Reaper look.

02:31:07.348 --> 02:31:08.549
With Widowmaker,

02:31:08.683 --> 02:31:10.351
we started with
the ideation stage

02:31:10.485 --> 02:31:11.586
exploring a
breadth of ideas.

02:31:11.719 --> 02:31:14.088
We tried things like a
more insectoid approach,

02:31:14.222 --> 02:31:15.523
a treasure hunter.

02:31:15.657 --> 02:31:18.492
One of them made her feel
like a classic Bond villain,

02:31:18.626 --> 02:31:19.593
so we kind of
steered it back towards

02:31:19.727 --> 02:31:21.562
one of the more
cyberpunk-looking ones,

02:31:21.696 --> 02:31:23.831
almost like a
futuristic femme fatale,

02:31:23.965 --> 02:31:24.965
which is basically
what Widowmaker is,

02:31:25.099 --> 02:31:27.568
but she even
feels more upgraded.

02:31:27.702 --> 02:31:29.871
So I'm super glad that
we went down this route.

02:31:30.004 --> 02:31:32.072
We played with
different hairstyles, too.

02:31:32.206 --> 02:31:34.041
Widowmaker has
the really long ponytail

02:31:34.175 --> 02:31:36.243
that's really a
key part of her look,

02:31:36.377 --> 02:31:38.612
and we really liked the
hairstyle on one of the designs

02:31:38.746 --> 02:31:40.380
that was more of a braid,

02:31:40.515 --> 02:31:42.783
and it was an
really interesting look

02:31:42.917 --> 02:31:45.252
that also preserves
Widowmaker's original silhouette

02:31:45.386 --> 02:31:46.553
with that long ponytail.

02:31:46.688 --> 02:31:48.856
We've been working a
lot with our Domino engine

02:31:48.990 --> 02:31:50.791
in making our
cloth look better.

02:31:50.925 --> 02:31:52.926
Now that we
have this cloth tech,

02:31:53.061 --> 02:31:55.329
we're going to
be able to make

02:31:55.463 --> 02:31:56.831
some really
cool, unique silhouettes

02:31:56.964 --> 02:31:59.666
that will help them
stand out from other heroes.

02:31:59.801 --> 02:32:02.536
We've put a lot of time
and effort into our face rig.

02:32:02.670 --> 02:32:05.305
What I mean is we're going
to be able to get really close

02:32:05.440 --> 02:32:06.407
to these characters,

02:32:06.541 --> 02:32:08.276
and it's going
to feel really cool.

02:32:08.409 --> 02:32:10.812
It is absolutely
worth the time and the effort

02:32:10.945 --> 02:32:13.014
to invest in
the technology,

02:32:13.147 --> 02:32:15.349
and that way we can make
these characters do more things

02:32:15.483 --> 02:32:17.285
that we haven't really
been able to do before.

02:32:17.418 --> 02:32:18.652
It's been super exciting
working on the new looks

02:32:18.786 --> 02:32:19.887
for Overwatch 2,

02:32:20.021 --> 02:32:21.856
and it's just
been so inspiring

02:32:21.989 --> 02:32:24.558
to watch the team try things
that we've never tried before,

02:32:24.692 --> 02:32:26.294
and I just can't wait to
show off more in the future.

02:32:30.498 --> 02:32:35.102
Overwatch 2 is probably the
largest opportunity we've had

02:32:35.236 --> 02:32:37.838
since the
inception of the franchise

02:32:37.972 --> 02:32:43.177
to really expand what Overwatch
even means to our players.

02:32:43.311 --> 02:32:47.381
In Zero Hour, we saw that some
of the heroes got back together.

02:32:47.515 --> 02:32:50.751
But we're also seeing that
there seems to be a second,

02:32:50.885 --> 02:32:53.087
huge omnic uprising.

02:32:54.122 --> 02:32:57.358
We're going to learn
who's behind that uprising,

02:32:57.492 --> 02:32:58.859
why that's happening,

02:32:58.993 --> 02:33:00.461
how globally spread it is.

02:33:01.929 --> 02:33:04.198
AARON: The Story and
Franchise Development group,

02:33:04.332 --> 02:33:05.666
they were
really the torchbearers

02:33:05.800 --> 02:33:09.403
of what the big story is that we
are going to tell for this game.

02:33:09.537 --> 02:33:11.939
JEFF K: We have a
lot of interaction with them.

02:33:12.073 --> 02:33:16.010
They're working on in-game
cinematic intros and outros

02:33:16.144 --> 02:33:18.145
for every one of
our story missions.

02:33:18.279 --> 02:33:20.180
SCOTT: It's
been really great to see

02:33:20.314 --> 02:33:21.882
this
collaboration grow and develop.

02:33:22.016 --> 02:33:24.151
The story's just a little more
integrated into our missions

02:33:24.285 --> 02:33:26.554
than it ever
really has been before.

02:33:26.687 --> 02:33:29.523
All of the available heroes get
dialogue for a given mission.

02:33:29.657 --> 02:33:31.091
We have NPCs.

02:33:31.225 --> 02:33:33.194
We have
multiple hero choices.

02:33:34.228 --> 02:33:37.765
We have all sorts of
events that are happening

02:33:37.899 --> 02:33:41.735
that are driving players
to interact with each other,

02:33:41.869 --> 02:33:43.938
or parts of the environment,
or other characters.

02:33:45.606 --> 02:33:48.108
JEFF C: The world of Overwatch
is this bright, shiny future.

02:33:48.242 --> 02:33:50.243
So how do you take
that bright, shiny future

02:33:50.378 --> 02:33:51.345
and create conflict in it?

02:33:51.479 --> 02:33:52.413
How dark can the
world of Overwatch go?

02:33:54.015 --> 02:33:55.515
JASON: The
different challenges

02:33:55.650 --> 02:33:58.052
that these characters
have to overcome in Overwatch 2

02:33:58.186 --> 02:34:01.321
are things that they
haven't ever dealt with before,

02:34:01.456 --> 02:34:02.456
or dealt with
on this scale.

02:34:02.590 --> 02:34:04.992
So what we had to do was

02:34:05.126 --> 02:34:07.628
literally strip it
down to its bare bones of,

02:34:07.762 --> 02:34:09.663
"Where are these
characters in their lives?"

02:34:09.797 --> 02:34:10.998
Overwatch has
been disbanded,

02:34:11.132 --> 02:34:12.099
and now
there's a situation

02:34:12.233 --> 02:34:14.801
where the
world's in trouble again

02:34:14.936 --> 02:34:17.672
and the world
needs the heroes again,

02:34:17.805 --> 02:34:18.839
but they're
not allowed to be

02:34:18.973 --> 02:34:20.174
doing what they
were supposed to be doing.

02:34:21.843 --> 02:34:25.312
We started to book
these off-site locations

02:34:25.446 --> 02:34:28.349
so we could
brainstorm, over and over,

02:34:28.483 --> 02:34:30.017
different versions

02:34:30.151 --> 02:34:33.220
of what the Overwatch
2 story campaign could be.

02:34:33.354 --> 02:34:34.721
And we basically
had these whiteboards

02:34:34.856 --> 02:34:36.490
that we were
carting around everywhere.

02:34:36.624 --> 02:34:37.658
The room was
filled with whiteboards,

02:34:37.792 --> 02:34:39.293
and we would
write, every day,

02:34:39.427 --> 02:34:41.595
story beats
with the characters,

02:34:41.729 --> 02:34:42.897
and who was
doing what, and where.

02:34:43.030 --> 02:34:44.598
"Oh, my gosh!
We forgot this character!"

02:34:44.732 --> 02:34:47.401
JEFF K: We had
these giant sticky notes,

02:34:47.535 --> 02:34:49.103
and each one of
them would be a mission

02:34:49.237 --> 02:34:50.971
with a
bunch of notes on it.

02:34:51.105 --> 02:34:53.373
Somebody would say, "Well, what
if we moved Rio to the end?"

02:34:53.508 --> 02:34:55.709
and they would
physically grab it off the wall

02:34:55.843 --> 02:34:56.910
and stick it on the end.

02:34:57.044 --> 02:34:58.646
We did this for a while,

02:34:58.779 --> 02:35:02.516
until we all felt that it was
pretty close to what we wanted.

02:35:02.650 --> 02:35:03.917
JASON: Then we could
send that to our writers' rooms.

02:35:04.051 --> 02:35:06.854
They then had a
much clearer direction

02:35:06.988 --> 02:35:08.155
of where we
wanted this process to go.

02:35:08.289 --> 02:35:09.990
JEFF C: Ultimately,
we come up with something

02:35:10.124 --> 02:35:12.192
that we think is
right for the game,

02:35:12.326 --> 02:35:14.896
and then we pitch it
back to the game team

02:35:15.029 --> 02:35:16.263
and they point out
everything we missed.

02:35:16.397 --> 02:35:17.998
That allows us
to actually back up,

02:35:18.132 --> 02:35:20.067
make it right
and make it better.

02:35:20.201 --> 02:35:22.136
JEFF C: Because the
scale of this story is so huge -

02:35:22.270 --> 02:35:24.071
it takes place over
the whole world, basically -

02:35:24.205 --> 02:35:27.541
it just requires
constant iteration,

02:35:27.675 --> 02:35:28.876
constant refinement

02:35:29.010 --> 02:35:30.444
to make the story the
best that we can make it.

02:35:31.279 --> 02:35:33.480
JASON: From
writing, we go to storyboards.

02:35:33.614 --> 02:35:35.415
The storyboards
get crafted with the edit.

02:35:35.550 --> 02:35:37.585
From there, it will go
to our Previz department,

02:35:37.718 --> 02:35:40.520
where they'll start
to flesh it out in 3D.

02:35:40.655 --> 02:35:42.857
Once we've figured out our
camerawork and cinematography,

02:35:42.990 --> 02:35:44.925
that will then go to
the Animation department.

02:35:45.826 --> 02:35:47.862
And then we end up going
right into the game engine.

02:35:50.665 --> 02:35:53.767
AARON: One of our
challenges was to figure out

02:35:53.901 --> 02:35:56.069
how to
integrate this story

02:35:56.203 --> 02:35:58.272
and this
narrative with the gameplay.

02:35:59.407 --> 02:36:02.809
So, the intro cinematic
ends with this huge battle,

02:36:02.944 --> 02:36:04.078
this huge war.

02:36:04.211 --> 02:36:05.980
When you get into
the game, it has to mesh,

02:36:06.113 --> 02:36:08.649
feel the exact same
way and blend together.

02:36:11.185 --> 02:36:13.253
We're also
trying to get the music

02:36:13.387 --> 02:36:14.888
to be
equally as consistent,

02:36:15.022 --> 02:36:17.458
from in-game cinematics
straight through to the mission.

02:36:17.592 --> 02:36:19.292
Adam Burgess,
our Lead Composer,

02:36:19.427 --> 02:36:22.930
has been working with the
IGC team, with the game team,

02:36:23.064 --> 02:36:26.267
developing
themes for all of the heroes,

02:36:26.400 --> 02:36:27.100
all of the locations.

02:36:27.234 --> 02:36:29.670
As the Cinematic Director,

02:36:29.804 --> 02:36:32.472
whatever we're crafting should
never override the gameplay.

02:36:32.607 --> 02:36:34.742
We want to make sure
that the player's experience,

02:36:34.875 --> 02:36:37.378
from the cinematic to
the gameplay, is seamless

02:36:37.511 --> 02:36:39.280
and feels like a
completely immersive experience.

02:36:39.413 --> 02:36:41.848
As the engineers,
we have to find a way

02:36:41.983 --> 02:36:43.483
to actually make
that come to fruition.

02:36:43.618 --> 02:36:46.153
The Choreo tool allows people
to coordinate different events

02:36:46.287 --> 02:36:47.688
to happen at
the same time.

02:36:47.822 --> 02:36:49.623
Like in Rio,
if you played our demo,

02:36:49.757 --> 02:36:51.491
one of the
buildings explodes,

02:36:51.626 --> 02:36:54.528
and so that kind of event needs
to happen as you're running by.

02:36:54.662 --> 02:36:55.896
And you need
to have it timed,

02:36:56.030 --> 02:36:58.632
because if it blows up
too early then you don't see it.

02:36:58.766 --> 02:37:00.501
It allows story
moments in missions

02:37:00.635 --> 02:37:02.302
that interact
with the environment,

02:37:02.436 --> 02:37:04.505
that interact
with our characters.

02:37:04.639 --> 02:37:06.340
The new tech and new
abilities that we have now

02:37:06.474 --> 02:37:08.608
are allowing us to
push our storytelling

02:37:08.743 --> 02:37:10.377
further than we
could have previously.

02:37:10.511 --> 02:37:12.513
JULIE: What's also
really powerful about Choreo is

02:37:12.647 --> 02:37:14.348
that if
somebody has an idea

02:37:14.482 --> 02:37:15.783
they can mock
things up really quickly.

02:37:15.916 --> 02:37:16.917
We got to
show the Widowmaker

02:37:17.051 --> 02:37:19.586
making this
awesome introduction,

02:37:19.720 --> 02:37:20.854
and Casey was
able to quickly put

02:37:20.988 --> 02:37:24.057
a really rough
prototype in Choreo.

02:37:24.191 --> 02:37:25.191
Phillip would
come in, and on-the-fly

02:37:25.326 --> 02:37:26.459
he's making quick changes,

02:37:26.594 --> 02:37:27.595
they would playtest it.

02:37:27.728 --> 02:37:29.063
Something that
would normally take weeks

02:37:29.196 --> 02:37:31.699
was done in
a matter of days.

02:37:33.901 --> 02:37:38.805
AARON: Each story mission has
its own custom map built for it,

02:37:38.939 --> 02:37:42.343
and these maps
are absolutely gigantic.

02:37:42.476 --> 02:37:45.512
India's one of our
biggest maps, and it's a fun map

02:37:45.646 --> 02:37:46.747
because it has
a few story beats

02:37:46.881 --> 02:37:48.749
that we've taken
from recent comics

02:37:48.883 --> 02:37:51.118
and
characters in the game.

02:37:51.819 --> 02:37:54.621
We've written a
history for each city

02:37:54.755 --> 02:37:56.223
so that the
cities feel lived in.

02:37:56.357 --> 02:37:59.092
And I think that
informs the artists

02:37:59.226 --> 02:38:01.695
so that they know what it
is that the city's been through.

02:38:01.829 --> 02:38:05.232
Gothenburg was our chance
to show Torbjörn's workshop.

02:38:05.366 --> 02:38:07.768
We created a
space that fits his height,

02:38:07.902 --> 02:38:10.737
so there's
stepladders and scalable tables.

02:38:10.871 --> 02:38:13.506
We designed the space as if
we were Torbjörn. (CHUCKLES)

02:38:13.641 --> 02:38:14.742
Molten steel is
pouring out of walls.

02:38:15.910 --> 02:38:18.446
It's pretty epic
when you walk into it.

02:38:20.514 --> 02:38:22.750
Toronto is under
siege by Null Sector

02:38:22.883 --> 02:38:23.783
when the
players arrive here.

02:38:23.918 --> 02:38:25.586
There's a
dynamic snowstorm.

02:38:25.720 --> 02:38:26.820
So, as you make
your way through the level,

02:38:26.954 --> 02:38:28.588
the snow is
slowly building up.

02:38:28.723 --> 02:38:30.624
By the end, the characters

02:38:30.758 --> 02:38:32.258
are caught in this
gigantic snowstorm

02:38:32.393 --> 02:38:33.661
and they must
fight their way through.

02:38:35.329 --> 02:38:37.564
SCOTT: In Overwatch 2,
we want to give you the choice

02:38:37.698 --> 02:38:40.734
to be able to play this
character or that character,

02:38:40.868 --> 02:38:41.902
so we're
designing a lot of systems

02:38:42.036 --> 02:38:44.805
around how their
relationships develop

02:38:44.939 --> 02:38:47.575
over the course of the
story missions in the campaign.

02:38:49.210 --> 02:38:52.479
JASON: We have a
cast of many characters,

02:38:52.613 --> 02:38:54.548
all on screen,
all at the same time,

02:38:54.682 --> 02:38:56.249
and it's fantastic
to actually see them

02:38:56.383 --> 02:38:57.417
all interacting
and working together

02:38:57.551 --> 02:38:59.486
in ways that we've
never actually seen.

02:38:59.620 --> 02:39:01.955
We've always thought
about it and hoped for it,

02:39:02.089 --> 02:39:03.357
but now it's
actually happening.

02:39:03.491 --> 02:39:05.258
It actually
requires us to have

02:39:05.392 --> 02:39:06.693
a whole new
branching dialogue system.

02:39:06.827 --> 02:39:09.329
If you happen to
have Genji and Mercy

02:39:09.463 --> 02:39:11.197
in the second
mission of the game,

02:39:11.332 --> 02:39:14.135
you might get different dialogue
that's about their relationship.

02:39:15.970 --> 02:39:17.237
JASON: Our voice characters
bring characters to life

02:39:17.371 --> 02:39:19.305
in ways that everybody
has fallen in love with,

02:39:19.440 --> 02:39:21.542
from inside
Blizzard to the fans.

02:39:22.343 --> 02:39:26.379
Just to hear this collection
of actors all together,

02:39:26.514 --> 02:39:28.715
all interacting,
has been a real treat.

02:39:28.849 --> 02:39:30.284
(AS REINHARDT)
Hammer down! (LAUGHS)

02:39:33.854 --> 02:39:37.023
JEFF K: I wish people could
see how many people are involved

02:39:37.158 --> 02:39:39.827
when it comes to something like
telling the story of Overwatch.

02:39:39.960 --> 02:39:41.995
It doesn't just come
from a singular person,

02:39:42.129 --> 02:39:43.430
it comes from a group,

02:39:43.564 --> 02:39:46.500
and it's a really talented, fun
group that all works together.

02:39:47.401 --> 02:39:49.436
JEFF C: We've been
developing what we hope

02:39:49.570 --> 02:39:51.271
is one of the
best stories ever.

02:39:51.405 --> 02:39:53.807
There are some key story moments
that I'm really excited about,

02:39:53.941 --> 02:39:56.276
and I really can't wait to
see how people react to it.

02:39:56.410 --> 02:39:57.244
# EPIC ORCHESTRAL MUSIC

02:40:05.853 --> 02:40:07.687
JEFF K: We've been
talking about the game,

02:40:07.822 --> 02:40:09.022
about this universe.

02:40:09.156 --> 02:40:12.059
I know you all
love it so much.

02:40:12.927 --> 02:40:14.495
SCOTT: Right
now, we can sit down

02:40:14.628 --> 02:40:16.429
and have a
night of Overwatch 2

02:40:16.564 --> 02:40:19.700
and have it be a
really fun experience.

02:40:19.834 --> 02:40:20.934
And I think we
need some more time

02:40:21.068 --> 02:40:24.070
until we can say
it's perfectly polished

02:40:24.205 --> 02:40:24.872
in the way
that we want it.

02:40:25.773 --> 02:40:27.073
JULIA: We've made
such incredible strides

02:40:27.208 --> 02:40:28.808
since we first
announced this

02:40:28.943 --> 02:40:31.478
and showed it to the
community at BlizzCon in 2019.

02:40:32.646 --> 02:40:34.915
JEFF K: The thing
that I'm most excited about

02:40:35.049 --> 02:40:39.786
is that moment when we hand
the game over to the players.

02:40:40.454 --> 02:40:41.154
JULIA: We want
to create something

02:40:41.288 --> 02:40:43.390
that really moves people,

02:40:43.524 --> 02:40:44.324
that really
touches people.

02:40:44.458 --> 02:40:45.525
JEFF K:
That's why we do it.

02:40:45.659 --> 02:40:46.994
We create the game because

02:40:47.127 --> 02:40:49.063
we want it to
become part of your life,

02:40:50.030 --> 02:40:52.466
and for you
to experience it.

02:40:53.667 --> 02:40:55.902
# DRAMATIC
ORCHESTRAL MUSIC

02:40:56.737 --> 02:40:58.439
For Overwatch 2,
we're upping the bar.

02:41:00.107 --> 02:41:01.941
It's kind of
five games in one.

02:41:02.076 --> 02:41:02.943
It's a huge undertaking.

02:41:04.979 --> 02:41:07.013
ARNOLD: The new tech and the
new aesthetics for the new looks

02:41:07.147 --> 02:41:09.616
bring it closer to the
characters that you love,

02:41:09.750 --> 02:41:10.251
and we're
just getting started.

02:41:12.052 --> 02:41:12.920
The fidelity of the gameplay
experience is much richer.

02:41:14.355 --> 02:41:17.224
We are making cinematics
that push the boundary

02:41:17.358 --> 02:41:19.526
of what we can
do to tell stories.

02:41:21.028 --> 02:41:22.396
We want this dynamic world.
We want things to feel alive.

02:41:24.598 --> 02:41:26.666
I'm really looking
forward to being able to

02:41:26.800 --> 02:41:28.302
expand the
world of Overwatch

02:41:28.435 --> 02:41:29.102
for our players.

02:41:30.104 --> 02:41:33.540
We're really trying
to tell this epic story.

02:41:35.109 --> 02:41:37.311
In order to
make a game great,

02:41:37.444 --> 02:41:40.647
it takes time,
energy, collaboration.

02:41:40.781 --> 02:41:44.450
We have just an incredibly
professional team of designers

02:41:44.585 --> 02:41:45.652
and artists and engineers.

02:41:45.786 --> 02:41:47.788
We just love
bringing this world to you guys.

02:41:47.922 --> 02:41:50.891
So many cool characters.
It's just a blast working on it.

02:41:51.625 --> 02:41:53.260
Our goal is
for Overwatch 2

02:41:53.394 --> 02:41:57.764
to be the worthy
successor to the first game,

02:41:57.898 --> 02:42:00.167
to be the next
evolution, to be a true sequel.

02:42:01.168 --> 02:42:02.703
We have big plans, so
thank you for sticking with us.

02:42:03.704 --> 02:42:04.838
We're going to
cross the finish line

02:42:04.972 --> 02:42:05.706
and I know it's
going to be awesome.

02:42:07.341 --> 02:42:10.143
I hope people just end up
loving playing it over and over.

02:42:10.277 --> 02:42:12.046
That's what we
want to try and capture.

02:42:12.179 --> 02:42:13.380
"I'm jumping
in. I'm playing.

02:42:13.514 --> 02:42:14.748
(CHUCKLES)
Let's go be heroes."

02:42:14.882 --> 02:42:16.650
# MUSIC CRESCENDOS

02:43:08.836 --> 02:43:14.108
# OMINOUS MUSIC

02:43:29.423 --> 02:43:30.090
We're going to
watch the video sent to us.

02:43:32.259 --> 02:43:34.762
WHISPERS

02:43:37.197 --> 02:43:39.767
GAME: Now you know what I seek.

02:43:41.201 --> 02:43:42.936
Here's the game that it's from.

02:43:43.070 --> 02:43:43.871
We got Cain back?

02:43:45.139 --> 02:43:47.674
Oh, it's II, it's II!

02:43:47.808 --> 02:43:49.309
It's Diablo II remastered.

02:43:50.577 --> 02:43:52.779
It's Diablo II
remastered, come on!

02:43:52.913 --> 02:43:54.580
GAME: No-one...

02:43:54.715 --> 02:43:54.915
Come on!

02:43:55.482 --> 02:43:57.317
# GUITAR MUSIC

02:43:58.252 --> 02:43:59.386
Show it to me.

02:44:02.756 --> 02:44:04.858
GAME: Could this
be our darkest hours?

02:44:06.360 --> 02:44:07.527
There we go!

02:44:07.661 --> 02:44:10.263
I knew it was there somewhere.

02:44:10.397 --> 02:44:11.698
Somewhere out there, I knew.

02:44:12.533 --> 02:44:13.400
# OMINOUS MUSIC CONTINUES

02:44:16.370 --> 02:44:17.304
Somewhere out there.

02:44:19.473 --> 02:44:20.808
It's sick!

02:44:21.942 --> 02:44:22.443
Love the campfire.

02:44:25.612 --> 02:44:26.847
That multi shot looks awesome.

02:44:27.581 --> 02:44:28.515
GROANS

02:44:31.185 --> 02:44:32.386
Wow!

02:44:36.590 --> 02:44:37.324
GROANS

02:44:38.692 --> 02:44:39.326
No...

02:44:44.298 --> 02:44:45.199
And console, okay!

02:44:47.835 --> 02:44:48.868
YELLS

02:44:49.002 --> 02:44:49.068
Alright.

02:44:50.904 --> 02:44:52.005
That'll be interesting.

02:44:52.940 --> 02:44:54.341
MUSIC GETS LOUDER

02:44:55.242 --> 02:44:57.010
That leap attack was awesome!

02:45:00.380 --> 02:45:01.782
Oh!

02:45:03.550 --> 02:45:04.785
This Diablo fight is gorgeous.

02:45:06.420 --> 02:45:07.787
GAME: Not even death can save...

02:45:07.921 --> 02:45:09.756
Oh, Resurrected!

02:45:09.890 --> 02:45:11.492
Let's go!

02:45:12.493 --> 02:45:13.827
Come on!

02:45:15.596 --> 02:45:18.465
That was super exciting.

02:45:18.599 --> 02:45:20.868
I am Mr. Llama SC,
I am a Twitch streamer.

02:45:21.001 --> 02:45:22.803
I have been playing
Diablo II for 20 years,

02:45:22.936 --> 02:45:25.239
streaming it for six years.

02:45:25.372 --> 02:45:27.541
I probably have over
25,000 hours in this game.

02:45:27.674 --> 02:45:30.911
I absolutely love it.

02:45:31.044 --> 02:45:32.946
Seeing the remaster happen,

02:45:33.080 --> 02:45:35.849
I have to ask a
very high level question.

02:45:35.983 --> 02:45:38.819
Why did you remaster right now?

02:45:38.952 --> 02:45:41.420
Diablo II is a very
special game to Blizzard,

02:45:41.555 --> 02:45:43.657
as many of you will know.

02:45:43.790 --> 02:45:45.959
Diablo II also had
its 20th anniversary.

02:45:46.093 --> 02:45:50.697
We wanted to stay true to what
that authentic experience was

02:45:50.831 --> 02:45:52.432
for Diablo II

02:45:52.566 --> 02:45:54.535
and bring it to
the current audience

02:45:54.668 --> 02:45:56.803
and improve it for
the existing audience

02:45:56.937 --> 02:45:58.071
and invite new people

02:45:58.205 --> 02:46:00.641
to experience
it for the first time.

02:46:00.774 --> 02:46:03.743
It's also a good
learning opportunity for us,

02:46:03.877 --> 02:46:06.013
especially when we're partnering
up with Vicarious Visions

02:46:06.146 --> 02:46:09.316
to work on a
remastered project together.

02:46:09.449 --> 02:46:11.050
It's been great.

02:46:11.185 --> 02:46:12.319
We've actually
worked with Blizzard

02:46:12.452 --> 02:46:15.588
for a while now,
a couple of years,

02:46:15.722 --> 02:46:18.124
starting with some
Battle.net type things.

02:46:18.258 --> 02:46:22.228
We just finished
Tony Hawks remaster.

02:46:22.362 --> 02:46:24.497
Before that we
did Crash Bandicoot.

02:46:24.631 --> 02:46:26.900
It just seemed like a perfect
match for us to work together.

02:46:27.034 --> 02:46:31.972
It was a shock to my system,

02:46:32.105 --> 02:46:35.308
first to come in and see
that it's a remastered trailer,

02:46:35.442 --> 02:46:36.342
I was not expecting that,

02:46:36.476 --> 02:46:39.145
but then to see
the complete shift

02:46:39.279 --> 02:46:42.549
in the design
and art and everything.

02:46:42.683 --> 02:46:45.919
I'd really love
to hear more about

02:46:46.053 --> 02:46:48.488
how you came up
with the process for

02:46:48.622 --> 02:46:52.258
how you wanted to
remaster the look of it?

02:46:52.392 --> 02:46:55.161
How you wanted
it to visually exist?

02:46:55.295 --> 02:46:56.863
I'd love to
bring in Chris Amaral

02:46:56.997 --> 02:46:59.766
to talk about some of that.

02:46:59.900 --> 02:47:03.736
Chris, can you discuss
a little bit of the philosophy

02:47:03.870 --> 02:47:07.373
and the design
decisions around this remaster?

02:47:07.507 --> 02:47:10.743
Luckily, we had a
ton of good inspiration

02:47:10.877 --> 02:47:13.146
from the original DIIR.

02:47:13.280 --> 02:47:15.448
Our primary
focus is always nostalgia.

02:47:15.582 --> 02:47:17.717
We want to focus
on getting this right,

02:47:17.851 --> 02:47:20.487
and deliver an
authentic experience

02:47:20.621 --> 02:47:22.188
that players
remembered from 20 years ago.

02:47:22.322 --> 02:47:25.892
Every choice we make with
the art, models, environments,

02:47:26.026 --> 02:47:27.827
should really
ignite that nostalgia

02:47:27.961 --> 02:47:31.030
and reinforce that emotional
connection from the original.

02:47:31.164 --> 02:47:33.733
We came up with
a 70/30 visual guide.

02:47:33.867 --> 02:47:36.603
70% is basically the classic.

02:47:36.737 --> 02:47:39.305
This is where we
don't touch what works.

02:47:39.439 --> 02:47:43.143
We want to maintain those
iconic shapes, colors, tones.

02:47:43.277 --> 02:47:45.879
Everything should be one to one
and faithful to the original.

02:47:46.013 --> 02:47:47.981
We want things
that feel authentic

02:47:48.115 --> 02:47:50.116
and preserve original designs.

02:47:50.250 --> 02:47:53.854
With the 30% new, that's where
we're going to elevate that art.

02:47:53.987 --> 02:47:56.022
Every change should
signify that step forward.

02:47:56.156 --> 02:47:58.958
I also noticed
that the game was 3D.

02:47:59.092 --> 02:48:02.362
Can you talk a little
bit about the discussion

02:48:02.496 --> 02:48:06.366
or how you moved and some
challenges moving from 2D to 3D?

02:48:06.500 --> 02:48:12.305
Evolving from a 2D image
game to 3D is very challenging.

02:48:12.439 --> 02:48:18.011
We basically had to write
a complete new PBR renderer,

02:48:18.145 --> 02:48:21.548
a physically-based renderer
on top of the old game logic.

02:48:21.682 --> 02:48:23.883
It's difficult because
you're dealing with models

02:48:24.017 --> 02:48:26.653
versus 2D sprites,

02:48:26.787 --> 02:48:31.991
where you have dynamic
lighting, you have dynamic VFX.

02:48:32.125 --> 02:48:34.961
A lot of that is
an additional system

02:48:35.095 --> 02:48:37.163
that we have to
work out on our end.

02:48:37.297 --> 02:48:39.565
The follow up to that
question, I suppose is,

02:48:39.700 --> 02:48:40.667
I wasn't able to see any items

02:48:40.801 --> 02:48:43.470
when I was
watching that trailer,

02:48:43.603 --> 02:48:45.405
are all of the items
going to be remastered,

02:48:45.539 --> 02:48:49.509
all of the gems,
every single piece of this?

02:48:49.643 --> 02:48:53.646
What sorts of flavor did you
decide to add to that artwork?

02:48:53.780 --> 02:48:56.215
We remodeled everything

02:48:56.350 --> 02:48:57.917
because everything
has to drop in the game.

02:48:58.051 --> 02:49:01.455
You have your icon art,

02:49:01.588 --> 02:49:04.557
and then you have your
3D space art, that flips right.

02:49:04.691 --> 02:49:07.193
We had to
basically model everything.

02:49:07.327 --> 02:49:09.429
As far as the inspiration,

02:49:09.563 --> 02:49:12.098
we were inspired by
original documentation

02:49:12.232 --> 02:49:15.868
that we found
from Blizzard North.

02:49:16.002 --> 02:49:17.938
There was a huge Word doc,

02:49:18.071 --> 02:49:20.574
where the original
artists pulled inspiration from.

02:49:20.707 --> 02:49:24.477
A lot of that was
historical landmarks, locations,

02:49:24.611 --> 02:49:26.580
very much real
world referencing.

02:49:26.713 --> 02:49:29.949
That was where we
pulled our inspiration as well.

02:49:30.083 --> 02:49:32.919
We wanted that functional design
that the original art had.

02:49:33.053 --> 02:49:35.088
Everything felt believable.

02:49:35.222 --> 02:49:39.926
That detail really does help to
give it some realistic context

02:49:40.060 --> 02:49:41.927
and makes you feel
more immersed in the game.

02:49:42.062 --> 02:49:44.163
It makes you
feel part of the world

02:49:44.297 --> 02:49:46.600
when you see
every buckle and strap.

02:49:46.733 --> 02:49:49.803
ROB: I remember when
we went into the tavern,

02:49:49.936 --> 02:49:54.173
and there was garlic
hanging from a beam.

02:49:54.307 --> 02:49:56.108
We're like, what's the garlic?

02:49:56.243 --> 02:49:59.646
This is all full 3D.

02:49:59.780 --> 02:50:01.314
They said, there
was garlic in the game.

02:50:01.448 --> 02:50:03.783
We go back and
it's four white sprites.

02:50:03.917 --> 02:50:06.219
You're like, "Holy cow!"

02:50:06.353 --> 02:50:09.121
There's garlic and
hams hanging and everything.

02:50:09.256 --> 02:50:12.526
It was really cool to see
it almost for the first time.

02:50:12.659 --> 02:50:17.197
It's funny because
original SD was made in all 3D.

02:50:17.330 --> 02:50:20.233
We actually came
across a bunch of screenshots

02:50:20.367 --> 02:50:21.601
that were a different camera.

02:50:21.735 --> 02:50:23.103
It wasn't the isometric camera,

02:50:23.236 --> 02:50:26.206
it was either marketing
arts or something else,

02:50:26.339 --> 02:50:27.974
or maybe exploration.

02:50:28.108 --> 02:50:31.911
There was shots of
that garlic in the original art,

02:50:32.045 --> 02:50:36.149
you just could never
really see it in the pixel art,

02:50:36.283 --> 02:50:36.783
but it was there.

02:50:36.917 --> 02:50:39.685
It was telling

02:50:39.820 --> 02:50:40.719
that they were
trying to achieve something

02:50:40.854 --> 02:50:43.123
very grand with this art.

02:50:43.256 --> 02:50:44.423
I'm not going to lie,

02:50:44.558 --> 02:50:47.560
I never knew they
had garlic hanging in there.

02:50:47.694 --> 02:50:50.797
Maybe a couple of white sprites,
I thought they were candles.

02:50:50.931 --> 02:50:51.497
Who knows?

02:50:51.631 --> 02:50:53.699
No clue.

02:50:53.834 --> 02:50:55.535
That makes me
excited to go look around now

02:50:55.669 --> 02:50:58.972
and just explore all
of the areas in the game

02:50:59.105 --> 02:51:01.975
to look at the new
detail and see what's there.

02:51:02.108 --> 02:51:04.810
It's good now too
because the one new feature

02:51:04.945 --> 02:51:06.379
on the art side of things

02:51:06.513 --> 02:51:09.615
is a zoom camera.

02:51:09.749 --> 02:51:12.185
It'll help you get in there
and look at all the details.

02:51:12.319 --> 02:51:14.987
It was something
I really felt strongly.

02:51:15.121 --> 02:51:17.456
A lot of ARPGs that
come out have a zoom feature.

02:51:17.591 --> 02:51:20.126
This will really get you
up and close and personal

02:51:20.260 --> 02:51:21.293
with like your armor,

02:51:21.428 --> 02:51:23.996
your weapons, the runes.

02:51:24.130 --> 02:51:27.734
ANDRE: Another way to look at it
too is our legacy toggle feature

02:51:27.868 --> 02:51:30.102
where you can flip
back between the old visuals

02:51:30.237 --> 02:51:31.671
and our current visuals.

02:51:31.805 --> 02:51:34.341
It's not even just
a simple art swap.

02:51:34.474 --> 02:51:36.575
It is really the old
game running underneath,

02:51:36.710 --> 02:51:39.378
because like
Chris was mentioning,

02:51:39.513 --> 02:51:44.083
our new graphics engine
is running on top of the game

02:51:44.217 --> 02:51:46.152
and is mimicking
what is running underneath.

02:51:46.286 --> 02:51:49.155
It's still following all
the same data and rules

02:51:49.289 --> 02:51:51.824
and timings of everything.

02:51:51.958 --> 02:51:54.661
We are still being true
to that 25 frames per second

02:51:54.794 --> 02:51:57.163
of what the game is underneath,

02:51:57.297 --> 02:52:00.867
but rendering it in a way
that has more frames per second.

02:52:01.001 --> 02:52:04.504
I'd love to hear
more about that specifically

02:52:04.638 --> 02:52:09.642
because when I go into
Diablo II, I'm thinking 25 FPS.

02:52:09.776 --> 02:52:12.245
Like you said,
that game is built on that.

02:52:12.379 --> 02:52:15.181
My faster
casting, my hit recovery,

02:52:15.315 --> 02:52:19.352
all of this stuff
is built on 25 FPS.

02:52:19.486 --> 02:52:22.855
How do you
create a game at 60 FPS?

02:52:22.989 --> 02:52:25.958
All of the logic and how
the game talks with the server

02:52:26.092 --> 02:52:27.126
and everything like that

02:52:27.260 --> 02:52:30.763
runs on that cycle
and that hasn't changed.

02:52:30.897 --> 02:52:35.301
We still have benchmarks
in all of our content,

02:52:35.435 --> 02:52:38.204
say for animations of
when a hit frame occurs,

02:52:38.338 --> 02:52:40.039
how long it can
recover and all of that,

02:52:40.173 --> 02:52:41.741
that all matches exactly.

02:52:41.875 --> 02:52:45.545
Now it can run at
a faster frame rate.

02:52:45.679 --> 02:52:48.848
Modern games
run on time, not frames.

02:52:48.982 --> 02:52:50.683
We've done that,

02:52:50.817 --> 02:52:53.353
but all of the logic still
runs on those same things,

02:52:53.486 --> 02:52:55.654
so you can wait for
your breaks to occur.

02:52:55.789 --> 02:52:59.292
All of those things still
happen at the exact same time

02:52:59.426 --> 02:53:03.463
and that was through no small
effort, but it's super important

02:53:03.597 --> 02:53:05.732
because even if you
can't see it right away,

02:53:05.865 --> 02:53:08.734
you feel it when it's different.

02:53:08.868 --> 02:53:11.303
We've done
that on lots of areas.

02:53:11.438 --> 02:53:14.807
For example, the original
game only had so many sprites

02:53:14.941 --> 02:53:16.909
to render your rotation.

02:53:17.043 --> 02:53:20.313
You'd have your character
rendered out at so many angles,

02:53:20.447 --> 02:53:21.614
and the monsters even less,

02:53:21.748 --> 02:53:26.486
but you could actually
walk at greater directions,

02:53:26.620 --> 02:53:28.888
I think it was 64
different squares around you

02:53:29.022 --> 02:53:30.489
that you would path to.

02:53:30.624 --> 02:53:32.258
You'd have feet sliding.

02:53:32.392 --> 02:53:33.226
We've increased that,

02:53:33.360 --> 02:53:35.228
so now there's more granularity.

02:53:35.362 --> 02:53:38.297
It's 3D, your
character can run straight,

02:53:38.431 --> 02:53:39.565
but underneath the hood,

02:53:39.699 --> 02:53:41.901
this is a game made

02:53:42.035 --> 02:53:43.469
back when the
inspiration was tabletopgames,

02:53:43.603 --> 02:53:45.638
so the game is a grid.

02:53:45.772 --> 02:53:49.876
It still respects that grid.

02:53:50.010 --> 02:53:52.845
The controller will still
allow you to move small amounts,

02:53:52.979 --> 02:53:55.114
because you
push the stick a bit,

02:53:55.248 --> 02:53:58.051
but you're either
in a run or walk state.

02:53:58.184 --> 02:53:59.752
Still, stamina is a
thing, you're on a grid.

02:53:59.886 --> 02:54:01.821
All of that logic, once again,

02:54:01.955 --> 02:54:04.290
is all being
driven by the old game.

02:54:04.424 --> 02:54:05.558
That hasn't changed.

02:54:05.692 --> 02:54:07.327
Rob was mentioning animation.

02:54:07.460 --> 02:54:10.262
Animation was very challenging,

02:54:10.397 --> 02:54:14.634
because you only had a few
of the frame counts basically

02:54:14.768 --> 02:54:17.403
to make the animation.

02:54:17.537 --> 02:54:18.538
It wasn't like a modern game

02:54:18.672 --> 02:54:20.940
where you have
tons of different keys

02:54:21.074 --> 02:54:23.475
to make it very fluid animation.

02:54:23.610 --> 02:54:26.912
We had to really like maintain
that timing and that pace.

02:54:27.047 --> 02:54:31.784
The posing was a
big focus for us too.

02:54:31.918 --> 02:54:35.955
We wanted to get those
iconic poses for each character,

02:54:36.089 --> 02:54:36.922
then align that silhouette.

02:54:37.057 --> 02:54:40.326
Continuing to talk of visuals,

02:54:40.460 --> 02:54:44.330
I want to talk about characters,
cosmetics, how they look,

02:54:44.464 --> 02:54:47.033
because something
that I love with Diablo II

02:54:47.167 --> 02:54:49.535
is putting on a Scale Mail,

02:54:49.669 --> 02:54:52.539
a Full Plate
Mail, a Sacred Armor

02:54:52.672 --> 02:54:55.508
and seeing the character
have the different change,

02:54:55.642 --> 02:54:58.511
but some things
still don't reflect over

02:54:58.645 --> 02:55:00.512
in the color of it or whatnot.

02:55:00.647 --> 02:55:02.281
Some do, some don't.

02:55:02.415 --> 02:55:03.682
Is there going to be changes?

02:55:03.817 --> 02:55:06.218
Or is it just
enhancing all of those visuals

02:55:06.352 --> 02:55:08.220
and making
them look even cooler.

02:55:08.354 --> 02:55:11.925
Our goal was to
basically be one to one

02:55:12.058 --> 02:55:13.759
with parity with
the original game.

02:55:13.893 --> 02:55:17.096
We built every piece of armor
that was in the original game

02:55:17.230 --> 02:55:18.864
and is in the
remastered version.

02:55:18.998 --> 02:55:22.401
The only difference
we did is with the icon items

02:55:22.535 --> 02:55:27.240
so we now we have a more
connected visual with the icon

02:55:27.373 --> 02:55:29.308
and the item that
drops in the game.

02:55:29.442 --> 02:55:32.711
Before you
had different visuals.

02:55:32.846 --> 02:55:33.879
That's really the only
thing we've pushed on as far

02:55:34.013 --> 02:55:35.814
as modernization.

02:55:35.949 --> 02:55:37.417
Shields or weapons,

02:55:37.550 --> 02:55:40.453
a lot of the unique
items you find in the game

02:55:40.587 --> 02:55:42.455
had special icon arts right.

02:55:42.589 --> 02:55:45.257
That is
something we'll be showing

02:55:45.391 --> 02:55:46.592
on your
character in the new visuals.

02:55:46.726 --> 02:55:50.730
That's something that a
lot of people would love to see,

02:55:50.864 --> 02:55:53.465
to have the cool stuff actually
on their character there.

02:55:53.600 --> 02:55:56.369
It will be the
same see on those icons.

02:55:56.503 --> 02:56:00.339
Are we going to see remastering
of the cinematics in the game?

02:56:00.473 --> 02:56:02.074
-Yes.
-Yes, we are.

02:56:02.208 --> 02:56:05.111
The entire full 28 minutes

02:56:05.245 --> 02:56:07.780
or whatever is
going to be all remastered

02:56:07.914 --> 02:56:09.648
with new high fidelity art.

02:56:09.783 --> 02:56:11.817
It's going to look
amazing by the way.

02:56:11.951 --> 02:56:15.454
Will the sound in
those also be remastered

02:56:15.588 --> 02:56:17.757
and any other sound in
the game be remastered?

02:56:17.891 --> 02:56:19.926
All of the audio
has been remastered,

02:56:20.059 --> 02:56:25.765
and we've added additional
surround sound features.

02:56:25.899 --> 02:56:28.601
There's more ambient,
there's more effects going on.

02:56:28.735 --> 02:56:30.837
It's just a richer experience

02:56:30.970 --> 02:56:33.973
if you want to
play with your full setup.

02:56:34.107 --> 02:56:38.477
However, we have to use
that measured hand as well

02:56:38.611 --> 02:56:39.678
with audio.

02:56:39.813 --> 02:56:43.082
There's just a lot
of very iconic sounds

02:56:43.216 --> 02:56:45.418
that we really couldn't change.

02:56:45.552 --> 02:56:49.289
If you put a skull into
your inventory, that pop noise.

02:56:49.422 --> 02:56:51.490
MR LLAMA SC: Don't
change my skull sound!

02:56:51.624 --> 02:56:54.360
Exactly!

02:56:54.494 --> 02:56:56.562
There are
certain things you'll hear,

02:56:56.696 --> 02:57:00.866
for your footsteps running,

02:57:01.000 --> 02:57:02.868
we'll have some variety.

02:57:03.002 --> 02:57:05.671
When the rain kicks in,
it's going to have more depth.

02:57:05.805 --> 02:57:06.839
When you go to the jungle,

02:57:06.973 --> 02:57:11.377
you'll hear more
life in those trees.

02:57:11.511 --> 02:57:13.078
There's some
sounds we couldn't mess with.

02:57:13.213 --> 02:57:17.149
With Blizzard,
when we're creating new games,

02:57:17.283 --> 02:57:22.054
one of our design philosophies
is what we call 'find the fun'.

02:57:22.188 --> 02:57:24.824
When we were
looking at DII again,

02:57:24.958 --> 02:57:27.526
we very much felt
like the fun is still there.

02:57:27.660 --> 02:57:30.362
It's actually one
of our core pillars

02:57:30.496 --> 02:57:33.066
for Diablo II
Resurrected specifically

02:57:33.199 --> 02:57:36.402
was to keep the fun
and keep it at its core.

02:57:36.536 --> 02:57:38.037
Because of that,
we were looking at it more

02:57:38.171 --> 02:57:40.073
as a remaster at that point

02:57:40.206 --> 02:57:43.709
and what can we do based
on what the community wants

02:57:43.843 --> 02:57:46.212
or what features
naturally are necessary

02:57:46.346 --> 02:57:49.448
for making this game run on
modern day systems and things.

02:57:49.582 --> 02:57:52.018
I think also, we want to thank
and appreciate the fan base.

02:57:52.151 --> 02:57:55.454
This is really a true
nod to the original game.

02:57:55.588 --> 02:57:58.391
We want to preserve that game
and structure as much as we can.

02:57:58.524 --> 02:58:00.960
If we went with the remake,

02:58:01.094 --> 02:58:03.629
it would be a
completely different game.

02:58:03.763 --> 02:58:05.431
I think that was
why we chose this.

02:58:05.565 --> 02:58:08.568
Speaking from my experience

02:58:08.701 --> 02:58:10.336
or from some of my
community's experience

02:58:10.470 --> 02:58:12.171
and the discussions we've had,

02:58:12.305 --> 02:58:16.542
we definitely felt
like with Diablo II,

02:58:16.676 --> 02:58:18.110
a remaster just
seemed like the way to go

02:58:18.244 --> 02:58:20.279
because, like you said,

02:58:20.413 --> 02:58:23.082
there's so many of those
tiny details within the game

02:58:23.216 --> 02:58:26.118
that create, overall
when you add them up,

02:58:26.252 --> 02:58:29.421
such a big part of the
experience of the game.

02:58:29.555 --> 02:58:32.658
Remaking that into a
new game could be a challenge,

02:58:32.792 --> 02:58:35.294
and could also be a challenge

02:58:35.428 --> 02:58:38.664
in keeping the fun
of the game in that way.

02:58:38.798 --> 02:58:41.199
I look at it a little bit
like StarCraft: Brood War,

02:58:41.334 --> 02:58:44.236
which also had a remaster,

02:58:44.370 --> 02:58:48.407
and how a lot
of the pathing bugs

02:58:48.541 --> 02:58:51.109
and the duds from the reverse,

02:58:51.244 --> 02:58:55.581
all of these things in the
game that were kind of awful,

02:58:55.715 --> 02:58:57.983
are what make the game beautiful

02:58:58.117 --> 02:59:00.619
and what make the
game super balanced.

02:59:00.753 --> 02:59:03.823
If you were to go and remake it
and remove some of those issues,

02:59:03.957 --> 02:59:07.226
it would change how
the whole entire game plays.

02:59:07.360 --> 02:59:11.264
I felt like with Diablo II,
that's kind of the same idea.

02:59:11.397 --> 02:59:14.433
You've got to keep
some of those difficult details,

02:59:14.567 --> 02:59:17.436
because then it ends up
creating the overall experience

02:59:17.570 --> 02:59:19.705
that we all know and love.

02:59:19.839 --> 02:59:23.509
We also felt that for a game
during this time back in 2000,

02:59:23.643 --> 02:59:25.711
based on limitations,

02:59:25.845 --> 02:59:27.846
where everyone was still
looking at strategy guides,

02:59:27.981 --> 02:59:30.182
or just limited internet,

02:59:30.316 --> 02:59:33.052
it led to that typical
game design you see

02:59:33.186 --> 02:59:36.488
where there was a lot
of mystery and discovery.

02:59:36.622 --> 02:59:40.125
We felt like that was
another part of keeping the fun,

02:59:40.259 --> 02:59:45.464
which was to maintain that
discovery of the game as well.

02:59:45.598 --> 02:59:50.069
Speaking to maintaining
and possibly changing,

02:59:50.203 --> 02:59:54.073
what are the pieces that
did end up staying the same?

02:59:54.207 --> 02:59:56.843
Or if it's easier to
explain the other way,

02:59:56.976 --> 02:59:58.611
what are the pieces
that were really changed

02:59:58.745 --> 03:00:01.814
to bring this remaster to life?

03:00:01.948 --> 03:00:07.286
At its core, we want
this to still be the same.

03:00:07.420 --> 03:00:10.088
Right away,
things like the balance,

03:00:10.223 --> 03:00:12.525
the monsters
you see, spawn rates,

03:00:12.658 --> 03:00:14.726
item drops, and things like that

03:00:14.861 --> 03:00:16.162
are the same.

03:00:16.829 --> 03:00:17.930
What we're doing is

03:00:18.064 --> 03:00:23.102
trying to take any of
the rough edges you see,

03:00:23.236 --> 03:00:26.538
compatibility
issues, resolution issues,

03:00:26.672 --> 03:00:31.210
and obviously,
improving the arts as well.

03:00:31.344 --> 03:00:33.879
All these things you've
come to know from a remaster,

03:00:34.013 --> 03:00:36.448
but still maintaining
very much what the game is.

03:00:36.582 --> 03:00:38.818
When it comes to
the features we added,

03:00:38.951 --> 03:00:41.120
this is
probably the biggest area

03:00:41.254 --> 03:00:43.356
where we scrutinize so much.

03:00:43.489 --> 03:00:47.927
Is this addition
straying too much?

03:00:48.061 --> 03:00:50.463
Does it still create the
same gameplay experience

03:00:50.596 --> 03:00:51.397
and other things like that?

03:00:51.531 --> 03:00:52.498
So...

03:00:53.766 --> 03:00:55.968
Things like a shared stash.

03:00:56.102 --> 03:00:58.103
We know people
use mule characters,

03:00:58.237 --> 03:01:01.840
so adding a shared stash,
we felt was the right choice.

03:01:01.974 --> 03:01:04.009
We have a shared stash?

03:01:04.143 --> 03:01:05.577
We have a shared stash.

03:01:05.711 --> 03:01:07.746
Oh, thank God!

03:01:07.880 --> 03:01:09.314
LAUGHTER

03:01:09.449 --> 03:01:12.785
ROB: Now the way that the shared
stash works is there are rules.

03:01:12.919 --> 03:01:15.221
You can't have
your ladder characters

03:01:15.354 --> 03:01:18.123
mixing a stash with
a non-ladder character.

03:01:18.257 --> 03:01:23.963
Your offline characters
can't use your online.

03:01:24.097 --> 03:01:25.631
But, we were like,

03:01:25.765 --> 03:01:27.767
people do this
anyway with a mule character,

03:01:27.900 --> 03:01:29.201
so it's okay to give them this.

03:01:29.335 --> 03:01:33.172
That is like the greatest
feature that you could add,

03:01:33.306 --> 03:01:37.376
because there's nothing
worse than opening up the game,

03:01:37.510 --> 03:01:40.646
going in, dropping all your
GG items to mule them over

03:01:40.780 --> 03:01:42.147
because you've run out of space,

03:01:42.281 --> 03:01:45.351
and then not
being able to log back in

03:01:45.485 --> 03:01:46.485
or the games not
created or didn't perm,

03:01:46.619 --> 03:01:48.787
and losing all of your gear

03:01:48.921 --> 03:01:50.255
or trying to find someone

03:01:50.389 --> 03:01:51.924
to help you
transfer your gear over.

03:01:52.058 --> 03:01:55.928
Oh my gosh,
a shared stash is amazing.

03:01:56.062 --> 03:01:59.198
Thank you so much.
That's such a help.

03:01:59.332 --> 03:02:01.900
Like I said,
maintain the core was a thing,

03:02:02.034 --> 03:02:04.603
but the other aspect of it
was to see where we can improve.

03:02:04.737 --> 03:02:06.872
The shared stash is one,

03:02:07.006 --> 03:02:09.808
because we saw it as
a very common concern,

03:02:09.942 --> 03:02:12.945
especially around muling which
people had workarounds anyway.

03:02:13.079 --> 03:02:14.380
That is one thing.

03:02:14.514 --> 03:02:17.817
Another example is we are
reimagining the UI in the game.

03:02:17.950 --> 03:02:20.519
With a reimagined UI,

03:02:20.653 --> 03:02:23.422
we're still trying to stay
faithful to what you expect.

03:02:23.556 --> 03:02:27.693
You'll still have the classic
globes on the HUD screen,

03:02:27.827 --> 03:02:29.862
the skill icons,
the potion belts and all that,

03:02:29.996 --> 03:02:31.363
but we are reorganizing a bit.

03:02:31.497 --> 03:02:32.931
We're trying to
integrate the menu buttons

03:02:33.065 --> 03:02:35.867
and the stamina
bars closer together

03:02:36.002 --> 03:02:38.070
within the HUD,
so it's a bit cleaner.

03:02:38.204 --> 03:02:40.773
Naturally with reimagining a UI,

03:02:40.907 --> 03:02:43.141
we have some
liberties to add a few things.

03:02:43.276 --> 03:02:45.444
Going back to the information,

03:02:45.578 --> 03:02:47.680
a big thing we're
doing on the character screen

03:02:47.813 --> 03:02:49.514
is adding an
advanced stats screen as well.

03:02:49.649 --> 03:02:52.818
We're going to be showing you

03:02:52.952 --> 03:02:54.720
all the information
you get from items as well,

03:02:54.854 --> 03:02:57.356
so you don't
have to do that math

03:02:57.490 --> 03:02:59.225
with looking at
each of your items

03:02:59.358 --> 03:03:02.060
and calculating how
much magic find you have.

03:03:02.195 --> 03:03:03.429
It's summarized there for you.

03:03:03.563 --> 03:03:07.199
Something that we've
seen already in a lot of mods

03:03:07.333 --> 03:03:09.268
where they've
taken that information

03:03:09.402 --> 03:03:10.302
and created a little screen.

03:03:10.436 --> 03:03:11.270
That's really nice to see.

03:03:12.405 --> 03:03:14.639
that you guys are
implementing that as well

03:03:14.774 --> 03:03:17.476
because, yeah, I don't
think anybody likes sitting down

03:03:17.610 --> 03:03:20.946
and counting up the magic
find of every specific item,

03:03:21.080 --> 03:03:23.249
you know,
and all of their charms

03:03:23.382 --> 03:03:25.151
and all of
everything to figure out

03:03:25.284 --> 03:03:26.619
what is the
number, how much do I have.

03:03:26.752 --> 03:03:29.621
We've also added auto gold.

03:03:29.755 --> 03:03:32.525
So, you can opt in.

03:03:32.658 --> 03:03:33.859
It is a feature
you can turn off,

03:03:33.993 --> 03:03:35.193
which is one of the
things we have to be careful

03:03:35.328 --> 03:03:37.062
and a handful of other
things, where we're like,

03:03:37.196 --> 03:03:39.164
if you want this,
you can turn it on,

03:03:39.298 --> 03:03:40.900
but you can also keep it off.

03:03:41.033 --> 03:03:43.468
This was something
that we started looking into

03:03:43.603 --> 03:03:45.604
when we were
adding controller support.

03:03:45.738 --> 03:03:48.240
There's a lot of paths that we
could have taken

03:03:48.374 --> 03:03:49.408
with controller support,

03:03:49.542 --> 03:03:52.210
obviously for controllers
on console,

03:03:52.345 --> 03:03:52.678
which we're coming out on,

03:03:53.646 --> 03:03:55.681
but allowing you

03:03:55.815 --> 03:03:59.151
to just plug that controller
in for accessibility reasons,

03:03:59.285 --> 03:04:00.319
for that might be
the way you want to play.

03:04:00.453 --> 03:04:03.556
But it's a PC-first experience.

03:04:03.689 --> 03:04:06.492
Like, for example,
there's still an inventory grid,

03:04:06.626 --> 03:04:08.127
there's still inventory Tetris.

03:04:08.261 --> 03:04:10.329
That's core to
what Diablo II is, right?

03:04:10.463 --> 03:04:12.831
Taking that away,

03:04:12.965 --> 03:04:14.799
it starts to become,
you know, something different.

03:04:14.934 --> 03:04:16.201
MRLLAMA: And I love that.

03:04:16.335 --> 03:04:18.804
I think that's the perfect way
to really introduce it

03:04:18.938 --> 03:04:21.907
because you're gonna have
that split in the community.

03:04:22.041 --> 03:04:23.809
You're gonna have
half the people who say,

03:04:23.943 --> 03:04:26.212
"Just give me
auto gold, it's 2020,

03:04:26.345 --> 03:04:28.613
I don't want to worry about
having to pick up

03:04:28.748 --> 03:04:30.583
every individual gold stack."

03:04:30.716 --> 03:04:33.418
But then you're gonna have the
opposite side of purists who go,

03:04:33.552 --> 03:04:35.353
"Don't touch this,

03:04:35.488 --> 03:04:38.023
like, that completely changes
my game experience.

03:04:38.157 --> 03:04:40.192
And if I'm speed-running through
the game

03:04:40.326 --> 03:04:41.060
or playing through whatever,

03:04:41.193 --> 03:04:43.562
I want to be able to go over

03:04:43.696 --> 03:04:46.098
and pick up the gold stacks
and take that time,

03:04:46.232 --> 03:04:47.533
and that's more a part of the
Diablo II experience for me."

03:04:47.667 --> 03:04:51.270
I want to jump back
to features in a second.

03:04:51.404 --> 03:04:54.239
But before we do, you
just brought up console play.

03:04:54.373 --> 03:04:58.877
And I feel like we
have to jump into console,

03:04:59.011 --> 03:05:00.378
the decision for console,

03:05:00.513 --> 03:05:02.981
and how you're
going to make that work.

03:05:03.115 --> 03:05:05.251
We already talked a
little bit about like auto gold,

03:05:05.384 --> 03:05:07.619
having that be a
little more accessible.

03:05:07.753 --> 03:05:09.154
But when I think of Diablo II,

03:05:09.288 --> 03:05:13.025
I think of a mouse
going and clicking everywhere.

03:05:13.159 --> 03:05:14.360
How does this work on a console?

03:05:14.493 --> 03:05:18.196
To say it wasn't a
challenge would be a bold lie.

03:05:18.331 --> 03:05:19.065
CHUCKLES

03:05:19.198 --> 03:05:21.399
So, there's a couple of things.

03:05:21.534 --> 03:05:24.203
For the most
part, the game thinks

03:05:24.337 --> 03:05:26.705
you're still using a keyboard
and mouse with a controller.

03:05:26.839 --> 03:05:29.008
That was almost a
rule we kept, you know,

03:05:29.141 --> 03:05:32.544
that helped keep us on the rails

03:05:32.678 --> 03:05:34.546
because we didn't want to be
sacrificing, once again,

03:05:34.680 --> 03:05:36.782
that authentic experience,

03:05:36.916 --> 03:05:40.352
but we did want to
make it more accessible,

03:05:40.486 --> 03:05:41.520
bring it to a modern audience.

03:05:41.654 --> 03:05:44.456
I mean, there are people who
were born

03:05:44.590 --> 03:05:46.224
after this game came out

03:05:46.359 --> 03:05:49.595
that we want them
to enjoy it and play it,

03:05:49.729 --> 03:05:52.331
and a lot of those
people are used to, you know,

03:05:52.465 --> 03:05:54.232
just more modern titles
that could use a controller.

03:05:54.367 --> 03:05:56.435
Also from an accessibility

03:05:56.569 --> 03:05:58.637
of just allowing you to plug in
a controller

03:05:58.771 --> 03:06:01.774
helps for many other
people to get to enjoy the game.

03:06:01.907 --> 03:06:03.775
The biggest thing I think that
we are...

03:06:03.909 --> 03:06:06.178
the two big things we'll say,

03:06:06.312 --> 03:06:09.481
one is you're changing
the way you play the game

03:06:09.615 --> 03:06:12.317
from being kind
of an eye in the sky

03:06:12.451 --> 03:06:16.622
and telling your hero
what to do by clicking on areas

03:06:16.756 --> 03:06:18.057
to actually being your
character and directing them.

03:06:18.190 --> 03:06:19.925
So, pathfinding is gone, right?

03:06:20.059 --> 03:06:21.026
Because you're doing

03:06:21.160 --> 03:06:23.128
the pathfinding
and things like that,

03:06:23.262 --> 03:06:24.696
which creates some
very interesting things.

03:06:24.830 --> 03:06:27.600
For example,
the Sorceress's Teleport

03:06:27.733 --> 03:06:30.235
is meant to teleport
you to wherever you click.

03:06:30.369 --> 03:06:33.806
So, in that area, when
you're playing on the console,

03:06:33.939 --> 03:06:38.076
we will have a
kind of a default distance

03:06:38.210 --> 03:06:38.977
that you will teleport to,

03:06:39.111 --> 03:06:40.879
whereas on the PC,

03:06:41.013 --> 03:06:43.048
you can click wherever you want.

03:06:43.482 --> 03:06:44.716
Same with the Barbarian's Leap,

03:06:44.850 --> 03:06:45.984
although that one's
interesting because it levels,

03:06:46.118 --> 03:06:48.787
but it's become a
very, very fun challenge.

03:06:48.921 --> 03:06:49.955
Like, I'm a Necromancer

03:06:50.089 --> 03:06:52.191
and I need to target an item on
the ground

03:06:52.324 --> 03:06:53.959
that has a certain keyword.

03:06:54.093 --> 03:06:55.628
It's working so far,

03:06:55.761 --> 03:06:56.796
but you guys will be
the ultimate judgeof that.

03:06:57.430 --> 03:06:59.098
SPELLS FIRING

03:07:00.366 --> 03:07:02.767
ANDRE: Yeah, we've had a
lot of talks back and forth,

03:07:02.902 --> 03:07:04.470
play-testing
and things like that.

03:07:05.271 --> 03:07:09.374
You know, a big challenge
we had too was categorizing

03:07:09.508 --> 03:07:12.143
all those 300 character skills

03:07:12.278 --> 03:07:14.279
to see how each
of them really played

03:07:14.413 --> 03:07:15.581
to see what
challenges there were.

03:07:15.714 --> 03:07:17.583
And like Rob mentions,

03:07:17.716 --> 03:07:19.517
the Necromancer Iron
Golem is a favorite of ours

03:07:19.652 --> 03:07:21.653
to continuously talk about

03:07:21.787 --> 03:07:24.856
because of the
various ways it can target,

03:07:24.990 --> 03:07:26.692
also like a Sorceress's
Telekinesis is another example.

03:07:26.826 --> 03:07:28.760
Watching the trailer,

03:07:28.894 --> 03:07:30.762
we see the Assassin and Druid at
the campfire,

03:07:30.896 --> 03:07:32.831
we see a Druid casting Tornado.

03:07:32.965 --> 03:07:35.301
I have to ask
though, even still,

03:07:35.434 --> 03:07:37.369
this includes Lord
of Destruction, right?

03:07:37.503 --> 03:07:39.938
This is going to be a
full encompassing remaster

03:07:40.072 --> 03:07:41.973
and not just
classic or just a piece?

03:07:42.107 --> 03:07:43.308
That's correct, yep.

03:07:43.442 --> 03:07:47.079
We are going to be including
both the base Diablo II

03:07:47.213 --> 03:07:50.616
and the expansion
Lord of Destruction as well.

03:07:50.749 --> 03:07:52.818
And we're even keeping the mode

03:07:52.952 --> 03:07:55.954
of playing
non-expansion character as well,

03:07:56.088 --> 03:08:00.292
so you can still play a classic
Barbarian, for example,

03:08:00.426 --> 03:08:00.893
and things like that.

03:08:01.026 --> 03:08:02.260
OK, awesome,

03:08:02.394 --> 03:08:03.128
'cause that was something
I was also going to ask,

03:08:03.262 --> 03:08:05.063
will there be Classic Mode?

03:08:05.197 --> 03:08:06.898
Because, once again,
when you go to the purists,

03:08:07.032 --> 03:08:08.901
you always have the
people that go back all the way

03:08:09.034 --> 03:08:12.070
and they're like,
"Classic is pure, and that's it.

03:08:12.204 --> 03:08:13.639
And I won't play anything else."

03:08:13.772 --> 03:08:15.840
That's very much
what we wanted to keep.

03:08:15.975 --> 03:08:17.409
Even based on popularity,
it might not be as much,

03:08:17.543 --> 03:08:18.877
but it's still a
part of the game

03:08:19.011 --> 03:08:22.281
and it still leads to
interesting ways

03:08:22.414 --> 03:08:23.415
of playing too, right?

03:08:23.549 --> 03:08:25.083
A lot of it's
even data-driven, too.

03:08:25.217 --> 03:08:28.286
So, a lot of the reasons to
keep it were pretty clear to us.

03:08:28.420 --> 03:08:31.456
Like, let's make sure we're not
taking something away

03:08:31.590 --> 03:08:33.359
from this remaster.

03:08:33.492 --> 03:08:35.126
I'd love to go ahead
and start talking about

03:08:35.261 --> 03:08:36.729
some of the other features

03:08:36.862 --> 03:08:38.897
or continue talking about
some of the other features

03:08:39.031 --> 03:08:40.632
that you had mentioned before

03:08:40.766 --> 03:08:42.167
because hearing each of these

03:08:42.301 --> 03:08:44.903
already has gotten me
so excited for this game.

03:08:45.037 --> 03:08:47.906
And so what are
some other little things

03:08:48.040 --> 03:08:49.742
that you felt could be changed,

03:08:49.875 --> 03:08:51.577
wouldn't change the
whole flow of the game,

03:08:51.710 --> 03:08:53.712
but just
improve it a little bit?

03:08:53.846 --> 03:08:55.848
One thing we're looking into is

03:08:55.981 --> 03:08:58.517
making it easier to
interact with your stash,

03:08:58.651 --> 03:09:02.887
so having quick keys to
move to your stash is one thing,

03:09:03.022 --> 03:09:04.490
versus dragging the mouse

03:09:04.623 --> 03:09:06.392
every time you want to move
something in your stash.

03:09:06.525 --> 03:09:09.528
Other things we're
looking at as well are

03:09:09.662 --> 03:09:10.963
like an item
comparison tool tip.

03:09:11.096 --> 03:09:12.597
So, in case you're
looking at some other item,

03:09:12.731 --> 03:09:14.934
you can you can hold shift

03:09:15.067 --> 03:09:19.037
or whatever
controller button is mapped

03:09:19.171 --> 03:09:21.540
and then you can see
what equipment items you have

03:09:21.674 --> 03:09:23.909
just for quick
glances, things like that.

03:09:24.043 --> 03:09:26.411
ROB: The item comparison is a
great example of us adding it,

03:09:26.545 --> 03:09:28.680
but added it with
a measured hand.

03:09:28.814 --> 03:09:31.483
So, we don't
suggest any gear for anybody.

03:09:33.218 --> 03:09:36.588
The gear in Diablo II
is so quirky and different

03:09:36.722 --> 03:09:38.756
and every class
can use everything,

03:09:38.891 --> 03:09:40.859
that us
recommending things like that

03:09:40.993 --> 03:09:42.694
actually was more
confusing to people.

03:09:42.828 --> 03:09:46.131
So, literally you hold Shift

03:09:46.265 --> 03:09:48.933
and it's like, here's what you
got equipped, here's what it is.

03:09:49.068 --> 03:09:50.135
I think that's the great way to
do it with Diablo II,

03:09:50.269 --> 03:09:51.803
'cause I agree, I mean,

03:09:51.937 --> 03:09:53.672
that's one of
the best parts about Diablo II,

03:09:53.806 --> 03:09:56.141
the items are so fantastic,

03:09:56.275 --> 03:09:57.676
so unique in their own design,

03:09:57.810 --> 03:10:01.079
that it would be near impossible

03:10:01.213 --> 03:10:02.280
to have a tool tip

03:10:02.414 --> 03:10:04.083
that could
recommend one or the other.

03:10:05.217 --> 03:10:06.985
You know,
because it's all so unique

03:10:07.119 --> 03:10:08.453
to every situation and character

03:10:08.587 --> 03:10:11.323
and skill build and everything.

03:10:11.457 --> 03:10:13.024
We added five more languages.

03:10:13.158 --> 03:10:14.459
So, that's a
feature to a lot of people

03:10:14.593 --> 03:10:16.762
who speak those other languages.

03:10:16.895 --> 03:10:20.265
The whole front end is
pretty much modernized now,

03:10:20.399 --> 03:10:22.867
so your
character is much bigger,

03:10:23.002 --> 03:10:25.504
front and center,
right in the beginning.

03:10:25.638 --> 03:10:28.573
And you'll have a backdrop
of whatever act you're in.

03:10:28.707 --> 03:10:30.675
So, that's all been streamlined.

03:10:30.809 --> 03:10:32.744
You can still, you
know, play online or offline,

03:10:32.878 --> 03:10:34.712
but that flow has been improved.

03:10:34.847 --> 03:10:37.516
So, what are
your favorite changes

03:10:37.650 --> 03:10:38.983
that each of you have

03:10:39.118 --> 03:10:40.619
within the game so far?

03:10:41.787 --> 03:10:44.723
There's a lot of fun...

03:10:44.857 --> 03:10:46.191
It's fun to see a lot of the art
come to life

03:10:46.325 --> 03:10:47.859
with our new visuals,

03:10:47.993 --> 03:10:50.261
so, for me, one of the
things I've been waiting for

03:10:50.396 --> 03:10:54.166
since, like, first
starting on this game was

03:10:54.299 --> 03:10:57.736
to see that character creation
campfire scene come to life

03:10:57.870 --> 03:10:59.838
in our new art.

03:10:59.972 --> 03:11:02.840
And I'm really happy to
see how far it's progressed

03:11:02.975 --> 03:11:03.942
and I love seeing that.

03:11:04.076 --> 03:11:06.378
It's just such an iconic moment.

03:11:06.512 --> 03:11:07.912
It's the first thing you really
see in the game

03:11:08.047 --> 03:11:09.114
before you get started.

03:11:09.248 --> 03:11:12.050
And it's amazing.

03:11:12.184 --> 03:11:13.585
Can I see all the art?
No, I'm just kidding.

03:11:14.753 --> 03:11:16.488
So, we added,
like Rob mentioned,

03:11:16.622 --> 03:11:19.758
we added like an act-specific
screen in the front end.

03:11:19.892 --> 03:11:22.660
And it's
basically this large screen,

03:11:22.795 --> 03:11:25.330
we have like a
diorama of each act.

03:11:25.464 --> 03:11:27.566
And it's just amazing to see

03:11:27.700 --> 03:11:29.067
because it feels
like you're really there.

03:11:29.201 --> 03:11:30.635
You can see the detail,

03:11:30.769 --> 03:11:34.439
you can see every
aspect of the scene,

03:11:34.573 --> 03:11:37.242
and it just has that
very immersive feeling to it.

03:11:37.376 --> 03:11:38.877
That's probably
my favorite part.

03:11:39.011 --> 03:11:42.347
I think mine is a
little bit more squishy.

03:11:43.849 --> 03:11:45.484
I think our game
has gotten a little scarier.

03:11:45.617 --> 03:11:47.318
And I think that there are
certain areas

03:11:47.453 --> 03:11:49.288
in the game where...

03:11:50.589 --> 03:11:53.558
just the way that,
you know, the 3D lighting is

03:11:53.692 --> 03:11:55.427
and the way the sound is,

03:11:55.561 --> 03:11:57.962
and I play it on a big
screen TV sometimes,

03:11:58.097 --> 03:12:00.665
and just it feels scarier.

03:12:00.799 --> 03:12:02.133
It feels creepier,

03:12:02.267 --> 03:12:05.237
a little more claustrophobic
in, you know, certain areas.

03:12:05.370 --> 03:12:06.571
That's not just one feature.

03:12:06.705 --> 03:12:08.573
There's a lot of
things going on there.

03:12:08.707 --> 03:12:09.707
But I think it's
really, really cool.

03:12:09.842 --> 03:12:12.744
I would like to hope that,

03:12:12.878 --> 03:12:15.814
had the original creators
had the tools we have today,

03:12:15.948 --> 03:12:18.416
it's the experience they
would have wanted to make.

03:12:18.550 --> 03:12:20.051
I think the lighting really
helps with that too

03:12:20.185 --> 03:12:21.453
because we're a 3D game.

03:12:21.587 --> 03:12:23.188
That's one of the biggest
differences

03:12:23.322 --> 03:12:25.157
between the original games.

03:12:25.290 --> 03:12:28.760
So, what the game
had before with the sprites,

03:12:28.894 --> 03:12:30.929
with 3D
models built into sprites,

03:12:31.063 --> 03:12:33.765
was not a true
real-time lighting system.

03:12:33.899 --> 03:12:35.767
So, because we're pushing that

03:12:35.901 --> 03:12:38.436
with a 3D graphics engine
running on top of the game

03:12:38.570 --> 03:12:39.671
and getting actual 3D lighting

03:12:39.805 --> 03:12:41.773
inside of those kinds of caverns
and corridors

03:12:41.907 --> 03:12:44.676
makes for a very
interesting experience now

03:12:44.810 --> 03:12:47.112
because it just
feels more immersive

03:12:47.246 --> 03:12:49.147
with having light
react around you like that.

03:12:49.281 --> 03:12:51.549
Yeah, I would definitely
say, I mean, I've only seen

03:12:51.683 --> 03:12:53.351
the trailer piece you sent me,

03:12:53.485 --> 03:12:55.754
but even from that

03:12:55.888 --> 03:12:58.523
just seeing like
down in the tunnels

03:12:58.657 --> 03:12:59.958
or the sewers

03:13:00.092 --> 03:13:03.895
or any of those, like you
said, cavern, dungeonous zones,

03:13:04.029 --> 03:13:07.666
the lighting,
it was darker and brighter.

03:13:07.800 --> 03:13:09.867
And then things like
the skeletons and the fire,

03:13:10.002 --> 03:13:12.637
it just had
like this different...

03:13:12.771 --> 03:13:14.773
I agree, creepier feel,

03:13:14.907 --> 03:13:16.274
though I haven't
seen much more than

03:13:16.408 --> 03:13:18.910
or anything more
than you guys showed me.

03:13:19.044 --> 03:13:22.346
But there was definitely
a different feel to the game.

03:13:22.481 --> 03:13:24.750
And I loved the
fire the most I think,

03:13:24.883 --> 03:13:26.818
just watching the
Sorceress's Meteor

03:13:26.952 --> 03:13:29.087
was like... gorgeous.

03:13:29.221 --> 03:13:32.257
Fire and lightning, once we got
all the effects up and running,

03:13:32.391 --> 03:13:35.427
there was
definitely a couple of times

03:13:35.561 --> 03:13:37.262
where we would be just
in a review and we were like,

03:13:37.396 --> 03:13:39.764
and ten minutes
would go by and we're like,

03:13:39.898 --> 03:13:40.833
"Oh, crap, we had a meeting.
We're just playing now."

03:13:42.501 --> 03:13:45.636
Another thing that kind
of goes without saying that,

03:13:45.771 --> 03:13:47.572
just because it's parody,

03:13:47.706 --> 03:13:50.842
but 8-player multiplayer,

03:13:50.976 --> 03:13:52.043
that just works,

03:13:52.177 --> 03:13:55.046
but, once again,
it was tricky to get that going.

03:13:55.180 --> 03:13:56.815
So, with multiplayer,

03:13:56.949 --> 03:13:59.917
are there going
to be any changes,

03:14:00.052 --> 03:14:01.586
any differences in there

03:14:01.720 --> 03:14:03.855
from PvP to trading

03:14:03.989 --> 03:14:07.091
to how players
interact, you know,

03:14:07.226 --> 03:14:09.060
units blocking each other,

03:14:09.194 --> 03:14:11.029
I mean, all sorts of stuff?

03:14:11.163 --> 03:14:12.664
So, with multiplayer,

03:14:12.798 --> 03:14:15.367
we want to keep all the features
you've known and loved there.

03:14:15.500 --> 03:14:17.035
So, PvP will still be there

03:14:17.169 --> 03:14:20.171
down to like opening the party
window and engaging hostility.

03:14:20.305 --> 03:14:23.475
Trading is still going to
function the same as you know,

03:14:23.609 --> 03:14:25.677
you know, you can go into games,

03:14:25.811 --> 03:14:28.113
trade items or talk in chat
channels and things like that.

03:14:28.247 --> 03:14:30.848
Areas of improvement
though were, like I said,

03:14:30.983 --> 03:14:33.685
going back to my mentioning
of reimagining a lot of the UI,

03:14:33.819 --> 03:14:35.754
improvements to
the bounded lobby.

03:14:35.888 --> 03:14:38.756
So, we were trying
to look at some things

03:14:38.891 --> 03:14:42.361
like improving how
you browse server games

03:14:42.494 --> 03:14:45.263
so when you're
looking at a game to join,

03:14:45.397 --> 03:14:47.633
and typing
passwords and things like that.

03:14:47.766 --> 03:14:48.633
That's the kind of area
we're trying to see

03:14:48.767 --> 03:14:49.935
how we can smooth out in ways,

03:14:50.068 --> 03:14:54.339
like making sure you
can browse Normal games,

03:14:54.473 --> 03:14:55.774
Nightmare games and Hell games,

03:14:55.908 --> 03:14:58.743
even though you've graduated
to the higher difficulty.

03:14:58.877 --> 03:15:00.011
I'm sure you've known that...

03:15:00.145 --> 03:15:03.214
There's nothing worse than
accidentally

03:15:03.348 --> 03:15:04.415
killing Baal in Nightmare

03:15:04.549 --> 03:15:05.750
when you're trying to do
Nightmare Baal runs

03:15:05.884 --> 03:15:07.218
and you're like,

03:15:07.352 --> 03:15:08.986
"I can't find any of the games
anymore

03:15:09.121 --> 03:15:11.222
for Nightmare Baal runs."

03:15:11.356 --> 03:15:13.524
But you can if you
log into a separate game.

03:15:13.659 --> 03:15:15.593
Right, you go to a
separate character.

03:15:15.727 --> 03:15:17.461
But that was
kind of our logic, right?

03:15:17.596 --> 03:15:21.265
And since there
are workarounds there,

03:15:21.400 --> 03:15:24.268
that made it easier for us

03:15:24.403 --> 03:15:25.904
to make the decision of
allowing some of those things.

03:15:27.606 --> 03:15:30.308
Another change
is also looking into

03:15:30.442 --> 03:15:33.378
reducing ladder seasons overall.

03:15:33.512 --> 03:15:36.281
So, right now, ladder seasons
in Diablo II are six months,

03:15:36.415 --> 03:15:38.550
and we're looking
at reducing that as well

03:15:38.684 --> 03:15:41.753
to help have more engagement

03:15:41.887 --> 03:15:44.422
and kind of drive
more incentives too.

03:15:44.556 --> 03:15:46.191
You know,
six months is a long time.

03:15:46.325 --> 03:15:48.993
And we're looking into,
like, if we make it shorter,

03:15:49.127 --> 03:15:51.462
we could probably have greater
beats throughout the year

03:15:51.596 --> 03:15:55.133
as well as seeing
people strive harder

03:15:55.267 --> 03:15:57.102
for getting like to
that level 99, right?

03:15:57.235 --> 03:15:59.805
So, is there going to be any...

03:16:01.340 --> 03:16:02.874
reward change on that?

03:16:03.008 --> 03:16:05.310
Or will it just
simply be, you know,

03:16:05.444 --> 03:16:06.578
the same standard,

03:16:06.712 --> 03:16:08.981
you get to 99,
you're 99 on that leaderboard,

03:16:09.114 --> 03:16:11.616
and, I mean,
that's a giant reward in itself.

03:16:11.750 --> 03:16:14.152
But I don't know,
if there's any other piece

03:16:14.286 --> 03:16:17.422
go up on the
Battle.net website or something.

03:16:17.556 --> 03:16:20.291
We're not looking into adding
anything beyond

03:16:20.425 --> 03:16:21.526
what the game really did,

03:16:21.660 --> 03:16:24.162
but in some ways
there will be some things

03:16:24.296 --> 03:16:26.431
that kind of come out of
because of what we change,

03:16:26.565 --> 03:16:29.734
so we have the
leaderboard, right,

03:16:29.868 --> 03:16:31.503
where you can see
each class going to level 99.

03:16:31.636 --> 03:16:33.505
There are easier
ways to filter through that.

03:16:35.374 --> 03:16:38.075
And we're also
looking into saving that, right,

03:16:38.210 --> 03:16:42.514
so you can always look back
to previous seasons as well.

03:16:42.647 --> 03:16:45.116
So, in a way we're
creating that kind of legacy now

03:16:45.250 --> 03:16:46.984
that had kind of
been dropped from the past.

03:16:47.119 --> 03:16:48.787
So, that's one advantage,

03:16:48.920 --> 03:16:52.023
is your names could be there
in the legacy

03:16:52.157 --> 03:16:53.258
to look into the past.

03:16:53.392 --> 03:16:54.659
I mean, I think that
would be cool enough,

03:16:54.793 --> 03:16:57.863
just anything to keep
it there as like a forever,

03:16:57.996 --> 03:16:59.831
like this was season
one, season two, you know.

03:16:59.965 --> 03:17:01.699
Like, that's when I got my 99.

03:17:01.833 --> 03:17:04.302
Because I know a lot of
people like to go and reflect

03:17:04.436 --> 03:17:07.405
on their old characters
and pushes and stuff like that.

03:17:07.539 --> 03:17:10.242
Character exploration will not
be a thing anymore, by the way.

03:17:11.643 --> 03:17:13.711
Due to modern
Battle.net integrations,

03:17:13.845 --> 03:17:16.548
you will not have to worry about
refreshing your characters.

03:17:16.681 --> 03:17:17.282
-So, they will stay with you.
-Awesome.

03:17:19.384 --> 03:17:21.219
Will there be anything different

03:17:21.353 --> 03:17:23.221
with like character
names then if that's the case?

03:17:23.355 --> 03:17:25.089
Oh, yeah.

03:17:25.223 --> 03:17:27.192
So, this is one
that we talked about

03:17:27.325 --> 03:17:28.359
for a very long time.

03:17:28.493 --> 03:17:32.764
So, because we are
moving to global servers

03:17:32.898 --> 03:17:36.033
and we have cross progression,

03:17:36.168 --> 03:17:40.138
we will allow duplicate names.

03:17:40.272 --> 03:17:43.074
Because with
everyone being in the same thing

03:17:43.208 --> 03:17:46.111
across all consoles and all
platforms

03:17:46.244 --> 03:17:47.311
and other things like that,

03:17:47.446 --> 03:17:49.413
you would very
quickly bump into duplicates,

03:17:49.548 --> 03:17:51.783
like day one.

03:17:51.917 --> 03:17:53.518
And so we are
allowing duplicate names.

03:17:53.652 --> 03:17:55.953
However, they
will also be appended

03:17:56.088 --> 03:17:58.556
with your gamer tag

03:17:58.690 --> 03:18:00.558
or your Battle.net ID
or other things like that

03:18:00.692 --> 03:18:02.828
so that you are still unique.

03:18:03.562 --> 03:18:05.063
But this also allows us

03:18:05.197 --> 03:18:06.898
for people to
play the game quicker

03:18:07.032 --> 03:18:10.068
because we can
autofill in your name

03:18:10.202 --> 03:18:10.936
and other things like that.

03:18:11.069 --> 03:18:12.604
Rob touched on it a little bit,

03:18:12.737 --> 03:18:17.308
but another mention there was
going to global servers as well.

03:18:17.442 --> 03:18:19.777
So, this is a
new thing we're doing

03:18:19.911 --> 03:18:24.316
where the concept of US
West, US East, Asian regions,

03:18:24.449 --> 03:18:25.783
and the EU

03:18:25.917 --> 03:18:28.286
are no longer
going to be a thing

03:18:28.420 --> 03:18:31.556
in terms of like
being on separated servers.

03:18:31.690 --> 03:18:35.426
So, now for the
push for a global play,

03:18:35.560 --> 03:18:36.594
with Diablo II,

03:18:36.728 --> 03:18:39.397
you can now be able to join

03:18:39.531 --> 03:18:40.598
across people around the world.

03:18:40.732 --> 03:18:42.400
I think that's a
fantastic change.

03:18:42.534 --> 03:18:44.502
That's always been one
of the saddest parts is, like,

03:18:44.636 --> 03:18:48.273
they're on EU and I'm on US East

03:18:48.406 --> 03:18:50.175
and then my other friend
is on US West and it's like,

03:18:51.776 --> 03:18:53.111
how do we get us all together?

03:18:53.245 --> 03:18:56.247
OK, somebody has
to make a sacrifice there.

03:18:56.381 --> 03:18:59.851
And so this seems
like a really positive change.

03:18:59.985 --> 03:19:01.419
So far, you guys are killing it.

03:19:01.553 --> 03:19:03.220
I'm loving all,
I mean, absolutely.

03:19:03.355 --> 03:19:06.424
I just want to say like
I'm loving all of the changes

03:19:06.558 --> 03:19:07.759
and all of the
decisions that have been made.

03:19:07.893 --> 03:19:09.760
It feels like you're respecting

03:19:09.895 --> 03:19:11.863
the integrity of the game,

03:19:11.997 --> 03:19:14.632
but really bringing in
a lot of that quality of life

03:19:14.766 --> 03:19:18.169
because Diablo II just
had some of those pain points

03:19:18.303 --> 03:19:20.138
like muling and separate servers

03:19:20.272 --> 03:19:20.805
and all of that.

03:19:22.574 --> 03:19:25.343
So, I just want to throw
that out there really quick.

03:19:25.477 --> 03:19:27.612
There's a lot of love
and care going into this,

03:19:27.746 --> 03:19:30.247
like, we're very passionate

03:19:30.382 --> 03:19:32.483
about Diablo II, you know.

03:19:32.617 --> 03:19:34.719
I can even give
you a little anecdote

03:19:34.853 --> 03:19:37.956
of when I started on the team.

03:19:40.125 --> 03:19:42.660
I was asking around
too, naturally as a designer,

03:19:42.794 --> 03:19:45.330
I want to know as much
as possible about the scheme

03:19:45.463 --> 03:19:47.432
in order to make good
decisions on a remaster.

03:19:47.566 --> 03:19:51.870
So, I asked around,
like, what kind of files

03:19:52.003 --> 03:19:54.639
and leftover archives do we have

03:19:54.773 --> 03:19:57.508
from before from Blizzard North?

03:19:57.642 --> 03:19:59.911
And I was given some

03:20:00.045 --> 03:20:01.512
and I was told that was it,

03:20:01.646 --> 03:20:04.282
which I quickly disbelieved

03:20:04.416 --> 03:20:06.184
because I like to just

03:20:06.318 --> 03:20:09.253
do my own little digging
around to find out things.

03:20:09.387 --> 03:20:12.324
So, I dug around a
lot in a lot of archives

03:20:12.457 --> 03:20:15.693
and I looked around
in different teams as well

03:20:15.827 --> 03:20:16.894
because naturally,
a game from 20 years ago,

03:20:17.028 --> 03:20:19.431
people have
moved on to different teams

03:20:19.564 --> 03:20:21.232
and like where has data
moved around and across

03:20:21.366 --> 03:20:23.234
our other games across Blizzard.

03:20:23.368 --> 03:20:27.205
So, it was within folders
within folders, I like to say,

03:20:27.339 --> 03:20:33.244
that I found a lot of old
source files from Diablo II,

03:20:33.378 --> 03:20:36.348
like within folders of saying
"stuff we should archive."

03:20:36.481 --> 03:20:38.583
And there was a lot
of stuff to archive indeed

03:20:38.717 --> 03:20:40.385
because there
were a lot of things,

03:20:40.518 --> 03:20:43.121
like, we're talking
model source files,

03:20:43.255 --> 03:20:46.457
old tools that
Blizzard North used

03:20:46.591 --> 03:20:49.026
to create the
sprites and things like that,

03:20:49.160 --> 03:20:52.063
all these things I spent
months and months organizing

03:20:52.197 --> 03:20:54.298
and communicate
that out to the team

03:20:54.432 --> 03:20:56.234
and help getting Blizzard
library team

03:20:56.368 --> 03:20:57.569
and other people to help out too

03:20:57.702 --> 03:21:00.171
to help share as
much information as possible,

03:21:00.305 --> 03:21:02.774
which helps a lot
with when we're adapting

03:21:02.907 --> 03:21:05.276
some of these models and whatnot

03:21:05.410 --> 03:21:06.744
and making new art out of it.

03:21:06.878 --> 03:21:09.480
Because, you know, sometimes
those pixels aren't good enough,

03:21:09.614 --> 03:21:11.282
and if we have some
kind of source model to look at,

03:21:11.416 --> 03:21:14.218
if we have it, now we can
make even closer decisions.

03:21:14.352 --> 03:21:15.886
On that day when he found that,

03:21:16.021 --> 03:21:17.455
I think that really
helped me greatly,

03:21:17.589 --> 03:21:20.525
like we sent all that imagery,

03:21:20.659 --> 03:21:21.760
even the models
I think we sent over

03:21:21.893 --> 03:21:25.530
to the outsource
teams and different art teams,

03:21:25.664 --> 03:21:28.299
which they used as
like a stepping stone.

03:21:28.433 --> 03:21:30.234
The only piece of art

03:21:30.368 --> 03:21:32.237
we actually reused one to one

03:21:32.904 --> 03:21:34.305
were Tyrael's wings.

03:21:34.439 --> 03:21:36.207
We actually
took the Tyrael wings

03:21:36.341 --> 03:21:38.777
from the Mac file

03:21:38.910 --> 03:21:41.045
and then just
repurposed that for ours,

03:21:41.179 --> 03:21:42.346
like we did very little work,

03:21:42.480 --> 03:21:43.648
very little
modernization of that,

03:21:43.782 --> 03:21:45.883
we just made it work.

03:21:46.017 --> 03:21:47.085
But, yeah, we did
follow a lot of the rules

03:21:47.218 --> 03:21:50.422
that the old game actually used

03:21:50.555 --> 03:21:53.825
for, like... another
example is the Succubus,

03:21:53.958 --> 03:21:58.029
the Succubus in which, I
think the old was a North model

03:21:58.163 --> 03:22:01.598
they reused the
Assassin mesh for the body,

03:22:01.733 --> 03:22:03.468
and we did the same thing.

03:22:03.601 --> 03:22:05.903
We used our
Assassin mesh for the Succubus.

03:22:06.037 --> 03:22:07.372
ANDRE:
A lot of fun discoveries

03:22:07.505 --> 03:22:08.606
that we're making
the same decisions on

03:22:08.740 --> 03:22:10.241
in interesting ways.

03:22:10.375 --> 03:22:12.610
Another example too is

03:22:12.744 --> 03:22:14.678
because we found a lot of stuff,

03:22:14.813 --> 03:22:17.448
there's a lot
of old texture files

03:22:17.582 --> 03:22:20.284
for some
environments on the game.

03:22:20.418 --> 03:22:21.419
So, another example is like

03:22:21.553 --> 03:22:24.422
the walls you
see in the Act 2 tombs

03:22:24.556 --> 03:22:27.124
with those kinds of
like paintings on them

03:22:27.258 --> 03:22:28.859
are the same
textures we're using

03:22:28.993 --> 03:22:31.763
because we found
the source files for those.

03:22:31.896 --> 03:22:33.831
CHRIS: Any symbology
we have questions on,

03:22:33.965 --> 03:22:36.334
we always like
jump into that archive,

03:22:36.468 --> 03:22:39.471
try to find some kind of source
or reference or something,

03:22:39.604 --> 03:22:40.638
and we just reuse it
as much as possible.

03:22:40.772 --> 03:22:43.107
We want this to
be as true as possible.

03:22:43.241 --> 03:22:45.009
That can even carry
over to the cinematics too,

03:22:45.143 --> 03:22:48.445
like a lot of the cinematics
cover the same locations.

03:22:48.580 --> 03:22:51.015
Think of like Tal Rasha's
tomb with Baal in front of it

03:22:51.149 --> 03:22:52.716
and there's all those
ruins going on behind them.

03:22:52.851 --> 03:22:55.653
Finding sources like that to
try to match as much as possible

03:22:55.787 --> 03:22:57.054
was a goal

03:22:57.188 --> 03:22:58.756
and I'm glad to say we
did find those kinds of things

03:22:58.890 --> 03:23:00.058
and they're
matching it very well.

03:23:00.191 --> 03:23:02.393
So, it's helping
our cinematics too.

03:23:02.527 --> 03:23:04.429
There was even
concepts we repurposed,

03:23:04.562 --> 03:23:06.430
like we found a couple of
concepts that were amazing

03:23:06.564 --> 03:23:09.166
from, you know, 20 years ago,

03:23:09.300 --> 03:23:11.335
the Blood Hawk
and the Blood Hawk nest.

03:23:11.469 --> 03:23:12.369
And we used that completely

03:23:12.504 --> 03:23:15.239
to rebuild our new asset.

03:23:15.373 --> 03:23:16.707
MRLLAMA:
Fascinating.

03:23:16.841 --> 03:23:19.677
I feel like I could just sit
here for hours and hours, days,

03:23:19.811 --> 03:23:21.045
as you guys talk
through every single thing

03:23:21.179 --> 03:23:23.114
you found in the source files.

03:23:23.248 --> 03:23:25.116
I could too. I'm still
organizing it to this day.

03:23:25.250 --> 03:23:28.052
I still could find
something I could work out.

03:23:28.186 --> 03:23:30.120
As I put it, I'm very
meticulous with this stuff

03:23:30.255 --> 03:23:31.222
because I love it so much.

03:23:31.356 --> 03:23:33.424
I'm
categorizing everything, like,

03:23:33.558 --> 03:23:34.992
this is everything
I found on War Eve.

03:23:35.126 --> 03:23:36.894
This is everything I found

03:23:37.028 --> 03:23:39.330
on the Greater Mummy
monster or Baal or something.

03:23:39.464 --> 03:23:42.700
And then if Chris
comes to me and needs help

03:23:42.834 --> 03:23:44.502
with like some
kind of way of identifying

03:23:44.636 --> 03:23:46.037
what parts of the
creatures are something,

03:23:46.171 --> 03:23:47.738
I even go through links of
seeing

03:23:47.872 --> 03:23:49.374
if I could open up those models

03:23:51.209 --> 03:23:53.244
and actually like
re-rendering them out in a way

03:23:53.378 --> 03:23:55.580
that we could actually
see it in higher detail,

03:23:55.713 --> 03:23:57.981
and see, like, what...

03:23:58.116 --> 03:23:59.316
Like, oh, the Greater Mummy is
showing

03:23:59.451 --> 03:24:01.285
part of his bone on his knee,

03:24:01.419 --> 03:24:03.287
so we should try to match
that or something like that.

03:24:03.421 --> 03:24:04.756
And there's a lot of interesting
stories and references

03:24:04.889 --> 03:24:06.357
that we actually used

03:24:06.491 --> 03:24:07.625
to create the new art

03:24:07.759 --> 03:24:09.326
by re-rendering models out
to see everything.

03:24:09.461 --> 03:24:12.463
Yeah, it was literally
like video game archaeology.

03:24:12.597 --> 03:24:16.434
And then even learning
how parts of D1 were made,

03:24:16.568 --> 03:24:18.636
parts of the code
that were commented out,

03:24:18.770 --> 03:24:21.405
totally geek out
on just all of the...

03:24:21.539 --> 03:24:23.073
you know, because it's a game

03:24:23.208 --> 03:24:25.310
that we all kind of grew up in

03:24:25.443 --> 03:24:27.278
and a lot of us got
into the games industry

03:24:27.412 --> 03:24:28.746
because of these types of games

03:24:28.880 --> 03:24:30.481
and this game
in particular, right.

03:24:30.615 --> 03:24:33.117
And so it was almost like

03:24:33.251 --> 03:24:34.918
we were treating it
like a classic car, right?

03:24:35.053 --> 03:24:37.922
Like, OK, it probably shouldn't
run on unleaded gas anymore,

03:24:38.056 --> 03:24:40.592
but you really
can't change it, right?

03:24:40.725 --> 03:24:43.528
You just got to make
it, you know, good enough,

03:24:43.661 --> 03:24:45.229
even better, you know,

03:24:45.363 --> 03:24:46.130
than they remember

03:24:46.264 --> 03:24:48.099
and good for another 20 years.

03:24:48.233 --> 03:24:50.034
Yeah, and even like
you mentioned, Diablo I

03:24:50.168 --> 03:24:51.502
is very key too, like,

03:24:51.636 --> 03:24:53.871
we look at Diablo I as well

03:24:54.005 --> 03:24:55.973
because naturally this
being a sequel to Diablo I,

03:24:56.107 --> 03:24:58.509
especially within the
timespan of when it came,

03:24:58.643 --> 03:24:59.877
like Diablo I,
you know, was 1996

03:25:00.011 --> 03:25:01.679
and Diablo II, 2000,

03:25:01.813 --> 03:25:04.548
a lot of things carried
over throughout development.

03:25:04.682 --> 03:25:07.351
So, going back to see
Diablo I and what it's like

03:25:07.485 --> 03:25:10.053
also helped us make decisions,

03:25:10.188 --> 03:25:13.424
even some of the art too because
we discovered certain things

03:25:13.558 --> 03:25:14.324
we didn't even realize before

03:25:14.459 --> 03:25:15.827
until later down the line.

03:25:15.960 --> 03:25:18.696
Like, an example is
in the Chaos Sanctuary,

03:25:18.830 --> 03:25:21.432
you see a lot of
statues with these Black Knights

03:25:21.566 --> 03:25:23.901
and those are actually
the same Chaos Knights

03:25:24.035 --> 03:25:25.736
you see in Diablo I.

03:25:25.870 --> 03:25:27.872
So, like sometimes some of the
things I find,

03:25:28.006 --> 03:25:29.507
even source files from Diablo I,

03:25:29.641 --> 03:25:31.442
were useful for us for reference

03:25:31.576 --> 03:25:34.112
so we can make the new
statues and things like that.

03:25:34.245 --> 03:25:36.581
Even the Sorcerer
and the Act 3 Hireling

03:25:36.714 --> 03:25:39.016
shared very similar models,

03:25:39.150 --> 03:25:42.219
like it was just
an addition helmet

03:25:42.353 --> 03:25:44.455
or a little bit of
detail on the chest,

03:25:44.589 --> 03:25:45.656
but that was
really the difference.

03:25:45.790 --> 03:25:48.258
You guys need to do

03:25:48.393 --> 03:25:52.196
a just like developer
archaeology discussion

03:25:53.264 --> 03:25:54.498
at some point where you just
sit down

03:25:54.632 --> 03:25:56.533
and talk through all of this

03:25:56.668 --> 03:25:58.602
because this is like the most
fascinating stuff I've heard.

03:25:58.736 --> 03:26:01.039
I mean, I've
played so much Diablo II

03:26:01.172 --> 03:26:03.908
that I know so many
intricacies of the game,

03:26:04.042 --> 03:26:08.012
but, like, when
there's more to be discovered

03:26:08.146 --> 03:26:10.347
it's like, oh, man,
I'm like itching to know.

03:26:10.481 --> 03:26:11.482
That's the funny thing.

03:26:11.616 --> 03:26:13.650
The funny thing is,

03:26:13.785 --> 03:26:15.986
going back to our, like,
core philosophies on the game,

03:26:16.120 --> 03:26:19.590
we wanted to keep discovery
in the mystery and stuff.

03:26:19.724 --> 03:26:21.058
Because you keep discovering
things all the time

03:26:21.192 --> 03:26:22.927
when you play Diablo II,

03:26:23.061 --> 03:26:25.162
like all these little
hidden rules and information.

03:26:25.296 --> 03:26:26.964
It kind of carries that
fascination over

03:26:27.098 --> 03:26:28.399
through development as well

03:26:28.533 --> 03:26:30.935
if we find a lot of
things in the code,

03:26:31.069 --> 03:26:33.804
design documents
and models, things like that,

03:26:33.938 --> 03:26:36.307
that were really fascinating
to see how they were made,

03:26:36.441 --> 03:26:38.175
and I learn something
new every day on the projects.

03:26:38.309 --> 03:26:42.246
One thing, shifting
back to the online play here,

03:26:42.380 --> 03:26:45.883
I know there's definitely going
to be questions around security.

03:26:46.017 --> 03:26:48.385
How are we handling duping?

03:26:48.519 --> 03:26:49.720
How are we handling bots?

03:26:49.854 --> 03:26:52.323
How are we handling, you know,

03:26:52.457 --> 03:26:55.492
just anything
around that, round-downs,

03:26:55.627 --> 03:26:57.595
and all of the disconnects

03:26:57.729 --> 03:27:01.932
and all of that good
stuff that we deal with online.

03:27:02.066 --> 03:27:04.035
What's being taken,

03:27:04.168 --> 03:27:06.203
or what action is
being taken there?

03:27:06.337 --> 03:27:08.806
So, moving over to
modern Battle.net helps us

03:27:08.940 --> 03:27:10.875
with a lot of
those types of things.

03:27:12.777 --> 03:27:14.878
To say it's,
you know, lock-tight,

03:27:15.013 --> 03:27:16.914
I wouldn't dare do that.

03:27:17.048 --> 03:27:18.882
That's like throwing a
gauntlet down to the community

03:27:19.017 --> 03:27:20.751
to try to break it.

03:27:20.885 --> 03:27:23.988
But all of that should
be far more secure now.

03:27:24.122 --> 03:27:27.558
Now, we do still allow
you to play offline, right?

03:27:27.692 --> 03:27:29.760
You will need to authenticate
that your copy is legit,

03:27:29.894 --> 03:27:33.597
but after that you
won't be bothered.

03:27:33.731 --> 03:27:35.666
And so, there's a reason
why we leave those characters

03:27:35.800 --> 03:27:38.535
quarantined away from online.

03:27:38.670 --> 03:27:40.004
I mean, yeah, we have
a server team working on it

03:27:40.138 --> 03:27:42.039
and these are
some of their efforts.

03:27:42.173 --> 03:27:43.774
We want to stop the bots,

03:27:43.908 --> 03:27:46.477
stop duping as much as possible.

03:27:46.611 --> 03:27:47.444
There's a lot of strides
happening

03:27:47.578 --> 03:27:48.779
in the back end of things.

03:27:48.913 --> 03:27:51.916
So, I'm excited
to play this game

03:27:52.050 --> 03:27:54.385
and I'm sure a lot of
other people are very excited.

03:27:54.519 --> 03:27:57.087
How can we sign
up for this closed beta

03:27:57.221 --> 03:27:58.989
and when will this
game be coming out?

03:27:59.123 --> 03:28:01.059
ANDRE: Actually, we will be
doing a technical alpha

03:28:01.192 --> 03:28:04.428
and you can sign up
on our webpage today

03:28:04.562 --> 03:28:05.963
for future waves of invites.

03:28:06.097 --> 03:28:08.865
As for the game itself,
it will be released in 2021.

03:28:09.000 --> 03:28:10.934
Well, I just
want to say thank you

03:28:11.069 --> 03:28:13.303
to the
remastering team right here.

03:28:13.438 --> 03:28:16.040
I absolutely am blown away

03:28:16.174 --> 03:28:19.409
by how much effort
and how much passion

03:28:19.544 --> 03:28:20.978
you guys are
putting into this game

03:28:21.112 --> 03:28:23.614
and just into Diablo II.

03:28:23.748 --> 03:28:25.850
I've loved
Diablo II for 20 years

03:28:25.983 --> 03:28:29.353
and I never thought that
the game could get better,

03:28:29.487 --> 03:28:32.123
but I think you guys
are respecting the integrity

03:28:32.256 --> 03:28:36.060
while upgrading and
adding and improving,

03:28:36.194 --> 03:28:38.963
and I think this is going to
overall just better the game.

03:28:39.097 --> 03:28:41.365
So, thank you guys so much,

03:28:41.499 --> 03:28:43.100
I appreciate you having me on,

03:28:43.234 --> 03:28:44.602
and for all the
hard work you've done.

03:28:44.736 --> 03:28:47.270
Awesome. Thank you. I mean, your
praise means the world to us.

03:28:47.405 --> 03:28:50.975
I mean, you and people like
you who keep the game going,

03:28:53.010 --> 03:28:55.079
for 20 years, like that's...

03:28:55.213 --> 03:28:56.913
If you guys didn't do that,

03:28:57.048 --> 03:28:59.149
this might not
be happening, right?

03:28:59.283 --> 03:29:02.420
We see the
passion in what you do

03:29:02.553 --> 03:29:04.855
and what your colleagues do.

03:29:04.989 --> 03:29:06.390
So, thank you even more.

03:29:06.524 --> 03:29:08.493
And thanks for
coming to do this, this was fun.

03:29:09.727 --> 03:29:12.063
# OMINOUS MUSIC

03:29:18.970 --> 03:29:19.103
HIT SOUNDS

03:29:27.645 --> 03:29:30.982
GAME: Not even death
can save you from me.

